        <p>DEn OroTHrs.—Shin. GOOD, BAD, AND INDIFFENT. —Ourselves, our 4metnies, and the rest of the wora Tim great trouble with the budding genius is that he is usually nipped in the lasl. FRANK: • What makes marriage a failure ?" May : "The tuunber of its liabilities, of course !" GASW ELL : I " tell you the phonograph is a great Invention." Larimer : " Yes ;it speaks for itself." " Tutor tell me, Proleasor, that you have mastered all the modern tongues." Prof, seer: "All but two—my wife's and her mother's MANKIND is always happier for having been happy once. The memory of happiness is happiness. \Your.), BE ONE lIIMSEL3.—" I hear your son is taking lessons on the {LW." " Yea ; he is study- izig with a private tooter. AT A Loss.—lom " What are you doing with SO many dictionaries?" Dick (plaintively) : "I'm trying to find words to tell Helen of my love." "So you have "written a novel ?" "Yes." "Has your heroine satin skin, velvet eyelashes, and hair like threads of spun gold ?•' "Yes.' "Is her name Gwendoline ?" "It is." "Then I don't viee why it shouldn't be a success." QUITE A RELIES. —Husband: "What do you do when you hit your thumb with a hammer? You can't swear." Wife : "No, but I can think, with all my might and main, what a perfectly horrid, mean, inconsiderate, selfish brute you are not to drive the nails yourself." WREN- Carlyle was introduced to Dr. Joachim, he simply remarked, "I have no great opinion of musicians ; they seem such a vain, wind-baggy sort of people." " ,Sle," said little Wilhelmina, "I don't think Solomon was so rich as they say he was." "Why, my dear ?" asked her astonished ma. "Because he 'slept with his fathers :' and I think if he had been so very rich he would have had a bed of his own."</p>
        <p>SCHOOL TEACHER (examining small class in his- tory): "Yes, that't right ; Henry VII., Henry VIII., Elizabeth. Now, who came after Eliza- beth?" Scholar (eagerly) , "Please, 'm, I know. No on; she didn't have any followers at all, awl died a' old maid." "I rc.N-oW, 11.1.-.rie,“ he said, "I think every child +shows in some way in what calling it is most likely to succeed in after years. Do you think so?" "Then we'd better make a real estate man of our Willie. I can't keep hint out of the dirt." " Wu don't place any value on things till we lose them," said Mrs. Smith. "That's so," said the Widow Jones. "I never knew what a good hus- band I had lost until I heard the minister preach his funeral sermon." Mm. NEWMAN : "What made you tell me he was the carver of his own fortune, when be got every penny he has by marrying an heiress r Newman: "Humph.: He had to eat out hal a dozen fellows to get her, didn't he ?" Murrain (at a Saratoga "Are you enjoy- ing yourself, dear ?I' Daughter: "No, I'm not." "What is the matter?" "I've refused George SIX dances running, and he don't seem miserable a bit."</p>
        <p>FAISE MoDE.sTr.—Lady : "This is tho second time you have received food from me, isn't it?" Tramp : "It is, madam; and you are at liberty to mention the fact to your friends if you wish." THE BErrEa WAY. - Why grieve we over vanished joys? Regrets are vain, as each one knows. Much wiser he who time employs rejoie• jug over vanished woes. Mn. Biowir : "There is one virtue about these</p>
        <p>biscuits, my dear." Mrs. Brown (blushing with pleasare): " What is it dearest ?" Mr. Brown (brutally): "If they were worth their weight in gold we should be millionaires." HOUSEKEEPER: "Why don't you ago to work, you lazy vagabond?" Tramp: "Lazy, mum? Do you call a man who tramps from here to Land's End an' back, year in an' year out, lazy? I think, mum, gents like me displays considerable energy." Two well-known clergymen lately missed their train, upon which one of them took out his watch, and, finding it to blame for the mishap, said he would no long..r have any faith in it. "But," said the other, "isn't it a question, not of faith, .but of good works ?" HE: "Yon don't love me as you did before we were married, I don't believe." She: "Of course I don't, John. You wouldn't expect a woman to love a.married man as she could a bachelor, would you ?" "WE might as well consider our engagement as broken, Reginald." "I don't see why. Your -father said ` postponed.' " " Postponed until you arrived at years of discretion and in your case, Reggy dear, you know what that means." - " in?Joa.E you say any more, Toni, I want to ask you one very serious question." " What is Dora?" "Mamma, as you know, is a widow. Now, would you object to marry her if I refused you? You see, we really don't want to lose you." WAITING —Ada: "No ; Priscilla will never murrY unless she finds her ideal.' Ida : "What sort of a man is her ideal?" Ada: "A man who will propose." FAIR-MINDED.—" I'm always wiling to meet a man half-way," observed Charley Shortpay, mag- nanimously, as he settled with his tailor at fifty -cents. on the dollar. HE: "I am rather in favour of the English than the American mode of spelling." She: "Yes?" Ho: •` Yes, indeed, Take parlour, for instance. Having' hrit makes all the difference in the world." VISITOR: "Why, how big you are growing, -Tommy ! If you don't look out you will be getting taller than your father." Tommy : "Won t that • be jolly! Then Pop'll have to wear my old trousers cut down for him. ALL (Min.—Lady (in curio store): "Does this clock go?" Dealer: "Alt, malaria lam so sorry, . but that clock is six hundred years old. It has already went." A FAREWELL LUNCH.—Skyhigh (in restaurant) : " What's that you're eating, Aigy—muslerooms ?" " Algy: "Yes. Mabel has refused me. All is over. It's the latest way." MRS. LARKIN—" I want a ittle money to-day, Fred." Ma Larkin—" I'm very glad of that." Mrs. Larkin (surprised)—" Why are you gad ?" Mr. Larkin.—" Because you generally want a good deal." Stern Maiden Aunt (keen on protecting her sister's jam cupboard): "Tommy, what were y doing on the shelf just now ?" Tommy (backing out of the door) : "Making room for you, Aunt. Angelina." , "Do you think," asked Mrs. Pepper, sharply, "that a little temper is a bad thin(' in a woman ?” "Certainly not, ma'am," replied Oa! gAlant philosopher. "It is a good thing, and she ought never to lose it I" Vicar (to sour-tempered parishioner): "How do you like the new bells in the church tower?" Parishioner: "I regard them as a nuisance." Vicar: "How strange 1 Why, enly to-day I was wishing that the ringers could practise oftener." Parishioner: "So should I wish it, if they would confine their practise to dumb-bell exercises." SOME one blamed De Marsh for chewing MI mind. " Well," said he, " that's the difference betiVeen a man and a jackass : the jackass can't change his mind, and the man • can. -It's a human r Youth is commonly considered to be ingenuous and inexperie -cad. The common, however, is field of observation on which we may learn that, not- withstanding all that is said ot green greese, young goose Is much more downy than an old one. FIRST SPENDTURIFT : "Have you telegraphed to the old man for money?" Second Spendthrift.: "Yes." "Got an answer?" " Yea. I tele- graphed the old man, Where is that money I wrote you for?' and his answer reads, 'ln my inside pocket.'" • lin ton the honeymoon) : " Why did yo a used to talk so much about being afraid that some one would marry you for money?" Site: Because if any one did marry me fur money, it would be sueli a terrible mistake, you know, because I haven't any." He: "Oh—um—yes, ye:, of course." MRS. MINES.—" There There is another torn silk handkerchief that my husband has stuffed into the rag-bag. That is the last of the half dozen he bought last year, or year bef .re, and he put them all in Ilse rag-bag himself. Now wait." Sister: "What for ? ' Mrs. Minks: "In about a week you'll hear him raving around wanting to know what has become of that half dozen new silk hand- kerehiefs he bought only a few months ago."</p>
        <p>Tha fioyal Laundresses say Critchliirs " STARCH GLas, .1s unequalled for impartins, a beautifol finish to starched linen, &amp;c., either cold or boiling water starch. Mr. —, Corn- wall. says :—" My wife will not finish our linen without your "Starch Gloss." From Mrs. —,,Bradford " Dear si: lam pleased to inform yon- that 1 have tried many so-ea 11,6 Starch Glazes and Polishes, but find none equal to yours." It • should be used in every house. Sold at Id., id.. and fid.„by most starch sellets. ft not in stock will procure it. 2. I.P.Ael:bura,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</head>
        <p>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>The tru'll about the "sew Australia" settle- ment in Paraguay, as tar as I can learn, is not yet known. Friends of the movement here, although not in po,session of definite information, maintain that the report of the breaking up of the settlement originated with a few of the pioneers who objected to remain" teetotal "and who have either withdrawn from the, colony or suffered dismissal. Withdrawal after the payment of about.£6o and a journeyto Para- guay is hardly likely, and, according to regulations dismissal "for persistent or unpardonable offence against the well being of the community "—and a revolt against teetotal principles could hardly be classed as such—can only be decreed by a five- sixths majority of all adult m'embera. The Agent General for New fouth Wales is in telegraphic communication with his Government respecting the colony, and his anticipations are not of an en- couraging nature.</p>
        <p>The Jewish agitation against the admission of money-lenders to synagogue honours has received a new importance within the last few days. So dis- tinguished a Hebrew leader as Sir John Simon has thrown the.whole weight of his influence on the side of those who demand exclusion, and it is believed that his intervention will compel the authoritatise organisations of Anglo-Judaism to give the subject official attention.</p>
        <p>The Conference room at the National Liberal club was well filled on Monday evening for the annual meeting of the Land Nationalisation Society. Dr. A. R. Wallace presided, and after the despatch of the usual prelitninaries,read a rather lengthy address in which he attempted to forecast the practical working of the Parish Councils Act, a measure wanting indeed, in absolute perfection, but yet containing much to be thankful for. He gratefully conceded that the passing of the Act marked a notable degree of progress towards the oljects of the society, and remarked in his closing sentences that " from a social point of view the century had been characterised by two great movements--the rise and full development of capitalism.in our man- ufacturing and commercial systems, and the con- tinuous political enfranchisement of the people." Mr. W. Saunders, M.P.,advocated practical co-oper- ation by the members in the carrying out of the new measure, exclaiming that working men were perfectly well able to aid in bringing it into effect. Mr. Charles Wicksteed also gave an excellent des- cription of everyday village life; and a resolution was passed exhorting social reformers to concen- trate their energies on land nationalisation.</p>
        <p>It is expected that the Chancellor of the Exche- quer's Budget statement on Monday will be one of the longest ever delivered on such an occasion. I have the very best reason to believe that the pro- posals are all of a tholoughly democratic character, and are calculated to give great satisfaction in advanced circles. The one disappointment, from the Radical point of view, is that no mention will be made of a graduated income tax.</p>
        <p>The Devonshirticlub, which is in such low water just now, and in behalf ot which some of the Lib- eral Unionists are making such strenuous efforts to find money, is housed in a portion of the building once notorious as "Crockford's "in St. James's-st. The club is on!) twenty years old, but it has been a celebrated rendezvous in its brief career.</p>
        <p>It was originally to have been called "The Lib- eral club," but eventually the name of the late Duke of Devorshire was given to it,and the present Duke became the first chairman. After :he Liberal split on Home Rule. it seemed for a time as if the Devonshire club might become the centre of dissen- tient Liberalism in Clubland,but as the dissentients continued to hold a strong position in the Reform, there was no real necessity for the capture of the Devonshire, and so it has remained in the amicable pc,ssession of both parties, with the result that— with such a club as the National Liberal in existence —it now lingers superfluous on the stage. Mem- bers have been leaving it in great numbers for some time past. Another attempt to save it for Liberal Unionism has-failtA, and now there is to be a general meeting of members preliminary to a winding up.</p>
        <p>The play in the first round of the Parliamentary Golf Handicap finished on Tuesday afternoon at Fumedown. The competition has been taken parr in with great relish by nearly all the sixty-four men who entered. So far the feature of the .match .may he said to •be the almost uniform success of the Lancashire members and their neighbours from Cheshire. No one has played better on his handi- cap form than the leader of the Opposition, and there are some who predict that, free from the worries of office, the right hon. gentleman will this year go well through the match. Mr. 'l'. W. Legh, who did so well last year, has again begun in good , form. Ills handicap has been polled down to six, hut at that figure he had no difficulty in beating Lord George Hamilton, though he had to allow nine strokes in the round. Mr. Seton-Karr has also been rednced by the handicappers, but he too has justi- fied their estimate of his game by defeating Mr. Dunbar Barton by three holes in a very well con- tested match. Mr. Shepherd Cross is another Lan- cashire player who has come through this stage of the match successfully. Since last year his game has materially improved, and as he is handicapped at 16 he is a difficult player far anyone to meet. Nlr. Henry Tollemache, the winner in 1891, Mr. 'Gerald Balfour, and Mr. Bromley Davenport have also won their ties. Mr. Brunner was a -second new- comer in this year's match. He is fond of the game. but was beaten. Sir William Houldsworth has been unable to play, and has scratched.</p>
        <p>I hear that if the new experiments with carrier pigeons are successful a larger number of birds will. be trained for the purpose of carrying naval des- patches. 'Vie first, result of the experiments—which are being made both at Devonport and Portsmouth —will be seen at the naval manceuvres, when the birds will be largely employed ,to make communi- cation with the shore and with the different sec- tions of the fleet. Experts in pigeon traininsr have reportel that there will be no difficulty in realising the purpose of the Admiralty.</p>
        <p>If we have a few more years like '93 the whole character of our people will change. A.little of the vivacity of the Southrons will be infused into their blood, end we shall become quite Parisian in our tastes. Riosques Will line the embankment, little marble-topped tables will make their appearance outside the places of refreshment, and we may even look forward to bands all day long in t he parks,and dancing and brightness generally. Our cold and damp and coquettish climate -is responsible for a great part of our moroseness and stolidity.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Astor, the multi-millionaire Ameri- can who has expatriated himself tor good and all and bac.' taken upon -himself the duties and the pleasures of an English country gentleman, is only one of our very many plutocratic cousins from over the waters, who revel in the delight of a Thames- side life. Mr. Vanderbilt—the. _Railway King—has just become the tenant of Mr. Scott Al urray, who has let to him his family seat betwei.n Marlow and Henley, a spot as beautiful as any in he Valley of the Thames. Mr. Vanderbilt has taken the house for six months, and if the weather continues through the summer as perfect as it is at present, a glorious time he and his guests will have. -</p>
        <p>• Reference has been made to the phonographic .courtesies that has passed between Mr. Gladstone and Mr. Edison. The inventor's message to the • statesman was given. here is that from the statesman to the inventor It is self-help that makes the man, and man-makina the aim which the Almi2lity has everywhere im- pressed upon creation. It is thrift by which self-help for the masses dependent upon labour is principally made effective. For them thrift is the symbol and the instru- ment f independence and of libel ty, indispensihle eon- ditions of all permanent human goxlness. But thrift is also the mother of wealth, and here comes the danger into view, for wealth is the mother of temptations, and leads many of us possessors into a new :form of slavery more subtle and net less debaseing than the old. From this slavery may all land9,and especially all lands of the English tongue, hold themselves for ever free'l</p>
      </div>
