      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>I'HE EX A MINER. SAT UR " AT, APRIL 14, 1894.</head>
        <p>I'HE EX A MINER. SAT UR " AT, APRIL 14, 1894.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Frances Hodgson Burnett is a great pedestrian and believes in a walk before breakfast. In summer she starts as early as six o'clock, and is generally at work at her desk by nine. The granddaughter of Nathaniel Hawthorne, Bible- garde Hawthorne. is about to publish her first book. It is to be called " The Fairest of the Fair," and is to con- tain a record of her experiences in Chicago last sum- mer.</p>
        <p>Mdme. Mudjeska never allowa her temper to get the 10(,V4.r of her. " I cannot afford to g,it angry," she a woman at my time of life must eamoinise her umotions and her nerves if she • wants to hold the rem- nants of her youth and beauty.</p>
        <p>Miime. Adelina Patti has announced her intention to give anothe.r concert for. the inmates of the Swansea Hospital and the poor of Craig-y-Nos. The prima donna has already raised five thousand pounds by her conceits for the Welsh poor. The concert will Is) givea in tine theatre at Craig-y-Nos Castle. Mrs. French Sheldon, the explorer, during her travels in Africa carrtei a banner ni which were imprinted the words, "D., not touch me,' and impressed it upon the blaek men that the white woman must never be touched. In speaking to her tollowers, or in giving her orders,she was always careful to stand above them and discovered that she bad more command over theta when talking in this rosition. When Mrs. ShelJou arrived at a certain place,the Saltau of the tribe sent her six hundre4 of the finest ostrich feathers, and offered sixty husbaal=, who filed into tier august presence. This proved t• IN -ather an embarrassing present, but she was obliged to explain that it was 0,4 custom of the white queen to have but one husband, and as she was already blessed with that commodity, she would be forced to de:Attie with thanks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST. NELLIE, With the earlier and brighter days of spring the first change from winter garments is, as you know, usually made in the outdoor wrap, which becomes oppressive when the sun shines, and a jacket of smart cut and light weight is altogether more comfortable and fi,ctive. Already spring jackets are sLown, minus the heavy frills and bulky collars aud pelerbaes which wcre fashionable last season, and where there is either the one or the other, it is cloWy shaped at the neck, and slopes out full and wide at the edge. The question of selecting head gear is always most important at this One of the year, and there is just now every opportunity for careful choice. Oze example of exceeding neatness which I have seen was made on a fht shape, covered with velvet, the edge being finished with a ruching of jet pailettes. A, bow of petunia velvet, with little clusters of Parma violets and a kit wing and mauve osprey, completed the trimming.</p>
        <p>Some charming outdoor capes are made of shot blue and gold velvet, reaching only to the elbow, closely pleated over the arms and lined with pink. In front they are double breasts I, ani fastened with large blue buttons enamelled with roses. Rare old lace mingles with a ruche at the neck, and the lace falls in a point at the centre of the back. • A- beautiful gown which may be worn by an elderly lady or adapted with advantage by a young woman has been bri ught to my notice. The skirt should be made of plain bla4c cloth or cashmere, edged all round with a narrow and quite inexpen- sive jet gimp. On the left side the skirt is out up to disclose a panel of white cloth braided diagonally with rows of the same jet. The bodice should be of black velvet or velveteen; but cloth may be substituted if preferred. The turn-down collar and revers should be faced with white moir, while the tight-fitting vest may be of white cloth, jet-braided to match the skirt panel. A dainty cravat of white chiffon and lace are then added, and a pretty bonnet of black velvet and jet, with a white osprey, will complete an unusually effective toilette.</p>
        <p>The following is a desoription in Cassell's Family Magazine for April of a tea gown which has set me longing to possess one like Its: "On a tightly-fitting lining was arranged a soft satin robe-front of turquoise blue, crossed at intervals with lines of tinted lace insertion, and fully gathered to a pleated yoke of satin and lace, with trim little collar shaped to the throat. The straight over- fronts and back of the gown were made in Ondine silk, shot blue and faint yellow with an opalescent effect; unlined were these over- fronts, the looped edge of the selvedge forming a pretty finish. A deep cape of tinted lace encircled the yoke, and the sleeve was composed of a large puff ef satin, and lines of insertion, finished with a lice frill below the elbow. At the back, the gown fitted close to the figure and was cut with a short train Those who wish fur ease and economy combined would do well to invest in a ca-hmere of a soft petunia colour and convert it into a tea•gown, cut straight and full from ncLic to feet, girdled at the waist with satin rih')ons, ills° tied at the throat with a bow of the same. The back to form a Watteau pleat and the hem trimmed with a gathered frill bordered with lace insertion, a deep frill also edged with insertion is inserted leo the arm-hole; a long puff to the elbow and thence tight to the wrist is the fashion of the sleeve." The mind of the average Englishwoman ía exercised at this sea ion by the difficulty of determining what pattern her spring jacket should follow. A very smart example is made of black serge, smartly cut, semi-fitting in the front, tight at the back, with admirably setting moire sleeves,, liaed with elk, and is completed with a moire bow at the neck.</p>
        <p>)110 ooes abroad,commands the leay. )0 esrefully coilocted for the •4 Litth</p>
        <p>ings of Ilk Sistfls of tho Poor.</p>
