        <p>THE DOUBLE LIFE ;</p>
        <p>On, THE HAMPTON MYSTERY.</p>
        <p>By the Author of " DAIIIEWOOD."</p>
        <p>CFIAPTICR VIII. GIRALDA.</p>
        <p>She was the pride Of her familiar sphere—the daily joy Of all who on her gracefulness might gaze., And in the light and music of her way, liars a companion's portion. WILLIS. The morning subsequent to the happy home ?eerie' we have depicted dawned dark and lower- ing upon The Evergreens. The wiiids sighed and wailed among the thick-growing trees with intense mournfulness, shaking the night's rain in spiteful guas from the surcharged branches. Bat heavy as was nature's gloom, it had not power to chill the happiness or subdue the light of that pleasant secret home.</p>
        <p>The family gathered at an early hour in the breakfast-room, the count as dark .and stately as usual, but with the glow of happiness on his features ; the Lady Beatrice as brOlt and beauti- ful and smiling as though her heart had never known a pang, and her eyes had never been wet with tears. Little Fay was basking in the sun- shine of his mother's presence, caring for nothing beyond. Rupert's proud, earnest face beamed witli Satisfaction at the promise he had won of being sent to a university.</p>
        <p>But one face Of all the group was graver than usual—but one brow was more intensely thought- ful. (liralda, thoughtful of others, and self-forget- ful, as was her wont, had yet an anxious, pre-occu- pied look, that did not escape the loving watch- fulness of her parents. She took little part in the cheerful conversation, and at the earliest moment possiblq made her escape from the room. " What is the matter with the child !" asked the Lady Beatrice, when returning with her huslund towards the drawing-room. Has she anything to trouble her, do you think ?" The count smiled at the idea. The countess, recovering her momentarily dampened spirits, said :</p>
        <p>"I am, going up to your study to see her a little while. Take the boys with you into the drawing- room, please." . . .</p>
        <p>She parted from the count in the hall, ascending the stairs, while he went into the apartment indi- cated. She -hastened to the study, in which she had surprised her husband the preceding evening. Everything was in exquisite order here, but the room was unoccupied. Throwing herself carelessly upon the tiger-skin couch, she awaited the entrance of her daughter. Scarcely a minute had elapsed when (liralda entered. She came forward half timidly, and the Lady Beatrice noticed that her face was pale, and her changeful eyes almost black with suppressed feeling. "Come, sit beside me, my darling," said the mother, Making room on the couch. Giralda obeyed taking the proffered seat. The countess then put her arm around the slen- der graceful figure, drawing it so near that the maiden's head rested upon her bosom. "Now, Giralda," she said, half lightly, "you may enbosom your heart to site. What do you m ant most ! . Not 'a doll—you are too old to play with dolls. My darling has just passed her six- teenth birthday." " Mamma, pray be serious with me," exclaimed Giralda, in a low, quivering tone, full of emo- tion. "I am no longer a child. Do not treat me as one."</p>
        <p>The Lady Beatrice looked full into that lovely, earnest face, and sighed as the truth of the girl' words came home to her.</p>
        <p>"No longer a child!" she echoed, as if the word', cost her a pang. "Is that true, Giralda ? Hay. you crossed the boundary between girlhood an woutanhood ? If so, you have left behind you.the happiest portion of your life—l mean, at least the portion freest from care. A woman has licav burdens to bear, my child. I had hoped to keep you for years to come in childlike uneonsciousuei•cs of sorrows and cares."</p>
        <p>"It could not be, mamma," replied the yowl); girl. " Could I play on with toys, and be satis- fied with idleness and luxury in this home of ours, when I have known that every flower I tended, every pretty dress I wore, every joy that came to me, was bought with your hard lab-air ? " The Lady beatrice flushed slightly, and her lip: trembled with sudden feeling. " Maa.iina,," said G-iralda, in quick, low, passion- ate tones, "night after night I have lain in my soil bed provided by you, and thought of you as sing- ing to crowded audiences, singing your very heart out, that you might gain a support for us. I have dreamed of you as an actress, applauded and well paid, as you are, but still longing as you must long to live in your own home alwayu, to see contin- ually the husband and children you love so much, and I have waked to find my pillow wet with tears. Mother, my love and sorrow for you are killing me ! "</p>
        <p>The flush had faded from the face of the Lady Beatrice, leaving it very pale, and now her tears dropped thick and fast upon the little head nest- tied in her bosom.</p>
        <p>"My darling, my precious daughter!" she mur- mured, in 'a choking voice. "1 distress you, do I not, mamma ? " asked the young girL "Yet I mnet say what I have in my mind. For a long time I have wanted to say it,. but I could not bear to trouble you. You have enough to, bear, darling mother, without my add- ing to your cares. Besides," she 'added, more hesitatingly, "I dare not say what I wished, lest you should think I blame papa for allowing you to work so hard. Ido not blame papa at all: I do not even question his motives. EU is so wise and good and grand, that I can but feel that what- ever he does seems to him right and just !" " And have you, not equal faith in me, Giraldal" questioned the Lady Beatrice. "Yes—O, yes mamma; but you are so ready V; sacrifice yourself us all. Ever since my earliesi remembrance you have come home only at gated times, and your longest visits were at the times when my brothers were born. You give yourseli no rest nor recreation—"</p>
        <p>"I have both at home, Giralda." The young gal looked up so pityingly 7. it sor- rowfully that a hot rain of tears dropped fro.o the mother's eyes. "Oh, mother," she said, caressing the hand of the Lady Beatrice with infinite-tenderness, " does your heart never grow weak in this struggle for your children's bread ? " "Dots your brain never tire in it's V.:timings for us It." "How could they? My husband and my chil- dren are the life of my life—the soul of my soul " "If you could only live with us all the time I." sighed,the maiden. "These visits, always night ones, seem so like stolen visits, mamma. Did I pain you by my expression?" she asked, as her ladyship started. "I beg your pardon. Home would be heaven if you were always here." , The Lady Beatrice dashed her tears from her eyes' and gazed reflectively at the fire. " You are thwaghtful,. Giralda," she said, sadly. "I wish I might have kept you in your unques- tioning, unreasoning childhood longer. But I do not blame you for the turn your thoughts have taken. What has decided you to speak to me at last, my darling ? " The girl hesitated. Then answered, in a low , tone: ,</p>
        <p>_ ." Rupert is ambitions, mamma. He has profited by papa's careful teachings, and by the instructions given him. He wants to go to Oxford by and "He ~old me so last evening. I have promised that he shall be sent to college, Oiralda." _ . .</p>
        <p>" But, mamma;" said the girl, blushing, and speaking only by an effort, " I have read so much about the distinctions of society. I know that papa is a Spanish grandee, and that he has blue blood in his veins. I know that you are his hon- oured wife, the sharer of his title; but mother, Ru- pert is very proud-spirited, and if the young gen- tlemen at Oxford should look down upon him be- cause—because you are an actress, he would suffer keenly—" "1 know it. I have considered all that," re- plied the Lady Beatrice. "But Rupert may be sent to a German university as the son of a Span- ish count, and no one would have a word to say against his origin. There is a possibility, Giral- da," she added, "that I may not always be an ac- tress," and she smiled. "in case I abandon my frotession, my children will have nothing to blush "We have not now 1" exclaimed the maiden. "But I have more to say, mother. Let me speak of myself now. I have been carefully educated by papa and by .competent daily governesses. I have learned all the accomplishments usually taught young ladies. I can speak in papa's language and in our own with equal. fluency. You have 'often praised my musical abilities and my 'voice. "Well ? "questioned the countess. ' training woulii have fitted a prinoess,".</p>
        <p>continue ,I thrahia, glowing with earnestness. "1 have always been treated as one who is to be the heiress of immense wealth, and who will yet sake a lofty place in society. Dear old MeggymEeck is always scrupulous to address me aii the Lady Gir- alda before my governess. She is tenacious of my title. You, dear mother, have been at pains to teach me ease and self-possession under all circumstances. When 1 reflect" on these things, one of the two conclusions forces itself upon me : Either I am an heiress, or you have intended me as the companion of heiresses. lam not an heir- else you would not be obliged to follow your profession. My other conclusion must Le truo t him."</p>
        <p>"I do not comprehend you, Giraldr.," sftid her mother. " What i companion of heiresses, if not an heiress? " _ _</p>
        <p>" Why, a governess," replied the young girl, with a faint smile. " I must turn my accomplish- ments to account in some way. This dear home liL:s sheltered inc all my life. It is time I took so:11C of the burdens from your shoulders. If you can work for me, it will not injure me to work for you. In short, dear mamma, my mind is made up i am going to be a governess ! " She concluded with evident relief at having un- burdened herself of her weighty resolve, and looked up with a bright fearlessnese ; as if ready to combat all argument against it. " You a governess " exclaimed the Lady Bea- trice, with an involuntary smile, as she regarded the pale, high-bred face, with its slight inclination to haughtiness, the patrician beauty, and delicate grace of her darling. "Yes, mamma," said Giralda firmly. "I have no false pride. If it be right for you to etrn money, it is right for me." "But you are but a - "I am older than you think, dear mother, in mind and heart."</p>
        <p>" But, Giralda, the life of a governess is very hard. You have seen your daily governesses well treated, but all are not so. Governesses, as a class, are- hard-worked and ill-paid. They have to put up with insults from their employers, and the servants as well. They are isolated, imposed upon, and neglected. Of course there are excep- tions, but this is the general rule." "1 can bear insults, work, and isolation;" said Giralda, with quiet resolution. "Does not my mother bear unmurmuringly separation from her husband and children? My trials cannot be hard- er than yours, and they will he sweetened by the reflection that I have removed from you the burden of my suppert. I canoot be longer de- pendent on your exertions, mother. If we must live at such expense, ta;:ing your energies contin- ually. and compelling you to continue your labors, you must not have me to work for. I must con- tribute something, too, to Rupert's educational expenses." . She spoke with a woman's calm decision. Her sense of ri.,ht, and justice gave force to her man- ner, and the mother saw that she would not easily be swerved from her purpose. The face of the Lady Beatrice worked with agi- tation. She put her daughter gently from her, and arose, pacing back and forth with impetuous movements. "I might have expected this," she murmured. "With her generous nature, her filial love, and her strict ideas of the right, Giralda could have done nothing different. Poor child! If she only knew !"</p>
        <p>Her heart yearned over the generous daughter se anxious to relieve her of her burdens. She reflected if she could have excused her absences from home in any way save on the plea of being an actress—a plea which she assured herself was literally true. A wild impulse arose within her to confide the truth to the loaiden—to tell her that she was a high-born lady, and that the theatre in which she acted was the great theatre of society, where she shone a star.</p>
        <p>But the impulse was choked in its birth. Remembering the nightly tears Giralda had shed, believing her a hired player, she could not face those innocent, loving eyes with her story, espec- ially, too, since her daughter would ask questions she might find herself unable to answer. She could not explain even to her why her mar- riage must be an inviolable secret from all the world. She could not lay upon her so great a burden as the knowledge that her mother was two .distinet persons in one—the Lady Beatrice Hamp- ton, the haughty and worshipped belle of society, and the Countess of Arevola, the loving wife, the devoted mother, the pretended actress. No, not yet could she make even Giralda her confidant.</p>
        <p>. She continued walking to and fro until she had regained her calmness. Then she ap?roached the .couch, leaned over its back, and laid her hand softly upon the maiden's disturbed brow. ' "My daughter," she said, tenderly, "I appreciate .your love- and devotion to me. You tire a good and .noble girls and I love you 113 you deeerve. I can never let you go from me; never iv:mat you to leave your father's '• watuhful re. Here You are well sheltered from the guile:•ml world. There .will come a change for the bett,!' and by, and you must wait hopefully for it s I um. Till then. :be content. "I cannot 1 I cannot !" whi:J.pered Giralda with 'pleading eyes. "You must, my. darling. With your rich and sensitive nature, your fresh, yenng beauty, what •would become of you in the .cold, bard world? You area home-nestling ; besides—but we will not pursue the subject further now. I must leave you 'again this evening. I will think of what you have said during my absence from home, and next week I shall perhaps have something of import- ance to say to you. • Let this matter rest a week."</p>
        <p>_ Ah if SOUIQ subtle instinct or listening guardian 'angel had but urged her to frankness then ! She kissed the lovely, passionate face again and again, and then released Giralda, saying, with as- sumed lightness : "Now, my darling, you must go' with your brothers to the conservatory to prepare. for our •picnic. Leave the shadows all behind you." The maiden arose and went out, smiles and tears struggling for supremacy on her face. The Lady 'Beatrice followed her, descending to the drawing- room, which she entered at the moment Qiraldo. and her brothers were leaving it. " Well, Beatrice," said the count, pleasantly, "has Giralda's mighty secret been unbosomed What did the child want—a new bird for her aviary, or a new necklace for her jewel casket ? " "Neither, my husband," replied the Lady Bea- trice, coming to his side "The generms. child has formed a project of assisting us, of contributing_ her share, as she calls it, to our family expenses. , She wants to be a governess.'l The count looked grave and astonished. " What must she think of me ? he asked with, agitation.. "All loving, just and tender thoughts, my hus- band," was the gentle response. •" I honor her motives and her noble heart. But was ever an idea so ridiculous? She, the heiress of a magnifi- cent fortune, for of course I shalt divide my own wealth eqtailly between her and Fay, Rupert being provided for by you—she a aoverness ! " The count retleated his ladyship's smile. " I have been thinking lately," said the Lady Beatrice, musingly, " If I dare tell our secret to our daughter. i should Else to have her with me- ..at Hampton Hduse, in the character of a protOg&amp;3, you know. She is so beautiful that lam impatient to introduce her into society. Would it be possible to have Girahla, with MC without her relationship to me being detected?" -" Quite impossible, I should say," responded the count. "Giralda would betray you every day, She is so warm-hearted and SO devoted to you. If he world did not suspect, your father, the earl, might." • " True, and Lord Adlowe doubtless would," said the Lady Beatrice, with an instinctive shiver, as she thought of that unwelcome, detested suitor for her. hand. "He watches me closely. lam so cell so *hers that if I were affectionate to Giralda he would know her to be my own child. If he sus- . pected the truth nothing could, result but ruin. No; my scheme is impracticable. Giralda must remain here." • "The same providence that releases Rupert will release her." "Till then, my husband, guard the child care- fully. If I could only have her under my own eyes continually. Woman as she fancies herself, she is still a chill, for she knows nothing of love, save such as she feels for her parents and brothers. May she long remain as innocent as now of warmer passions." With this earnest prayer, the Lady Beatrice dropped the subject. It was her habit, when at the Evergreens, to choose cheerful matter for conversittion, and if by chance an unpleasant one came up, it was dis- missed as quickly as possible, its unpleasantness greatly lessened, No shadows were allowed to linger wherever the sunshine of her presence came. So now she dispelled the look of sorrew that had gathered on her husband's face by her strong encour- aging hopefulness. They sat and talked as levers ,7,e far an heur or</p>
        <p>1 --•</p>
        <p>two, heediees of the passing moments. Then came a summons to the conservatory. The count and countess proceeded thit!oir once, not to keep their entertainers waiting. liowers had been arranged to form a perfect its er of beaifv. and in the midst of the bloola and filtgrin6e stood a little round table, covered with damask, and laden with heaps of orangos, baskets of pinci, dishes of cakes, and other dainties, the whole !wing very pleasing to the eye as well as tempting to the palate. Giralda, bright and joyous as a young Psyche, yet with. nevertheless a shade of gravity on her brow, stood ready to do the honors, a young knight eager to assist her standing at either hand. Good Memry Fleck stood behind them, her broad face beaming with smiles. The entertainment was a very pretty affair, and passed oil charmingly, the Lady iteatrice:being te belle of the occasion, although Giralda shared in part her laurels. After the picnic came a walk in the garden, the sky having cleared sufficiently to render the excursion agreeable. The remainder of the day passed but too qu:ckly to each of the inmates of The Evergreens. The afternoon was spent quietly in the drawing-room. The evening came on early, and the gloom of approaching separation darkened the faces of all when the gas- lamps were lighted and the curtains drawn. " If I were only earning something," thought Giralda, " mamma could spend more time with us. At any rate, she would not be oppressed with an- xiety as to what may become of us should anything happen to her." This idea had taken firm root in her mind, it . seemed.</p>
        <p>Soon after dark, the Lady Beatrice, attended by her danghter, went up to to her dressing room to attire herself for her return to Hampton House. She laid aside her pretty, simple robes with a sigh, and donned the disguise she had worn, transform- ing herself into the pretended aunt of her tiring- woman. The waterproof cloak was fastened about her, the great poke-bonnet concealed in its depths her lovely head, and the thick veil was in her hand, ready to he tied on when the leave-taking should be over.</p>
        <p>Oiralda assisted at the toilet with much secret agitat;on, yet with outward calmness. " I Hok like quite a respectable old Lady, do I wt?" asked the Lady Beatrice, gayly, glancing at her reflection in a pier-glass. "I ought to have a pair of green spectacles, had I not? I used to wear them on these journeys, but no one dis- covered that my eyes were young, so I left theta off as useless encumbrances. Now, my darling," she added, her tone changing, "I must say good- bye."</p>
        <p>She embraced her daughter, who clung to her with strange fervour. She pressed her to her bosom, calling her tender names, and at lengtk release.i her with a singular sense of bereavement. Without stopping to reason upon the feeling, she led the way downstairs. The little family were wait:ng in the hall to witness her departure, the eonnt alone being absent. The Lady Beatrice found him in the drawing room and parted from him there in tears and grief. She then came out, embraced her two sons, raining tears on the girl- like face of the younger, bade Meggy Fleck good- bye, and rushed out into her waiting carriage, eovering her face with her veil.</p>
        <p>The 'e'Dachmart, a faithful old family adherent, whipped up his ponies, and the carriage rolleel down the dark and winding avenue. At the garden gate he alighted, unlocked the way of egress, led his ponies through, locked the gate again, and then, having mounted his box, the car- riage went whirring along the road towards the station.</p>
        <p>The youthful Lady Cirald stood on the porch till the sound of the retreating wheels had died away. Then she murmured : . _</p>
        <p>"'lt will be long before I see mamma again. I am going away to earn my living. I cannot wait until her return. When she finds that lam not too tender to battle with the world, and that I caa earn money to assist her and lighten her labour, she will bless me for my energy in going. It is my duty to leave this dear home my dear parents, my darling brothers. It is not right that so many should be dependent upon one. One of us should go, and I am that one. When mamma comes horn. next week, I shall not be here ? "</p>
        <p>With a look of sad yet holy resolution, she turned and re-entered the house. (To be continued.)</p>
