        <head>AEHINL MACHINES.</head>
        <p>AEHINL MACHINES.</p>
        <p>Man has bcen trying to fly ever since the earliest his- toric times. The first 'notable record of an attempt in this directon describes the artificial nigeon of Archytat, a famous geometrician of the Pythagorean school, who flourished 400 B.C. He made a wooeeo bird, ‘vhieh, as is alleged, flew by mechanical means, its buo3ancy being effected by magnets. But, if it fell to the ground, it could not lift itself again. Under the reign of Nero an inventor is said to have flown in Rome, but he lost his life in coming down, an unfortunate fatality which seems to have terminated many ancient attempts at flying. The failure was attributed to fils .evil genius, which tackled him while aloft, taking him at a disad- vantage. John Muller is said to have constructed an artificial eagle at Nuremburg, which flew out to meet the Emreror Charles V., and accompanied him back to town. About the Same period a—monk named Dinerus flew about a furlong from the top of a tower in Spain. By means of a pair of wings a wrson named Danre l'erose was enabled to fly, and, while aniusittc the people of that city with his aerial performances, he fell from the ton of St. Mary's Church, and broke his thigh. It has been gravely suggested that, with the help Of trained birds hke the condor of South Arnerien, wirick can carry off a sheep or calf, man might be enabled to fly ; but this plan would certainly he attended with dan- ger, and op to date it has never been tried.</p>
        <p>Moy Hoe, a Chinaman, whose headquarters are at Sae Frane4en, ha e one of the most curios contracts of any existing in the United' States. Ho is employed by the Chinese companies at the Golden Gate to seek out and gather together the bones of his dead countrymen for shipment hack to China. In carrying out his -duties he travels incessantly [rim one extremity of the United States to the other. He has colrected tne bones of thou- \ sands of Chinamen..</p>
        <p>Every spring the Emppror of China"goes to "the em-, perces ploughs a portion of it. sows it with several It4vis of seeds, and superintends the ceremony, while the, princes and nioe courtiers perform the same act, in honour of the god of a7.riculture. The empress., at tho &amp;tine time, gives her ladies a lesson in silk culture.</p>
