      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <p>nit TRUTH, THE WHOLE TRUTH., AND NOTHING BUT THE TRUTH.</p>
        <p>In giving testimony, it is si.reia Ilv impressed Upon the maind of the witnesi that " The Truth," " The Whole Fruth," aid " Nothing bat the Truth" must be given. Partially concealed torts are worse than untruths. A story half told may condeinn, that if finished would justify. Mr. Henry Woodland, Stone Hill, Sellindge, near Hythe, was guided by this in giving his testimony, which makes it not soul's,- interesting, but valuable. He saYs, "In 1887 I was stricken with gravel. The pain I indured was beyond my ability to describe. T tried doctors and their .rmedicines, and I tried various rem' dies recommended me, but found no relief until I tivd Warner's Safe CKT,r, and after using nine bottles, the stone passed. I persevered -until I had passed five more stones ;so large, they tore and cut me frightfully, causing hemor- rhage, great .prostration, arid intense agony .; but now I am free from them, my he dth is reStored, and it is only fair that I tell my friends and possible fellow- sufferers what saved my life and delivered me from my misery."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>WAREINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WAREINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF ALDERMAN. 'PROTEST BY LIBERAL MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the Warrington Town goancil was held in the Council Chamber ef the Town Hall on Tuesday evening to elects successor to Alderman Platt, who has re- signed his seat on the. Town Council. There were present Alderman R. W. Francomb (in the chair), 'Aldermen Harrison, Sutton, Wright, Ashton ; Councillors P. Davies, 11. J. Lr)rigton, W. Shaw, Sbillcock, J. Baird, R. Heaton, J. Hill, J. Smethurst, J. Fairclough, Hutc.hinson, Cannell. 'l'. Food, Wil- kinson, R. T. Fairclough, 'llepherd, Piumpton, J. Muir, H. Roberts, J. Shaw Green, W. P. Skeltti ; Messrs. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), S. Wilson .2(deputy town clerk), J. Fairhurst (treasurer). 4-7 COMMUNICATION TO THE 'IIA7OR lIEFCSED HEARING. The CHLIUMAN said that the business before the Council was the election of an alderman in the rlace of Mr. John Platt. resigned. He had just had handed to Aim a communication bearing upon that question, and he would ask the Council whether he must road it then, -or after the business was settied. Councillor FLOOD moved that the ;letter be read at</p>
        <p>'once. Councillor MUIR moved that it be not read. In answer to a question, the TOWN CLERK said that 'the letters had never been taken before the other business • kad been disposed of. Councillor HILL : If it is meant to influence the voting, I hope you will not read it. Alderman HARRISON : Is it likely to influence the vesting? Councillor ROBRUTS said that he knew something of the contents of the communication. It could net pos- sibly influence the voters in any shape or form, and it was not intended to do so. He ,viould pre&amp;T as it was customary to read the Mayor's communications first, -that the letter should be read. The CHAIRMAN said that there were no Mayor's corn- ,- atunications mentioned in the Council summons, but if it was the wish of the Council to have the letter read then, Me would have great pleasure in reading it. The queston was then put that the later be read be- fore the election of alderman was proceeded with, but as only four members, via. Alderman Harrison, and Coun- cillors Wilkinson, Plumptort, and Flood voted for it, it was decided to read the letter at the end of the meeting. A member asked for the names for and against to be recorded, but the Town Clerk said that it could not ne</p>
        <p>done. EX-ALDERMAN BURGESS'S ETATTION TO THE cooKom. Councillor FLOOD: I ask your opinion on the question. I ask the Town Clerk, through you—it may be uncon- stitutional, or it may not be—but I want to take your opinion about the matter. In the case of the election of Alderman Ilurge4s, by resolution or otherwise, in what form was he electcd by the Council. I want to ask the Town Clerk's opinion about it. Was it a unanimous vote, or was it the resolution of a party ? (A. Voice : "No party," and hear, hear.) Councillor Saw rose to speak, but was inter- rupted by Councillor FLOOD, who remarked: lam asking a question ; and Councillor Shaw will be at liberty to criticise sylterwards. I want to know whether it is the constitutional usage of the Council that a resolution should be moved to invite an outsider, and can I move a resolution to that effect F I will be guided by the answer whether the re.,olution came from the Council or not in the case of Alderman Burgess, The TOWN CLERK : Alderman Burgess was re-elected in the us.ual way by voting papers. It cannot be done by resolution. Councillor FLOOD : Did the invitation come front the 'Council P The Tow); ('Link: I clout know, I am sure. Counoillor FLOOD: You cannot give the informa- tion ?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>ELECTION OF ALDERILLN.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF ALDERILLN.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN then asked for all voting papers to he handed in. The majority of the Tory members handed in their papers, but the only Liberal member to hand in his was Councillor Flood who said as he handed it in, " There has been no caucus meeting for. that." The' result of the voting was as follows : for Council- lor Hutchinson : Aldermen Ashton, B. W. Francon-lb, Wright, Sutton, and Harrison, Councillers Tinsley, Hen- herd, Hill, Shaw, Skelton, Muir, Carmen, Baird, Hut- chinson, J. Fairclough, Smethurst, Loneton, Davies, Heaton, and Shillcock ; for Councillor R. T. Fairclough ; Coune,ill Jr Flood. The CHAIRMAN said that there were 20 votes for Coun- cillor Hutchinson and one for Councillor R. T. Fair- dough, and he therefore ciclared, Councilor Hutchinson to be duly elected an alderman :of the borough of War- rington. Councillor HUTCHINSON said that he thanked the members of the Council very much for the honour they had conferred upon him that night—the honour of being elected alderman after eleven and a half yearn services as councillor. He hoped and trusted that during the time he stayed in the Council, he would always try to de his duty with integrity. (Applause.) Alderman HUTCHINSON was appointed returning offi- cer for the Whitecross Ward on thetmotion of the Chair- Inauseconded by Alderman Wright. A PROTIAST BY Ta LUSI4RAL MhDf BEM OF TIM COUNCIL. The CHAIUMAN then read the following letter :—" To his Worship the Mayor of Warrington—Sir,—We were aesirous that advantage should be taken of the present aPportunity of re-electing to the Council in the person at Mr. F. Monks, J.P., a gentleman whose abilities and long public services will entitle him to any honour the Corporation may have at its ctisposaleand whose presence again in the Connell would have been heartily approved a large majerity of our fellow-townsmen. We regret That the overtures made by us to the Conservative mem- bers of the Ceuncil with this object have been rejected, &amp;Lid that it has been determined that the offie,e shall he Ailed-by a nominee of the Tory party. Seeing that the Conservatives are already in a large :majority in the Council, and that therefore the alForption of the present 14eat is of no importance to them, that the seat was prev- iously:filled by a: Liberal in politics, and that the Liberal members of the Couecil by their members, and by virtue of the 'large body of burg,eases whom they represent, are entitled.to a relative proportion of the aidermanic aeati, we protest against the present unjustifiable andimpolitic action of the Conservative inejerity, as being opposed to the bestintereets of the bor.ugh— Councillor HMATON interrupting, said that he &amp;ejected to being lectured by anybody. It was entirely out of order. Why was net that said in the Council and not sent by lettflr ? COuncillnr lionsters.: We have not the chance. The roven Clerk ruled it out of order to speak on the election ot an alderman. Councillor IleAron : Then von have no right to send a let.ter which we vannot answer. rhe CHAIRM&amp;N Said thet the business of tneCennnil Was now over, and if 'Councillor Heaton ehiected to • hearing the letter read he could leave. He had now tOnly a few more lines to read. He would leave it in the ef few 'ineil whether he should read the remain- ulg Portion oft to letter. there being no motion not to read the letter, the Chairman continued the lettee., which was as follows As we are debarred ley the recent ruling ef the Town Clerk from public.lyeliscnssing this important matter in -he Council Chamber, we decline to take any part what- ever in the election, and have adopted the present plethod of placing or opinion thereon upon record. We are, very respectfully, S. J. Beaman.</p>
        <p>Joseph Garner. .George Pitt mpton. Charles J. Holmes. Georae Blackhurst. Joseph Shaw Green. Henry Roberts. F. W. Monks."</p>
        <p>April 10. 1894. _ _ _ Tlifre buing no discusiinn permisiahle upon this letter, the proceccEngs of the meeting terminated.</p>
        <p>The editor of a Bangkok paper thus " wtites at" one of his local contemporaries :—" And we may add, that if Mr. Dash Blank Astern k will have the courage to Peat to our face what iv. has the imp^rtinence to write We shall have much pleasure in discolouring his eye. We are lame, and giving away fully four stone, but we mean it."</p>
