      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>TUB WARRINGTON AMATEUR PHOTOGRAPHIC SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>TUB WARRINGTON AMATEUR PHOTO- GRAPHIC SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>EXIIIBITION OF LANTERN SLIDES.</p>
        <p>Considerable interest seems to be taken in the 'work of local amateur photographers, judging by the large attendance at the seventh annual exhibi- tion of lantern slides by members of the War- rington Amateur Photographic Society, in the Co- operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Tuesday evening. The programme was on similar lines to those of previous exhibitions. A short address by the presi- dent of the society commenced the proceedings,and a selection of vocal and instrumental music, contri- buted by first class local artistes interlarde(V.the exhibition of slides. Amongst those present were Messrs. T. Birchall, J.-P., John 'Geddes, J.P., C. Madelev, Dr. Gornall, J.P., and Councillor Bolton. Mr. T. J. Down, the president of the a!ociety, said he had once more the honour to ask their attention, for the . annual exhibition of lantern slides. Last year he (the speaker) did not think the pictures were quite up to the I previous standard. This he attributed to the fact that many of the slides had been prepared from "snap shot" Iphotographs. His opinion might not be of much value, hut he did think that the snap-shot business had been carried to excess, and he for one 'should not be sorry when the craze died out. There was no doubt it was useful at times, but the plates were nearly always under-exposed, and it reqiiired a very -clever man and great skill and care in developmeet to obtain really good pictures from them. It was quite impossible to make a good lantern slide from a poor negative. This year he thought the pictures would give them pleasere and satisfactiou. He was pleased to know from their Secretary (Mr. Pritchard) that the society I continued to prosper, and it now numbered 85 members. (Applause.) He (the speaker) told them some years ago that the society had thoroughly established itself and I taken a firm hold of the town, and his opinion was that it would go on and prosper. A well-known newspaper I had a leading article on amateur phetogra.phy, in which I it stated that its rapid progress was a marked feature of ' the times. He considered it a most eatisfaetory state of I things. It surely was a better recreation than some he I could mention. The number of amateur photographers I was increasing rapidly. His duties caused him to travel I a great deal and at every place he went to,he found that , the amateur photographer was there. (Applause.) Then again if they took up—say 'a photographic almanac— they would be surprised to find such a great number of firms in the manufacture of photographic appal atue, which kept employed thousands of skilled workmen, These exhibitions he might say were not got up for the purpose of making money. The object was rather to advertise the society, and to show what could be done, • and what was being done by them as amateur photo- graph vs in the hope that some who had not taken up photography might he induced to do se. (Applause.) The art of making lantern slides, useful and interesting as that was, was only one branch of the photographic art, and it was within the power of anyone of average alaility to be able'to produce silver prints, delicata catata or platinotypapictures. (Applause.) In addition to the slides there would be selections of music duritig the even- ing as usual, and some of the best musicians in the town had kindly come forward and offered their assistance. He therefore had pleasure, on lashalf of the Society in tenderineato-them their hearty and sincere thanks for the help they were about to eive to them. (Applause.) While engaged in exhibiting the slides Mr. Houghton alluded to the -President's remarks about the excessive use on " snap-shot " process, and said he held an oppesite view. There were about 200 slides exhibited, many of them being views taken during the holidays of the members in various parts of the British Isles. Mr. Kirby's slides, however, were all taken on the Continent. Local places of interest had not escaped the amateur's camera. The Ship Canal seems to have been a favourite spot for the photographer, and a number of views were thrown on the screen, some of them having been taken at the open- ing of the Canal, others being of ships at Latchford Locks. One view was of a passenger ferry boat just • entering the locks, and for tha information of the audi- ence it was pointed out by the Secretary (Mr. Pritchard) who described the various pictures, that they could travel by canal from Latchford to Manchester the same day! A number of flower studies taken at Mr. Cresfiehi's gardens, Walton Lea, were shown by Mr. Isaac Wilkin- son, and they made capital pictures. Several cloud • studies were much admired, as was also a competition slide containing the photographs of officers of the Society. The following members contributed slides : Messrs. JonesaG.•Harding, Aylward, T. Welsby, I. Wilkinson, Kirby, Houghton, J. Harding. Hall, Pritchard, and J. W. Cavanaeh. Messrs. Houghton and Harding were the lantern manipulators. The musical portion of the nroeramme was performed by the following artistes : Vocalists, Mrs. R. Ha Pear- son and -Mr. A. Griffiths; violin, Mr. R. H. Pearson ; bass concertina, Mr. T.R. Ford, and accompanist Mr.w. H. 'Payton. Mrs. Pearson's two songs were" On the Hill " and-1' 'When the heart is young," in both of which she was exceedingly successful, and received well merited applause. " Good company" was Mr. Griffiths' best effort, and as a matter of course he received vociferous applause. Complying with a recall, he sang "The Three Beggars," in such good style as to still further delitilit the audience. The instrumental part embraced violin soles by Mr. R. H. Pearson and bass concertina solos by Mr. T. R. Ford. Both artistes performed their , taski with their usual well-known skill, and secured hearty receptions. Mr. W. H. Payton assisted in the pianaforte accompaniments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>WA'RRINGTON MINISTER. AND HIS 'CONGREGATION.</head>
        <p>A WA'RRINGTON MINISTER. AND HIS 'CONGREGATION.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. S. McNair, pastor of Buckley-street chapel .has of late not been on the best of terms with some of the members of his congregation, and things have gone so far that be has. resigned his pastorate. The dispute has arisen owing to differ- ences of oninion concerning the working of the chapel. The Rev. 1/7..5. McNair preached at the chapel on Sunday morning and evening, and in the morning he announced that after ,much prayer he had decided on Monday to meet the treasurer and one outsider, and hand over all that ha had belonging to the church. He further stated that he would cease his connection with the place that night. There was a very large congregation present at the evening service, the first part of which was con- ducted by Mrs. MeNair. The Rev. W. L. McNair at the colinmencement of the sermon again announced his in- tention of resigning that night. He then took for his text Isaiah x. iii. 3 A bruised reed shall he not break and the smoking flax shall he not .quench ; he shall brine forth judgment unto trill)." During the coarse of his sermon he made many pointed allusions to the strained relationsexisting :between himself, and the congregation and at the close he bade them good bye. He said he would see them again, but not there. He parted from them that night as a pastor of the church voluntarily, freely, without a word of animosity or hatred to any man. But lie reserved the saving clause, that he would never se long as his name was MeNair, trust the same men whom he had trusted before, until he saw in them the pattern of Jesus Christ, until he saw them built up fully with the perfect love to cast out fear.—Mr. Simp- son a member of the congregation then ascended the platform. He said that the committee were unanim- ously of the opinion that for Mr. McNair to attach any conditions to the giving up of the money he held belong- ing to the church was preposterous, beceuse any reason- able man would turn over the money without any conditions at all to the proper parties, which were the Committee. They were only a body of workingmen there, but they did not like paying a man to tread upon them ,hecause the majority of them were trades' unionists. Mr. McNair had mentioned his resignation. They accepted his resignation unanimously, without express- ing any regret, as his manner had of late become Un- bearable.—Mr. MeNair commenced :to •reply to the speech of Mr. Simpson,and the now excited congregation became rather demonstrative. At -length a.hymn was struck up, and seeing that it was useless to go on with his address, Mr. McNair and his wife -left the chapel, and the service closed without the benediction being pronounced. An altercation took place outsidethe chapel between two of the members ef the congrega- tion, and continued for some time. On Monday the Rev. W. L. McNair he nded over all the money 'beloneing to the church, which was in his possession, and expressed hia intentioe of calling a public meeting should not the Committee do so.</p>
        <p>ORGE AUGUSTUS SAL&amp; - attack he had of Bronchitis and • to "The Land of the Golden Fleece," says :—" And in 'articular a couple of All- Rick's Porous Plastersclapped in—one on the chest and another between the shoulder nodes —soon set me right again," and the above re- -.arks are contained in his tter to the Daily Tcleyraplt. Allcock's Plastersinstantly stops all Aches and Pains, and promptly relieve and cure ..4prainsandStrains,Lumbago, Sciatica, Rheumatism, RAT- . tures, Bronchitis, Asthma, and all affections of the throat and lungs, acting directly on the tissues and dilating the pores of the skin they soon show their healing power, and justify all that has been said in their favour. The popular name and just fame of Allcock's imitation plasters has led to shameful piracy by un- scrupulous imitators, many of the plasters containing Lead and. Cantharides (Spanish Fly), involving serious risks that should never be permitted, unless under the direction of a medical man; it is therefore necessary to ask the public, for their own safeguard, to see that they get the genuine ALLCOCK'S PLASTERS. ,M,WOOK's of all Cheagf2ts, from is. lid, each.</p>
