      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>WARRINGTON HIGHWAY BOARD.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON HIGHWAY BOARD.</p>
        <p>A. meeting of this Board which was the last meet- ing of the year was held on Wednesday afternoon. The Chairman of the Board (Captain T. E. Withing- ton) presided, and there were also present, Messn,. James Wright, Jos. Clare' Menry Bate, Philip 3. Bate, Richard Ormand, James Millington, Wm. Royston, Reginald Owen, jun., J. Kerfoo:.%, James Houghton, and John Grounds, Mr. H.. B. White (clerk), A. White (assistant olerk),and Mr. J. Thomp- son surveyor),</p>
        <p>HALTON BRIDGE AGAIN. The Surveyor reported that he, in company with Mr. Millington, had mkt Mr. Tunstall to endeavour to pre- vail upon him to la the Board have the small strip of land near the Halton Bridge for improving the approaches to the bridge, hut Mr. Turistall refused to let them have the land. The Surveyor, however, was of the opinion that he could alter the road a little higher up, which would make some improvement.—Mr. Millington said the Surveyor had done his best to bring about an im- provement at the bridge. He was afraid that Mr. Thom- pson would have some difficulty in obtaining the land for his suggested improvement higher up the road. The inhabitants of the township were of tl4e opinion that nothing would be done until the Main Roads Committee of the County Council compelled the alteration to be made. THE ACCOUNTS, . _</p>
        <p>The Clculc r,al a s-uniniary of the accounts fer tha year. Tbe balance in hand at the commencement of the year was £123 18.4. 11d. The income included grants. of £4,100 from the County Council, and the balance in hand after paying expenses was £783 Os. 10c1. The Surveyor explained that there was a further sum of about £2OO owing them by the County Council. The accounts were passed unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>MEMBERS' ATTENDANCES.</head>
        <p>THE MEMBERS' ATTENDANCES.</p>
        <p>The following are the attendances of members of the board at the seven meetings held during the year. Mr. W. Gough 3, Mr. James Wright 3, Mr. Joseph Clare 6, Mr. Henry Bate 7, Mr. James Stainer 3, Mr. P. J. Bate 6, Mr. Ormrod 6, Mr. James Millington 7, Mr. William Royston 4, Mr. R. T. Lowe 2, Mr. R. Owen jun., 4, Mr. James Kerfoot 5, Mr. James Houghton 7, Mr. John Grounds 6. and Captain T. Ellaines Withirwton 6. THE BOARD AND THE PARISH COUNCILS BILL.</p>
        <p>A considerable amount of discussion arose on the ques- tion whether the Board should petition the County Council to allow them to continue as at present for a pen ii of three years. Mr. Owen asked the Surveyor whether the Parish Councils would have any effect on work that was being done by the Board.—Mr. Thompson said there was the improvement between Culcheth and Gdazehrook which would not be completed this year. This improvement should be completed by them as the two townships were under the Board at present. IF Culcheth was joined with the Leigh Council in Novem- ber it would be more difficult for the joint authorities to carry out the work.—Mr. Millington said at the Penketh township meeting they did not come to a uuaniinoue opinion as to the continuatioa of the Highway Board, but a hope was ex- Pressed that the Highway Board should be abolished in November.—Mr. Crmrod said as far as Lowton was con- cerned they wished to be disconnected with the Board and manage their own roads through the district coun- cil.—Mr. Houghton said at their township meeting they came to the conclusion that it would be better to con- tinue under the Highway Board for a further period of three years, and they authorised him to support that view. He therefore proposed that they petitiou the County Council to allow them to continue for three years from the 8:h November next—Mr. Owen seconded the proposition. He did so on the grounds that the District Councils would at the end of three years be in a better position to take over the work of that Highway Board. He was not surprised at Lowton objecting; that town- ship was hostile to the Board.—Mr. Millington moved that the matter be deferred to a future date in order to get more information on the matter.—Mr. Kerfoot seconded.—Or. Mr. Millington's motioa being put to the meeting only the proposer and seconder voted for it,and the original motion was aarried.—Mr. Royston and Mr. Ormrod remained neutral.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>DARESBUILY HIGHWAY BOARD.</head>
        <p>DARESBUILY HIGHWAY BOARD.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Board was held in the Board room, Daresbury, on Thursday afternoon. The chair was occupied by Mr. J. Charlton Parr, C.C., and there were also present : —Messrs. Hall, Jones, Barber, Bradford, J. Parr, Aaron, haddocks, Rigby, Billington, Beswick, Tickle, Darbyshire, Darlington, Weir,Burgess,Swinton (surveyor),and Linaker(clerk)</p>
        <p>A NEW RAILWAY COMPANY AND TIMID. BRIDGES.</p>
        <p>An agreenaerit with the Lancashire, Derbyshire, and East Coast Railway was submitted for approval to the floard. It stated that they agreed to erect the bridges teettigh with the exception of two which were to be 15 feet high. Mr. Darbyslaire contended that 15 feet was not high enough, and asked the Board not to sign the agreement. As the agreement did not state where the two bridges proposed to be built 15 feet high were situated, the Clerk was instructed to ascertain the posi- tion of them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>TUE PAYLISII COUNCILS BILL.</head>
        <p>TUE PAYLISII COUNCILS BILL.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Cheshire County Council asking whether the Board intended to apply for an ex- tension of time to continue as a Highway Board.—Mr. Hall said the matter rested with the Cheshire County Council. It would be better if they could continue for a longer period as they had several questions which were pending with the Ship Canal Company with re- gard to the taking over of roads which a newly elected body would be unaware of.—Mr. Darbyshire thought it was very desirable that the Board should exist until final arrangements had been made with the Ship Canal Company and the Board.—Mr. Burgess said the town- ship he represented was in favour of continuing under the Board.—lt was decided, on the motion of Mr. W. Bradford, seconded by Mr. Aaron, that the Board ask the Cheshire County Council to allow them to continue for three years longer.</p>
        <p>STRONG COMPLAINTS AGAINST THE CANAL COMPANY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Leader Williams wrote askieg if it was possible for a committee of the Board to meet him the following Tuesday.—Mr. S. Burgess said he thought the Grinal Company had treated the Board in a very shabby manner. They had been arranging to meet Mr. Williams for about three months. He hoped the Board would show them that they were not going to he annoyed with them. Mr. Burgess cited two or three instances in which the recommendations of the Board had been ignored by the Canal Company. He moved that all agreements between that Board and the Company'res- pecting railway crws:ngs on the highways should ter- minate within fourteen days.—Mr.Aaron seconded the proposition, and said the Canal Company would promise anything to get their own Way, but they never carried out their promises.—The Chairman thought it was not advisable to take such strong steps. He was of the oisiffion that it would be better to give them notice that the Board would take those steps if the meeting with Mr. Williams was not arranged.—Mr. Burgess withdrew his motion, and the Clerk was instructed to write t;) the Canal Company stating that if Vie meeting of the com- mittee with Mr. Leader Williams be not arranged within fourteen days,notice would be given that all agreements with the company respecting level and other crossings should cease within fourteen days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>OVERDRAWING AT TIIII BANK.</head>
        <p>OVERDRAWING AT TIIII BANK.</p>
        <p>The Board decided to ask the bank to allow them to overdraw on their own account for a longer period. The Clerk said they had overdrawn on their account £1,300. They had been unable as yet to make arrangements for b)rrowing the money needed for an improvement.—Mr. Aaron said for a number of years they had £2OOO in the bank and re- ceived no interest for it. Now that they wanted to borrow some, the bank wanted four per cent. The Chairman explained that the Board did dot pa a trea- surer, and when the bank acted as such, n 9 interest was paid.—Mr. Aaron was of the opinion that it would have been better to have had a treasurer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>BOARD RESPONSIBLE FOR DAMAGES.</head>
        <p>THE BOARD RESPONSIBLE FOR DAMAGES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Whitfield, jour., wrote to the Board stat- ing that his father met with a serious at:einem whilst driving at Walton, on a road belonging to the Board, owing to an engine belonging to the Ship Canal Com- pany being on the road. He had been informed that the HiL:hway Board were liable, as they had given the Canal Company powers to run their engines on the road. Dr. Bowden, house surgeon at the Warrington infirmary also wrote that Mr. Whitfield was an inpatient at the [lift/nary from injuries received six weeks previously. —The Clerk said he had informed the solicitors for the Canal company of the matter, and they had replied that it would be brought before the directors. The Board were liable for damages in the first instance, but under the agreement,the Canal Company were bound to recoup them. The Clerk was instructed to forward to the Canal Company any claim that mighebe made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>TEE NEW RATE.</head>
        <p>TEE NEW RATE.</p>
        <p>The Clerk stated that the total expenditure was £4,270 16s. 51. The ;rateable value of the townships was £151,332, and it was decided to fix a highway rate of sevenpence in the £ which would produce £4,413. The rate i a id. babe £ less than the previous rate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>EXCITING INCIDENT IN A MIDLAND EXPRESS. A MAD BRIDEGROOM.</head>
        <p>EXCITING INCIDENT IN A MIDLAND EXPRESS. A MAD BRIDEGROOM.</p>
        <p>_ Soon after the forenoon express for St. Pancras left Derby on Monday (says the Central News) a number of female passonger2 who were in the dining saloon rushed jute the smoking-room sasing that a madman was in the Pullman car. Sir Thomas Roe, M.P., and his two nephews were in the smoking-room, and they went to the assistance of the women. A rough-looking man, respectably dressed, was found struggling in the grasp of two or three persons and uttering imprem dons of the most violent kind. It transpired that the man, who was from Matlock Bridge,had only that morning been married, and he and his bride were on their way to St. Pancras, where a brother was to meet them. The next station was Luton, and it was suggested that the Ivan should be put out there. It was finally decided, however, that the women passengers, including the bride, should be trans- terred to another carriage. 'This was done at Luton ; and the man was kept well guarded till St. Pancras was leached, when he was given into safe custody.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>WARRINGTON CRICKET AND BOWLING</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CRICKET AND BOWLING</p>
        <p>CLUB.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Warrington Cricket and Bowling Club, the annual meeting ot members was held in the Lion Hotel Assembly Room on Monday night. The president (Mr. R. T. Fairclough) occu- pied the chair. In his opening remarks he said that he was glad to see such a good attendance of mem- bers present. The members made good use of the field and he thought that the members' wives and daughters ought to make more use of the ground than they did.</p>
        <p>A NSW WAITE PROPOSED. Mr. Unsworth (secretary) moved the following resolu- tions :—" That the name of the club be altered to "The Warrington Bowling, Cricket and Association Football "That a sub-committee be appointed to revise, amend, add to, or alter the existing rules of the club, with the object of more effectually amalgamating the Association Football Club with the Cricket and Bowling Club, or for any other purpose, and that the results of their labours be submitted to the next annual meeting for confirmation." He said that the Football Club was but a child of the Cricket and 13.-mling Club. Last year they were able to hanJ cver £l5 to the Cricket Club. There was no doubt that it would be much better for the Foot- ball Club to become part and parcel of the Cricket Club, but they must be careful to keep the power in the Cricket Club. _</p>
        <p>- Mr. C. Worthington said he should prefer the name of the Club to remain as it was.</p>
        <p>Mr. Kii.rnan said the-p:ropc;sed name was too long. fie had a name much shorter and it would cover all. Let them call it "The Watrirr-4ton Omnium Club." (Laugh- ter. • Omnium meant "all." . _</p>
        <p>It was ultimately decided to leave the alteration of the name over until the neat annual meeting. Mr.- Tinnion propoied that a sub-committee be formed to alter and amend the rules, and it was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Young proposed, and Mr. G. Pilling seconded, that Mr. 'Thomas Morris be elected president for the year. It was carried unanimously.. Mr. Wm. Dooley proposed a vote of thanks to the retiring president and Mr. I'. Morris seconded.—The accounts were then sub- mitted and passed, the receipts being £436 4s. 10d., and the expenditure £354 3s. Bd., showing a balance of £B2 Is. 2d. The other officers were re-elected. Mr. Reginald Smith was re-elected captain of the Ist team and Mr. Leonard Smith was elected captain a the second cricket team. Mr. T. Morris was elected captain of the Bowling Club, and Mr. S. A. Wright captain of the Tennis Club. Mr. George Piling was re-elected secretary, and Mr. D. Tinnion auditor of the Bowling Club. The following committees were elected :—Cricket : Mr. Crosfield, Mr. Chidlaw, Mr. Leip;di, Mr. Voisey, and Mr. Smith. Bowl- ing : Mr. Higginbottotn, Mr. R. T. Fairclough, Mr. W. Young and Mr. Leigh. Tennis: Mr. Foster Jackson and Mr. Hannah.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The weekly meeting of this society on Sunday was presided over by Mr. Themes Morris, F.G.S. In the course of a short address he related an incident which had come under his own observation, and illustrated the good work that P.S.A. society was doing. He alsci gave some good advice to the young men present. Mr. Wel- ford, of Lymm, was the speaker for the afternoon. He referred to the recent statement of Mr. Gladstone, that the era which was just closing had been one of emanci- pation. It had witnessed the emancipation of the slaves, of the poor man's bread,of the public press,of education, of workingeneri's right of combination in trades' unions, an last but not least the emancipation of the electoral rights of the people. For the first time in the history of their country the government was in the hands of the COMMOri people. Already they saw the spirit of the new forces that were coming into play and the changesthat were destined to take place under the new order of things. They had seen the Parish Councils Act, the Emeeloyers Liability Bill,and Labour commissions. They heard of labour churches, and of labour members of Parliament. These things spoke to them more loudly than anything, and pointed them to the changes that must inevitably ensue as the years went by. The speaker then spoke on the abuse of power,and suggested three rules for guidance in the use of the power they possessed. (1) The practice of compromise ; (2) that they sh)uld not forget they were members of a great empire, and (3) that they should he guided by the prin- ciples of the religion of the Lord Jesus Christ. The ad- dress throughout was listened to with close attention, and was frequently applauded. Mr. W. H. Wallington and Miss Coubrough contributed solos. The Rev. J. Yong., Messrs. A. Birch, and J. Hawthorn also took part in the service. There were about one thousand men present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>CONCILIATION IN TUE COAL TRADE.</head>
        <p>CONCILIATION IN TUE COAL TRADE.</p>
        <p>Mr. B. Pickard, M.P., speaking at Barnsley on Tues- day, said the coalowners had made a great mistake in objecting to the minimum wage, for they had left the gate open to proprietors who had no desire either to pay fair wages or get fair prices for their coal. The miners had deternsined that they would secure a living wage, and if they were to be met on the Board of Conciliation with the Old cut-throat policy, then November 17, 1894, would be a red-letter day in the annals of the coal trade. Mr. Haslam, the Derbyshire Miners' leader, speaking at Alfreton on Tuesday, said Lord Shand had distinctly stated that he had an open wind on the minimum wage question, and it would have to be settled on its merits. dt a mass meeting of miners at Hucknall Torkard on Tuesday, Mr. Bailey returned to his attack on Lord Shand, who, in his opinion—and he said it without mincing or flinching—was unfit to occupy the position to which the Speaker had called him. A resolution was passed condemning Lord Shand's decisions, at the meet- ing of the Board of Conciliation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>MR. BAILEY AND LORD SHAND.</head>
        <p>MR. BAILEY AND LORD SHAND.</p>
        <p>A crowded meeting of miners was held at Sutton-in- Ashfield on Thursday night, when Mr. William Bailey, the men's agent, reverting to his attack on Lord Sh and, said when they came to examine the work of the Coal Trade Conciliation Board in all its nakedness and saw his Lordship making rules giving him absolute power to decide the wages of 300,000 man they would recognise that great danger existed and that it was time to pro- test. He wished to know from Mr. Pickard and his colleagues who gave them power to agree to striking out the reference to the minimum wage, especially in view of the resolution passed at the Miners' Conference at Birmingham. The Nottinghamshire men had never empowered him (Mr. Bailey) to agree to this, and he would not consent to sit under Lord Shand and work with the CLneiliation Board unless the minimum wage was inserted to protect those he represented. He found his colleagues were exceed- ing their duty and that Lord Shand was not an impar- tial chairman, but a biassed and prejudiced partiean. Be had been told he ought to apalogise to his Lordship, and make reparation for the injustice he had done, but he considered the interest of 300,000 men more import- ant than that of his offended Lordship, despite the news- paper criticisms of his action. He thought they were drifting Into a positien of danger and it was time to pro- test. Newspaper writers might describe Lord Shaud as an impartial chairman, but in his (the speaker's) view the actinn of the chairman of the Board was that of blind partisanship. He was convinced that if any one of the coalowners had been asked to preside he would have acted with more oUscre,tion, more diset.imination, and less indecent haste Ulan Lord Shand. The Concilia- tion Board was only another name for the Coalowners' Association seeking a reduction in wages.—A resolution antagonistic to Lord Mend was passed.</p>
        <p>It is stated that at the next meeting of the Concilia- tion Board the comments of Mr. Bliley, the Nottingham- shire miners' representative, will be called in question, and an expression of confidence in Lord Shand will be adopted by the miner's delegates. THE STACKING OF COAL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of miners was held on Thursday night in the Enys-street Schoolr.otn, Pendleton, to consider the best means to be taken towards restricting the output of coal. Mr. Thomas Greensll, miners' agent, said the question had become a serious one, as large stacks of coal were accumulating on most of the pit banks in Lancashire. A special meeting was to be held next :saturday to consider the reports from various districts. It was resolved that miners should be asked to work four days a week and eight hours a day,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>SHOCKING DISCOVERY AT</head>
        <p>SHOCKING DISCOVERY AT</p>
        <p>PARTIN GTON.</p>
        <p>A LABOURER'S SAVINGS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Davenport, Church Farm, Partington, near Lymm, on t"unday morning, found the dead body of John llennigan, aged 25, labourer in his employ, on his premises. Deceased was discovered in a kneel- ing position, with h his head resting on a hamper. lie had evidently bled to death from a ulcer in. the leg. Deceased had suffered from the ulcer for some time, and it is supposed that he had loosened the bandages to ease the pain. He was last seen alive about six o'clock on Saturday evening, and was found at half-past eight the following morning. He was then quite stiff awd cold. Information was sent to Constable Bebbington, and the body was subsequently removed to an out- house on the premises. The constable searched deceased and found a bank book on the Warrington Savings' Bank for £97 odd. Thirty-five or thirty-six pounds in cash were also found. An inquiry into the circumstances attending the death was conducted on Tuesday afternoon by Mr. H. C. Yates. The wift'. of the deceased identified the body, and Mr. Davenport, P.C. Bebbington, and a man named Holmes gave evidence. The jury returned a verdict of" Death from natural causes."</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S CfroA.—" A food alike suitable for bu'lding up the growing body, and for repairing the waste which is incidental to all the processes of life."—Heal Is. (1)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Council was held at tke T.)wn Hall, on Tuesday afternoon, under the presidency of the Mayor (Alderman F. 11. Gossage). There was also present :—Aldermen Sadler, Tilley, Tirnmis, Wareing and Williamson: Cuuncillors Davies, Wright, Neil, Shuttleworth, Longton, Gos- sage, Singleton, Barlow, Quinn, Owens, Gough. O'Donnell, Bullough, Hodgkinson, Smith and Shores; Messrs. H. S. Oppenheim (town clerk). T. Craik (deputy town clerk). S. Hoyle (accountant), J. S. Sinclair (surveyor), I. Carr (engineer), Dr. O'Keete (medical officer), and W. Lowe (nuisance inspector).</p>
        <p>HIGHWAY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this Committee held on the 28th March, it was recommended that the Surveyor prepare as early as possible plans and estimates for the making and completion of the whole of the private streets and passages in the West Bank and Simm's Cross Districts, the %Vest Bank district to lee proceeded with first, and that he proceed with the sewering and making of the streets adjoining the site cf the proposed new technical schools. The following plans were submitted : 6 cot- rages, New-street, for Mr. Andrew Poulson, disapproved ; 3 houses, Peelhouse-lane. for Mr. William Willan, dis- approved; School., Waterloe-road, for Primitive Meth- odists, approved ; 22 houses intended streets off Albert- roal, for Mr. Eiwin Wood, disapproved; Post ()Mee 'buildings, Victoria-road, for Roberts Bros., approved.— The confirmation of the ininutes was move d by Alder- man Sadler and passed unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>HEALTE COMMITTRIL</head>
        <p>HEALTE COMMITTRIL</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Committee was held on the 30th March. It was recommended that Inspector Strickland be appointed inspector under" The Contagious Disease (Animals) Acts " upon the same terms and in place of Inspector Pattison, removed. Superintendent iNghley reported the presence of rabies in the neighbourhood of the Borough, and it was resolved that the matter be con- sidered at a special meeting of the Committee. The. Infectious Diseases Hospital report stated that the num- ber of patients at last meeting was 0, admitted since, 2 ; discharged since, 0 ; died since, 1 ; patients sill in, 1. Accident Hospital—number of patients at last meet- ing. 8 ; admitted since, 8 ; discharged, 7 ; died, 1 ; patients still in, 8. The Medical Officer's report stated that the births were, 89 ; deaths (total), 46 ; including children under 1 year, 7 ; children between 1 and 5 years, 10 ; zymotic diseases. 2 ; inquests, 1. 'The cases of infectious diseases notified were 16. Coun- cillor Neil proposed the adoption uf the minutes.—Coun- cillor Smith seconded.—Councillor Quinn said from the Medical Officer's report he noticed that a man was taken to the Hospital in the horse ambulance and his injuries were materially aggravated in consequence of the bad , springs of the ambulance. He asked whether this was receiving attention.—Councillor Owens referred to the sewer near the Central Station, and said tenants were removing from the neighbourhood in consequence of the stench arising from it.—Councillor Neil in replying said that the springs of the ambulance was in hand, and the sewer near the Central Station was beinerattended to. The minutes were passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>GAS AND WATER CnIIMITTEB.</head>
        <p>GAS AND WATER CnIIMITTEB.</p>
        <p>This Committee held their meeting on the 3rd April. The report of. gas tests made by Mr. C. E. Bellamy, of Liverpool, on the 19th and 29th inst., read : Illuminating p0wer,19.12 iind 1931. candles respectively. Gas delivered 14,502,000 cubic feet against 12,227,000 cubic feet last year..—The Engineer submitted pians and estimate for the erection ef new workshops, stores, &amp;c., at the gas works, Ann-st. east, and the same were con- sidered. It was recommended that the plans and esti- mates be approved and that the Engineer be instructed to carry out the works at an estimated cost of £1,065 4s. It was also recommended that a sub-committee consist- ing of Alderman Wareing and Williamson, and Council- lors Gough and Bullough be appointed to consider and report to this Committee from time to time on matters affecting the managemeet of the tire Brigade.—Alderman Ti:nmis moved the adoption of the minutes, which were paseed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>GENERAL PURPOSES AND PARLIAMENTARY' COMMITTEE</head>
        <p>GENERAL PURPOSES AND PARLIAMENTARY' COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>ee A special meeting was held on the 21st March. The resolution passed at the last meeting of the Council having been read the Committee proceeded to consider as to the best and most appropriate method of establish- ing a cemetery in the borougn. It was resolved that a deputation consisting of the Chairman, the Deputy Chairman, and Mr. Councillor Davies, with the Town Clerk, be appointed to visit other municipal boroughs where cemeteries hays been established since 1879 under the Burials Acts, and under the Public Health (Inter- ! moats) Act, 1879, respeetively,and to obtain information with reference thereto. And also that the Town Clerk advertise for offers of suitable sites for a cemetery.— The monthly meeting was held on the 4th April. A letter from Mr. F. Harrison, general manager, to the L. and N.-W. Railway, Euston, was read, stating that his directors were unable to see their way to make any alterations in the train service to and from Widnes for the mouth of April, but that the suLject would be fully reviewed later on, and the representations of the Coun- cil as regards trains and station accommodatien• duly considered.—The Town Clerk was instructed to reply thereto, asking that the Council may be fnrnished with particulars of any proposed alterations beim° the same are brought into operation, and also to specially request that the additional waiting room required at ! Appleton be provided at once, and that the 9.45 train from Liverpool be stopped at Ditton so as to form a connection with the 10.5 p.m. train from Ditton to Widnes. It was further recommended that the Joint Committee appointed or to be appointed under section 60 of the Local Government Act, 1894, be requested to take into consideration the question of representation of the various parishes throughout the Prescot Union upon the Board of Guardians, in order that such representa- tion may be more in proportion to the ratable values and , populations in such parishes respectively than at present ! and that for the purpose of such representation the said Joint Committee be further requested to divide the ! Parish of Widnes into wards. —Alderman Wareing moved the confirmation of minutes which were passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>AMATEUR DRAMATIC PERFORMANCE</head>
        <p>AMATEUR DRAMATIC PERFORMANCE</p>
        <p>AT ItUNCOItN.</p>
        <p>"PIKE O'CALLAGHAN."</p>
        <p>The St. Edwatd's Amateur Dramatic Society, Runcorn, presented on Wednesday evening in the Runcorn Public Hall, an original romantic Irish drama entitled " Pike O'Callaghan." 'Tile object of the performance was to raise sufficient funds to liquid- ate the debt upon the St. Edward's R.C. Schools, which had been contracted through the managers having car- ried out certain improvements required by her Majesty's Inspector ot schools. The drama, which consists of two atits, isbased upon the great Irish Rebellion of the 17th century, the part of Ireland selected for the develop- ments of events being in the picturesque county of Wicklow. The following is a re-production ef the caste : —Sir J. B'ackadden ( a Royalist), Mr. F. Coogan; Neil O'Connor (an Attainted Rebel), Mr.R. Williamson ; Pike O'Callaghali (a Faithful Adherent), Mr. Mitchell ; Serge- ant Snipes (Man of Discipline), Mr. J. Hassey ; Red Reins (Spy and Informer), Mr. J. Parker; Captain Wilson, Mr. J. Gleeson ; James (Servant), Mr. R. Cole- man ; Ledy Broughton (Betrothed to Neil), Mrs. Mutch; Honor O'Callaghan (Wife to Pike), Mrs. Hardie ; Pike's Children, Masters Durr and Gleeson. The Royalist, Sir J. Blackadder, desires to win the hand of Lady Brough- ton who is already betrothed to O'Connor, and in order to attain his object he causes O'Connor to be attainted as a rebel. Consequently the Royalist troops are ordered to scour the neighbourhood for the rebel, but 'Pike O'Callaghan" and his wife effectually conceal him. Through the information of Red Rufus, Pike is taken prisoner, but is subsequently rescued from prison by his master O'Connor. In their escape they meet a detachment of soldiers who take O'Connor pris- oner. In the meantime a pardon has been granted the latter, but Blackadder, in his villainy destroys it—(he supposes)—because Lady Broughton will not consent to become his wife. The real document, however, is taken away, unobserved, by Pike's wife, and a fictitious one put in its place. Blackadder is determinedthat O'Connor shall be executed and gives orders to that effect to the troops under his charge. He is prevented, however, by the sudden appearance of Honor with the real pardon, accompanied by Pike and Lady Broughton. Of course, as a natural consequence, the happiness of O'Connor, Pike, Lady Broughton, Honor and others is undisguised, whilst the anger of Blackadder and Rufus, at seeing their schemes frustrated, is intense. Taking the company as a whole they discharged their various parts in a most meritorious manner, and are deserving of the high- est praise. At the conclusion of the piece a laughable farce was presented known as " Popping the Question," which created no end of mirth, the different characters being represented by Mrs. Smith, Misses E. Gleeson, J. Coulon, E. Done. Messrs. E. Conan and Flaherty. Dur- ing the progress of the entertainment selections of music were pla) ed by the St. Eciward'a Brass Band, whilst songs were sung by Mrs. Hardie and Mr. Parker, the accompaniments being performed by Mr. Albert Ravenscroft in his usual efficient manner. The scenery was lent by Mr. H. Caddick, manager of the Runcorn Theatre, to whom the society desire to express their warmest thanks. The piece was repeated on lbursday night with marked success, the various artistes going through their respective parts in very goad style.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>PARLIAMENTARY GOLF HAND [CAP.</head>
        <p>THE PARLIAMENTARY GOLF HAND [CAP.</p>
        <p>In the second ties of the Parliamentary golf handicap at Nurzedown on Thursday, the 1-In. T. W. Legti, M.P.' who won the event outright last year, beat Mr. C. W Campion by 4 up and 2 to play.</p>
        <p>The poor of England are tly,se poor in health as well as in pocketbook. When health is undermined through malnutri- tion or other calves, Scott's Emulsion of Pure Norwegian CA Liver Oil, with h.% pn,,hisplet-s, is just wl:at is neede,i. Scott's Emulsion is a food rich in fat, and nourishes the system tuck to het ch where plain cod liver oil merely nauseates and taxes ti's' stomaeh. All Chemists sell Scott's Emulsion. 2s. 6d. and 4s. 6&amp;—(5)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>RUNCORN ANNUAL BAND CONTEST.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN ANNUAL BAND CONTEST.</p>
        <p>Under the arspiees of the Runcorn Town and District Band, the third annual band contest took place on Sat- urday afternoon on the Runcorn football ground. There was a moderate number of spectators, and there was rather more than usual interest manifested in the con- test as compared with Drevicu3 years. The amount offered in prizes was £53 13s. which sum was divided a 3 follows :—First prize, £2l 95., consisting of £l2 in casl: and a B.flat prototype cornet, value £9 95.; second prize 1385., consisting of £5 in cash and E. flat protetyp soprano cornet, value £8 Bs.; third prise, £lO 181., con- sisting of £2 10s. in cash, and a prototype contestiAg flugal horn, value £8 Bs.; fourth prize, £8 175., consisting of £1 10s. in cash, and a B. flat soloists' prototype trombone, value £7 75.; fifth pr'ze, £2 in cash, and sixth prize. £1 in cash. Messrs. Besson and Co. ot London supplied the instru- ments and as an extra prize gave one of their conduc- tor's batons, valued £1 is., for the band containing the best solo cornet. The contest, which was open to all amateur brass bands which had not taken a prize of , more than £2O in cash during 1893, was engaged in by sixteen bands, although there were 28 entries. Of this; number only eighteen turned up. The test selection was " Elixir of Love," (Wright and Round). So well was the piece played by each band that the adjudicator, Mr. A. D. Keate, of Denton' experienced some difficulty in arriving at a decision. The contest began at three o'clock, and at eight o'clock the awards were announced as follows :-Ist prize, Eecles Borough • 2nd and 3rd prizes divided between Atherton Public and Widnes Sub- scription ; 4th. Goodshaw, near Rawtenstall ; sth, Pemberton Old; 6,h, Silvertown Silver Band. The Atherton Public Band also gained the extra prize given for the best silo crnst. The members of the Runcorn Town and District Band undertook all arrangements, Mr. T. Wood discharging the duties of secretary. Mr. George Riley, of the Bridge Hotel, catered for the re- freshments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>REV. PETER MACKENZIE AT RUNCORN. LECTURE ON "PRINCE ABSALOM:,</head>
        <p>THE REV. PETER MACKENZIE AT RUNCORN. LECTURE ON "PRINCE ABSALOM:,</p>
        <p>The Rev. Peter Mackenzie paid another visit to Runcorn on Tuesday and in the afternoon preached to a large congregation in the St. Paul's Wesleyan Chapel. In the evening he delivered his popular lecture entitled "Prince Absalom" in the Public Hall, Runcorn. There was a large attendance and the chair was taken by Mr. Phillip Speakman, J.P., C.C., who was supported on the platform by the Revs. W. Arrowsmith, T. Wilkes, and J. Hanby, Messrs. Flarrissn, S. Rope, T. Boote, W. Temperley, T. Simister, and Beckett. The Chairman, in a brief address, said that night he was keeping a bargain made about eighteen months ago, namely to preside over that lecture and to entertain , their old friend the Rev. Peter Mackenzie. He remem- bered the lecturer visiting Runcorn in 1858, and seeing him nearly toppling over the Bruns wick pulpit. He (the ' chairman) always felt, after having heard their friend, some things that went straight to the heart. In preach- ing, the rev, gentleman always began with Christ, and finished with Christ. (Applause.) He had great plea- sure in calling upon him to deliver his lecture. The Lecturer, who was received with applause, then entered into a description of the character of Prince Absalom, his family connections, and his wealth. The lecture was delivered with all Mr. Mackenzie's well- known power, and the rev, gentleman concluded his remarks by appealing to all to take warning and endea- vour to act in an upright and straightforward manner, and to abstain from drink and bad company. (Ap- plause.) On the proposition of Mr. Pope, seconded by Mr. Sitnister, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded the Rev. Peter Mackenzie for his brilliant lecture. A similar compliment was paid to the Chairman, for presiding, on the motion of Mr. Mackenzie seconded by Mr. Beckett. The proceedings then terminated by the singing of a hymn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>MACMILLAN AND CO.'S NEW BOOKS.</head>
        <p>MACMILLAN AND CO.'S NEW BOOKS.</p>
        <p>The thirty-first edition of "The Statesman's Year Book" (1894) was published in March. This edition has been completely revised, in most cases by the Govern- ment of the various States included in the year book. With a view to making the section dealing with the navies of the various States of real service at present, when the question of our naval position is exciting so much interest, this section has been entirely re-cast and practically re-written by the editor, Mr. Keltie, assisted by Mr. John Leyland, a well known authority on such matters. All important changes that have occurred during the past year have been registered, such as the French annexations in Siam, and the vaf ions re-arrange- ments that have taken place in Africa. The facts and figures have all been revised after official returns, mak- ing the annual thoroughly reliable, invaluable for refer- ence, and perfectly up to date. A new edition at a popular price, of Mrs. Steele's new novel (Miss Stuart's Legacy) deserves to command a large sale. The scenery depicted is principally in India, the characters are Anglo-Indian, and the tale makes us we:l-acquainted and familiar with home fife and work in that country and climate. Mrs. Steele is evidently a keen judge of character, and has the capability of defin- ing it with power as well as refinement. Some of the pictures she pourtrays of domestic difficulties, pleasures, and disappointments, reveal a delicacy of conception and description eminently woman-like and pathetic. We hope to have the opportunity of reading many more of Mrs. Steele's novels—if this is the first, may it not be the last I "Blanche, Lady Falaise," by J. 11. Shorthouse, is the name of another new novel published at the same time and price. It is descriptive of English scenery, princi- pally that of Devonshire (a county for which the writer evidently has an affection) and English men and women. The intricacies and subtleties of the human mind, as well as the weaknesses of human nature are depicted with rare strenvth and skill by the author, compelling us to yield to the fascination of his high tone of expression, and to admit the artistic rendering of the narrative. The interest in the characters, (of whom there are not many), never flags, but each one is true to nature, and described with a masterly hand and mind.</p>
        <p>THE LARK. • Hail, charming bird of early spring, Now soaring in the blue above: How swift and graceful is thy wing, How pure thy warbling song of love! 0 Lark, 'tis surely thine to know The changings of the circling year; That thou may'st shun the winter's snow; And sing when daisy cups appear. Though fields be bleak, and keen the air, No sadness marks that song of thine; For earth and heaven to thee are fair, When lit with sunbeams all divine. Thou buildest on the turfy ground, Thy whole estate a tiny nest;: But heaven's wide arch alone may bound The freedom that inspires thy breast. Perchance thou hart thy little cares, Some sorrows, too, on thee may fail : But there is one dear mate that shares, And sympathises with them all. Her love adorns thy mossy home, All softly bends thee to her will ; And wheresoe'er ye rest, or roam, It makes thy world more lovely still. Sing on, sweet minstrel of the plain, Thou harbinger of birds and flowers: We need thy hope-inspiring strain To cheer this lonely heart of ours. Thy voice recalls the distant years, The vale and home where we were born And 0, how fair the scene a pptars. How blithe the lark that woke the morn! Noe this alone: thy warblings seem As notes that tell the weary heart, That life is still a lovely dream, When love and hope their joys impart. Some times of trial there will be. . Some shadows cross our little day: But what of these when we ran see, The heavenly glory tar away! JAS. H. W. CLINDINTNING.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>IMPROVED CARRIAGE SEATS ON THE CHESHIRE LINES.</head>
        <p>IMPROVED CARRIAGE SEATS ON THE CHESHIRE LINES.</p>
        <p>The Cheshire Lines Committee have this week begun to introduce an important improvement in the equipment of their coaches on the Liverpool and Manchester ser- vice. This is the introduction of a patent wire woven convex seat which does not require any springs or pad- ding of any description, with the exception of an • ver- lying covering of hair. Thus the prevailing boxed or welled seats, with their facilities for the retention and accumulation of dust, will be superseded by a structure which will render these insanitary and objectionable conditions impossible, because whatever dust may for a time rest on The seat falls through the wire meshes and on to the carriage floor, to be swept away in the course of the regular supervisien. In addition to this, the seat presents a plain surface without any buttons, and is fitted with screws at each end, so that it can be regularly taken off without any extra trouble for the purpose of cleansing and repainting. This is an irn- provement appreciable , y all, esp,.,cially by those who have constantly to travel by rai:, and frequently find themselves in course of an ail-night journey in crowd,d ! stuffy compartments, rendered all the most nauseous by the condition of the cushions and cavities of the carri- age seats. The flexibility and strength of the wirework 1 of the new mode of construc,ion forms a feature of ad- ditional comfort. We understand that the contract for supplying the seats has been Riven to the Lengford Wire- works, Warrington, who are co:Jet!rned in the patent of Mr. Edwin Woods.</p>
        <p>MRS. S. A. ALLEYS</p>
        <p>wORLD'S</p>
        <p>HAIR RESTORER.</p>
        <p>It is not an experiment, but has been in use for over sixty years throughout the civilized world.</p>
        <p>RESTORES THE YOUTHFUL COLOUR.</p>
        <p>It renews life, strength, and beauty, and restores the natural colour to gray hair.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>CONSUMPTION,</head>
        <p>CONSUMPTION,</p>
        <p>BRONCHITIS AND ASTHMA.</p>
        <p>I will demonstrate to the whole world, and to sufferers from these unfortunate maladies, how they can be perman- ently cured, by an entirely .New Method a! Treat then% with- out the chance of failure. Write with full oontiedee to Mr. WILLIAMS, 11, Borwood Place. Norfolk Crescent, London.. He will send you full particulars free of all charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>IMPERIAL PA RLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PA RLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commetis on Monday evening, Mr. Moneur stated, in rep!; to yeestions, that the accounts of the disturbances at Cork arising out of religious animosity had been grossly exaggerated. Ile was in- formed that not one of the preachers had received the slightest injury, and that the assaults which had taken place were in the nature of horseplay, and invariably committed by women and boys. Sir W. HARCOURT moved that for the remainder of the session Government business should have priority on Tuesdays and at morning sittings en FAidays. He pointed out that the customary eight days per month was absolutely insufficient for the transaction of Gov- ernment business. Mr. BALFOUR said it was absolutely impossible to assent to this demand. Mr. J. REDMOND said he would not take the responsibility of voting in favour of the motion unless he received assurance that the Evicted Tenants Bill would he introduced at once and pushed on with the same iacrlity as the Registration Bill. Mr. MORLEY assured the hon. member of his ear- nestness in this matter. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN said the Government had nobody to thank but themselves for the position they were in. Mr. DALZIEL, who had a motion down on the subj,ct of the Weise of Lords, saki private members on his side of ti e House did not wish to embarass the Government. The Mete] tis of CARMAR- THEN moved an amendment limiting the resolution of the Government to the pried before Whitsuntide. On a division Lord CARMA.RTHEN'S amendment was rejected by 263 to 244. Mr. HANBURY then moved an amend- ment providing that merning sittings should be held on Tuesdays as well as Fridays. It was defeated by 247 te 219, and eventually Sir W. Harcourt's motion was car- ried by 249 to 223.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on T iesday evening, th question of the imposition of fees o•I newly appointed magistrates was again referred to, and Mr. ASQUITH said he was about to issue a circular to clerks of the pesos which he hoped would lead ta a more satisfactory system._ .</p>
        <p>- Mr. MIINDELLI, replying to a question, said he hoped shortly to introduce a bill to confirm the Provisional Order respecting the classification of the goods and sche- dule of canal tolls. The Government proposed that the new code should come into operation on the Ist January, 1895.</p>
        <p>Mr. MIINDELLA informed an hon. member that the Board of Trade would consider the expediency of taking proceedings in regard to the sale of foreign meat as Brit-- tish if a suitable case were laid before them; but it would be much better for proceedings to be taken in the' locality by trade associations, Chambers of Commerce, or other bodies.</p>
        <p>The House went into Committeeof Supply on the Navy Estimates. Mr. FORWOOD complained that in the matter of shipbuilding, the policy of casting the burden on the future had been adopted throughout the Estimates. He said the mercantile navy as a nursery to the fleet was not improving, and demanded the serious attention of the Government. No doubt the fact of so many foreign- ers being employed in our mercantile PiaVy arose front the growing difficulty of finding experienced and sober Englishmen to man our ships. Mr. HAVELOCK WILSON said the reason why the foreigner was engaged was that he was cheaper. Commander BEntiara. commended the Admiralty for their appreciation of their importance of torpedo-catchers. Mr. BALFOUR said the House was really asked to deal with a programme amounting to an expenditure of thirty millions, and before the debate concluded large issues would have to lie brought before the House. Sir U. KAT-SHUTTLEWORTH repeated that the naval programme of the Government had beat adopted on the counsel of their naval advisers, and that it was emphatically the intention ef the Government to make the navy equal to the navies of any two other Powers. Sir E. A. BARTLETT having made a violent attack On the Government, Sir E. J. REED showed the humour of such exhibitions on ne part of gentlemen who had only just gone out of effice, and said the pre- sent scheme of the Government was as good as could be expected under the circumstances. Admiral FIELD said the Admiralty were justified hi ordering the 42 torpedo-destroyers without asking f‘r the permission of the House. Sir E. HARLAND found fault with the Gov- ernment for introducing the eight-hGur system in the naval establishments. Mr. GOSCHEN, while asking for some "general idea "as to the progress of expenditute on the navy, congratulated the Government on the unaminity with which their proposals had been received. The debate was adjourned at midnight. Before the House rose, the Select Conamittee on the Charity Com- mission was appointed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Wednesday Mr. KILBRIDE moved the second reading of the Land Tenure (Ireland) Bill. Be said it was designed to give effect to the inten- tions of Parliament when it passed the Land Act of 1881. and the subsuent measures dealing with the Irish land laws. It provided that twenty years' qitift enjoyment was not to be held by the court as compensation for improvements. and shortened the statotory term for the revision of judicial rents from fifteen to eight years. It also proposed to repeal the seventh section of the Act of 1887, known as "the evictions-made-easy clause," and that portion of the Act of 1871 which dealt with the purchaser's insurance fund. On a division there voted for the hill, 254 ; against, 165; majority, 89. The bill was then read a second time.</p>
        <p>Mr. HCZIEZ2 moved the second naling. of the Fruit Identification. Bill, which was, he said, desi,ned to pre- vent fraud and promote the interests of the producer and consumer of home-grown fruit. Mr. Mandella ridi- culed the provisions of the pleasure, describinz it as " Protection run mad." Sir W. Hart Dyke spoke in support of the bill, which on a division was rejnted by 210 to 110.</p>
        <p>The second reading of the Elementary Elucation (Exemption from School Attend-trice) Bill, promoted by the National Union of Elementary Teachers, was moved by Sir R. Temple, but as Mr. Acland could not allow the bill to pass wiilout discussion the motion was adjourned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>TIIURSDAY.</head>
        <p>TIIURSDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Cr)mtn.ms on Thurs lay evening Mr. Acuotb,in reply to questions,said that tho representation of private schooll on the Secondary E•lucAtion C-nnmis- sion by a member of the Council of the Girls' Public Day School Company and a member of the College of Precep. tors wa3 a 3 large as the Gwernment were able to give. Mr. ASQUITH told Mr. WOODS tilt it would be ink- posible for the Government to institute legislatioa on the subject of mining royalties during the present session.</p>
        <p>Sir W. HARCOURT, in reply to Sir G. 0. MORGAN, said be expected that the Welsh Disestabliirhment Bill would be introduced on an Early date. The right lion. gentleman informed Mr. MACLURE that the Government did not intend to bring a bill to carry out the rei,olirtion of the House on ttr suhj7.ct of a sepa- ate legislative body for Scotland. In a short statement as to the course of public busi- ness Sir WlLuau said the Registration Bill would be introduced on Friday. On Tuesday next the motion for the Scetch Grand Committee would be proceeded with, on Thursday the Evicted Tenants Bill would be taken, and Friday morning would he set apart for a discussion on Uganda. On this last-named question Sir William Harcourt read a statement to the effect that Her Majesty's Government had deter mined to establish a regular administration. and for that purpose to declare Uganda to be unchr a British protectorate. Mr. MUNDULLA introduced a bill to amend the Railway land Caual Traffic Act of 1833. He said it embodied the recommendations of the Select Committee appointed to consider railway rates and charges, and Mk-7re any loss had been sustained by a trader the Act was te be retro- spective. He suggested that the hill should be sent as soon as possible to the Grand Committee, to be considered along with a kindred hill which, he understood, was to be brought in. The Government bill was read a first , time.</p>
        <p>--The House went into Committee of Supply on the Navy estimates.</p>
      </div>
