      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY. APRIL 14, 1824.</p>
        <p>THERE is at the present time a good The Coal Trade. deal of disquietude with regard to the immediate future of the coal trade. The great stoppage of last year with its deplorable results is still too fresh in the public mind to allow of even the bare possibility of a re- newal of the dispute being contemplated with any- thing less than consternation. Yet, it is clear that the Conciliation Board which was established through the Rosebery agreement is not working without friction. LORD SHAND was appointed by the Speaker of the House of Commons to be ar- bitrator between the representatives of the coal- owners and the miners on the Conciliation Board, and the promptitude which his Lordship has shown in negativing the proposed fixing of what is known as a "living" or minimum wage in the coal trade has very seriously dis quieted the miners throughout the Federation districts. The" living wage" was the sheet anchor of all the miners hopes. Without some agreement on that point it is almost too much to expect anything like stabil- ity ix the relations between the coal-owners and their workmen. If there be not a written or a taeit understanding as to what shall constitute a minimum wage, upon what principle are the earn- ings of miners to be regulated ? It will no doubt be replied by those who are hostile to . the miners that wages will have to be regulated by the prices at which coal is sold. But an attempt to regulate wages in that way was the very thing that brought about the conflict last year, and a renewed attempt on the same lines would inevitably precipitate another and perhaps an even more disastrous conflict. And it must not be forgotten that the prices at which coals are sold are not controlled by the general body of coal-owners, and certainly not by ,those who have the strongest desire to pay the fairest wages, but by the irresponsible minority who indulge in rash and reckless speculation and faithless "undercutting." As a matter of fact the process has already begun. After the close of the recent dispute a number of Lancashire coal-owners com- bined for the purpose of discountenancing and, if possible, preventing the reckless competition which led to the stoppage ; but what has been the result? We are now told that although the bulk of the coal-owners joined this combination those who remained outside were sufficiently numerous to frustrate its objects. Prices have, consequently, fallen to a low level, as low, it is said, as they reached in the earlier part of last year, and there is a great deal of apprehension as to the figures at ; which the large gas-coal and locomotive fuel con- tracts will be taken when they are placed upon the market. It is clear, therefore, that, in the present state of affairs in the coal-trade, the miners will resist, and will be amply justified in resisting, to the very utmost any attempt to make wages fol- low prices. To them some kind of agreement as to a minimum wage is of vital importance. Whether the " minimum " should be the wages that are now paid, or five or ten per cent. more or less is net so material. Without the agreement neither the miners nor the public generally, who are only a little less concerned, can feel secure against a sudden reversion to the troubles of last year. It is hard to under- stand upon what grounde those coal-owners who deprecate reckless competition should hold out against a recognition of the minimum wage. Nothing better could be devised for secur- ing them against the undercutting they so much deplore. With the bulk of the masters and prac- tically all the miners combined together to main- tain fair wages and fair profits, a coal-owner who accepted contracts at ruinous rate would do so at his Own cost, and it is not likely that he would repeat the mistake. At the same time, it is surely a mistake on the part of a miners' delegate to denounce Lord SHAND in the uncompromising language used by Mr. Bailey of Nottingham. The Conciliation Board should at least have a fair trial, and there will be a vast arno unt of prejudice raised against the side which prevents it from having such a trial. It may be true that Lord SHAND has given his first decision against the miners, and it may also be true that in years past he has been bitterly oppos- ed to employers' liability, yet it is quite possible that before the Conciliation Board concludes its labours the workmen's representatives may find that his treatment of them and their cause has been just and fair.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <p>THERE can be no question that the An Aldermanie L., ' toeral members of the Warrington Election.</p>
        <p>Town Council took the proper course on Tuesday evening in simply making a written protest against the fashion in which the Tory majority now choose to select the aldermen. There was no satisfaction,and certainly no dignity to be derived from taking an active part in pro- ceedings which were to a large extent farcical. The Council was called together ostensibly for the purpose of electing an alderman to fill the position vacated by Mr. PLATT, yet in reality it met merely to formally endorse a decision come to by a Tory caucus meeting in the Conservative Club. In that way are the municipal affairs of Warrington now managed. The written protest of the Liberal members was necessary simply because they could protest in no other way. Under the rinling of the Town Clerk, which ha e been endorsed by the majority of the Council, there can he no discussion as to the relative marit of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>E MINER, SAri tilt 1) , A PhIL 14 1894</head>
        <p>THE E MINER, SAri tilt 1) , A PhIL 14 1894</p>
        <p>candidates for an aldermanic seat. That Iruling may be based on law or it may not, but that it places the Town Council. in an absurd and anomalous po3ition must be appAreat to everyone The Council may,for instance, discuss the appoint,. ment of a scavenger throughout a whole meeting and then adjourn if it liicce3 and discuss the Barrie subject at another meeting, but upon the election of an alderman, who is only second in dignity to the Mayor, there is not a Word to be said. As a matter of fact an election of this sort is not, and cannot be as a rule, made without discussion. If the discussion is not permitted in open Council it will take place at private meetings of parties and cliques, to the great disadvantage of the town. The election of an alderman should be openly de- bated in the Town Council—just as openly as less important matters are debated—especially in view of the fact that there is no actual prohibition to the contrary, and that the new rule has not always been observed in Warrington and is not, we believe, observed in many other boroughs. As to the election of Councillor HUTCHINSON to the vacant seat,the Conservatives have had tho matter entirely in their own hands and they deserve all the credit they can derive from the transaction. Nominally Mr. HUTCHINSON fills the place left by Alderman PLATT but in reality he does not. Before his partial retirement of the last year or two, Mr. PLATT was one of the most strenuous and influ- ential.members of the Council. He displayed high ability and great tenacity in his advocacy of pro- posals which were clearly for the benefit of the town, and he could always be relied upon to sub- ject any doubtful schemes to a keen and critical examination. Mr. HUTCHINSON has been in the Council several years, but we do him no injustice when we say that he has never shown any of the force or originality of his predecessor. Therefore, to the extent of the differences between the two men the Council loses by the change. If the Conservative majority had felt any desire to benefit the town rather than t 5 promote a mere partisan, they would have accepted the offer of the Liberals to fill the vacancy by the election of Mr. F. MONKS. There WAS never a period in its history when the Town Council of this borough needed strengthening rather than weakening more than it does now. There are very few pro- vincial towns so heavily rated, yet there are many which show much better results for the money that is spent in their local government. What is thought of the municipal management of the town by observant and well-informed "outsiders' may be to some extent gathered from an interest- ing interview with a local manufacturer which we publish this week. But to initiate and carry out such large reforms, financial or otherwise, as are there suggested the Council should be composed as far as possible of men of ability and clear- headedness. Such men will never be secured by the policy which is now in favour with the domi- nant party in the Council of pushing forward only the most docile and colourless of partisans.</p>
        <p>THE subject of the representation The Board of of Warrington on the Board of . Guardians. Guardians which Mr. A. J. , JOLLEY calls attention to in a letter in our corres- pondence columns is one of great importance to the town. The Parish Councils Act which has been passed by the present Government places,for the first time, the election of members of Boards of Guardians on a democratic basis, and enables the workingmen of Warrington to elect direct representatives if they so desire. That working- men think they ought to be represented is shown by the proceedings of the last two or three meet- ings of the Trades and Labour Council. Unfor- tunately, however, all the members for Warrington on the present Board are Tories, and, with the exception of Mr. JOLLEY himself, they seem to be quite willing to assent to a scheme which disfranchises Warrington to a very serious ex- tent. That the vote of an agricultural labourer in Cuerdley should be equal to the votes of twenty-eight ironworkers in Warn ngton is grotes- quely unfair, and Mr. JOLLEY does well to pre- test against any such proposal. It is, as he says, contrary to the spirit of all modern legislation,and certainly not in accordance with the purpose of Parish Councils Bill. Yet Mr. JOLLEY's protest was, we are told, "derided and voted down by the Warrington Guardians." It may, surely, be as- sumed that the Board will be cleared of represen- tatives of this kind at the election in November.</p>
