        <p>STUART ACCIDENT AT RUNCO!IY. A little boy, named Fred Le;. five years of age, son of Mr. David Lewis, grocer, of Widnes, sustained some- what severe internal icjurieg on Monday afternoon through being run over by an empty railway van in Greenway.read, Runurn. He was playing at the time, and fell between the wheels of the vehicle as it waa pro- ceeding up the read. Mcdioal advice was immediately procured and the child is now progressing very favour- ably. Josysrs PRITCHARD, Watchmaker aid Jewel/er, 51, 1110- st., Ituacern.—Advt. POLICR INSPRCTION AT_WIDNIS.</p>
        <p>Sir Herbert- 60-ft, Government Inspector, attended at Widnes on Tuesday afternoon to make the annual inspec- tion. There were present nn parade Saperidtendent Beighley, Inspector Strickland, six sergeants, and 31 constables and there were absent on duty nine ofbcpro and men. After the men had been put through a num- ber of drills the Inspector examined the books, offices, tee., and expressed hirnsalf pertectly satisfied, he not having a single complaint to make. PRITCHARD'S have now stood the test for fiftv yonrs, aid quality and first class workmanship are still guaranteed.— Mkt.</p>
        <p>THE ASSAULT ON A WIDNES WOMAN. At the Widnes Petty Sesshns on Thursday, before Mr. H. Gaskel!, jun., Elizabeth Bushell, of Edmund-street, was again brought up on remand charged with unlaw- fully wounding Margaret M'Cann, of Marg,aret-street,on the 2nd inst. As already reported,prisonor went into the house of the prosecutrix and assaulted her. She pulled the plaintiff to the floor by the hair of her head and dur- ing a struggle a large portion of her ear WaS bitten off. Dr. O'Keefe stated that a portion of prosecutrix'A ear was torn away. measuring about two inches in holgth and one inch in depth. She would be mutilated for life. Tho prisoner was committed for tiial at the nex.t Licr- pool Assizes. EXTENSIVE TNEFT OF WIRE-NETTING AT RUNCORN%</p>
        <p>Yesterday (Friday) morning at the Runcorn Po!i CQurt bei9re Mn.'. F. peakman and F. J. Norman, Thomag Rhaney wag cliarged with stealing 111 yards of wire-netting, value 9s. 61., the pr4perty of Messrs. J. and E. Percival, farmers, of Agon, near Runcorn. The theft was alleged to have been committed on or about the 26th Feb. From the evidence of the various wit- nesses the defendant had sold two lots of wire-no ;tin similar to that which was missed by the proreentrirs. He subsequently asked the purchasers to tell thepolice that they had the wire-netting naore than six months should they make any inquiries respecting it.—The defeutiant pleaded not guilty, and made a long rambling state- ment which the Magistrates were not disposed to be- lieve, and accordingly fined hiT £1 and costs or four- teen days' hard labour. ENTERTAINMENT AT GREENWAY BOARD SCMCOLS, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening the spring session of the Green- way-road Continuation School was brought to a close by an enjoyable entertnintnent consisting of limelight views illustrating a trip " Round the world in a hun- dred minutes," portraits of distinguished public men, and several comic pictures. The results of various corn- paitions were also announced. i'or neatneys in geo- graphy Wiliiam Molyneux, W. Gleave, and F. E. Ward gained prizes ; whilst Thomas Ctrs secured another for good spelling. At the conclusion of the proceedings Mr. J. F. Moulton, head master of the schools, delivered an a.ddsess, in the course of whizth he referred to the sue- cessf ul nature of the session which was then terminating and urged the scholart to continue their studies with renewed zest for the name of the school. The descrip- tive readings were given by Mr. Moulton and Mr. Epp, and the lantern was manipulated by Mr. T. Bowyer. iiSUPH PRITCHITM, 51, Iligh-street, .Thmeorq, keeps lirmer.soLrus to suit all sights, from a shiaing a pair.—Adat. INTERESTING PRR3ENTATION AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>An event possessing unusual interest took place on Monday evening, at Mr. Riley's Cate, Bridge-,treet, Runcorn, in the form of a presentei,in to Mr. U. H. Broady, a late member of the Runcorn Footb,di Club and also of the Y.M.C.A_. Club. Mr.P. Sp,altina,., J. P.;C.C., president of the Runcorn Football C!uh, eccupied tn. chair.—Mr. Charles Pownall said that Mr. Brhady had been connected with the club since its forinition in 1878 and had b,-,en instrumental in bringing it up to its present standard.. He had done active and honourable service for the club and richly merited the present they were about to make him.—The Chairman in presenting the clock to Mr. Broady, expressed his pleasure at being chosen to perform the ceremony. (Applause.) The testimonial had been willingly subscribed for bv his follow club mates and the townspeople generdly ; and he was sure that it would prove an article ot furniture as well as affording much pleasure to the recipient. (Applause.)—ln accepting the present, Mr. Broady thanlied the members and the subscribers heartily for their generosity towards him. It had always ben a pleasure to him to play for the clubs, and to assign LI gaining their victories.—Sobsequen ly votes of thanks were accorded the subscribers and the Chairman for presiding. Oo to PEITCHARD's, 51. High-st.. Runcorn, for the !best ! FIVE G uhs EA WATCH a working man can hay.!. RECITING COMPETITION AT RUNCOIRN.</p>
        <p>An interesting prize competition in connection with the Runcorn Women's Temperance Association took place on Monday evening in the new schoolroom,Green- way-road. There was a large audience and the chair was filled by Mr. A. R. Norman, whilst Messrs. F. J. Norman, J.P., Handley, and E. Marshall acted as adjudica- tors. The Chairman in his opening remarks observed that for the purpose of creating an interest in temper- ance principles, this competition had been organised. The prize for the best reciter was a silver medal, and the winner could afterwards compete in a subsequent corn- pAition for a gold medal. After that there would be a contrst for a grand gold medal, and lastly one for a gold medal set with diamonds. (Applause.)—The following is the list of the competitors : Misses Lillie Bazler, Lillie Houghton, Nellie McDonald, Edith Moores,Newall. Annie Rogers, Agnes Davies ; Messrs. Torn Fitton and Fred Stubbs. In the course of the competition various vocal items were rendered, and much enjoyed, the accompanist being Mr. Wm. Cooke. The medal was won by Miss Annie Rogers, and was presented to her by the Chairman. The judges were of the opinion that. all the pieces had been well recited, but it was their unanimous decision that Miss Rogers deserved the medal. The singing of the Doxology terminated the proceedings. BRUNSWICK MUTUAL INPROVEMEMT CLASS, RUNCORN. The spring session of the shove class was brought to a happy termination on Wednes day evening by a tea meeting in the schoolroom' Brunswick-st; An excellent tea was served to about 55 members,the ladies in charge of the necessary arrangements being Misses Anderson, Crosber, J. Crosby, Taylor, Rushton, Littler, C. Littler, and others. Subsequently the tables were cleared and the evening's entertainment began. Previous to com- mencing, however, Mr. T. Sisnister, president of the class, delivered an interesting address, in the course of which he touched upon the present flourishing condition of the society, and the success with which they had been attended during the session. He ,was glad to see it pro- gressing so rapidly, and he trusted that their next ses- sion Would show even better results. (...ltr,4hinse•) At a subsequent stage of the proceedings Mr. J. D. Jones, vice-president., addressed the meeting, his remarks also having relation to the Success which t!“, class had at- tained during the spring session. Sen gs wore sung by Miss A. M. Taylor, and Mr. John Leathworyl ; and two humorous readings were eiren by Miss A. Littler, and Mr. J. L. Rain. It was e;iiied ''hat I h..cit ss should ho formed as previously into a rumbling climb for the sow- maser months, Mr. J. H. Pickthall, being re-appointed secretary. Varieus games wero afterrr.trd rtrilll:f.)d in and the meeting broke up after a most onpy able even- nr; had been spent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>ACCIDENT AT NVIDNIS.</head>
        <p>ACCIDENT AT NVIDNIS.</p>
        <p>A man named WiUiim Walker, of Bridge-st.,sustained a fracture of the right leg whilst working at Messrs. 13.7,1t0n and Swts Copper Works, on Monday morning. Ho was immediately conveyed to the Accident hospital and attended by Dr. Hutchinson. NEW Poon "UTE FOR WIDNES.</p>
        <p>On Thursday at the Widnes Petty Seasions, Mr. J. Goff, a.isistant ovfrsper, appiied for the levying of a new poor rate ot 3s. 81. in the £. This was granted. It may be meatioiv,d that last year's rate waAi 3s. The new rate is estimated to prolue,e £20,285 14:3. Bd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>ACCIDENT AT RUNCORN DOCKS.</head>
        <p>ACCIDENT AT RUNCORN DOCKS.</p>
        <p>On Monday attcrnoon mau named Joseph Sidley, living in English-row. Runcorn, and employed at the Eunc.irn Docks, met with a somewhat serious accident. VO•iiliit engaged in uncoupling some waggons which were lteing shunted by an engine he was crushed between the buffers. Ile received medical attention and is last re- covering from the internal injuries he sustained. ALBERT-ROAD WESLEYAN BAND OF HOPE, WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A very succassful optical entertaimnent, in aid of the above, was given on Tuesday evening. There was a good stendance, presided over by Mr. J. H. Lee. Miss Silcox read in an extremely in'eresting 'manner extracts from " The life and time f John Vtresh,y," and this was made more enjoyable by excellent lantern views at intervals. Altogether a very pleasant evening was speot.</p>
        <p>THREATENING TO TAKE LIFE AT WIDNES. At the Widnes Petty Sessi,.ns en Thursday before Mr. Holbrocl jon., and Lieutenant Col. Blnckburn, Richard Toiakies, of Wright-st., was charged with threatening to take the life of Mr. James Davies, Estate Agent, Witt-road. Evidence proving the plaintiff's statement having been given, the defendant was bound over to key the peace for six months, or in default one month's imprisonment.</p>
        <p>WIDNES FIRS nBIGADII.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Carr, Superintendant of this brigade has issued lii s annual report. The t)tal number of fires which on- rimed during the year ending 25'11 March was 9 in the borough and 1 outside, only 4 of which were Ferioug.The brigade had ten calls, and oa five occasions they extin- guished the fires. The estimated total loss from fires vrts £323, and the estimated value of the property at risk was £15,740. The total strength of the brigade is 14 and niue of ti.ie firomen reside iu the fire station cot- tages. Mr. Carr suggests that the Council should take into consideration the advisability of providing the brigade with a steam fire engine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>EXTENSION OF TYE?icons GAS ;YORKS.</head>
        <p>EXTENSION OF TYE?icons GAS ;YORKS.</p>
        <p>The work of eftcting a new building on the banks of the Bridgewater Canal, Runcorn, for the supply of gas to the town has Tl*Vir been completed and the production of ga3 was commenced on zsaturday. The erection con- sists of a retort house 701 t. by 120, containing 96 retorts. each 20ft. long, capable of producing 700,000 cubic feet of gas per day, provision being made for the production of double that quantity when required. The buildings and appliances for the purification of gas are all on the latest principles. The telescopic gas holder is 1051 t. in diameter by 60;it. deep, and has a capacity of 500,000 cubic feet. The older One, v inch is capable ef holding 70,000 cubic feet, can also b--t</p>
        <p>WIDNES DISTRESS RELIEF FUND.</p>
        <p>The final meeting of this Committee was held on Wed- nesday at the Town Had. Mr. IL Wade Deacon, J.P., C.C., preMded, end there was a good attendance. A tialince of £9l 153. ad. was shown to have been left. This was not es large a balance as had been antici.i.' ated, lint the relief had been carried on longer than was expected. This balance was ordered to remain in the bank for use in any further emergency, if such should arise. A resolution was pr mosed, which was ordered te be engrossed and framed and sent to the Tyneside employits of the United Alkali Co., Ltd., for the generous manner in which they bad helped their fellow workmen in Widnes during the distress, they having subscribed about £162 to !he fund. A vote of thanks was prop'ised to Mr. Wade Deacon for presiding and in replying he said he hoped it would be a long time before it would be necessary for that committee to meet again.</p>
        <p>WtDXB3 PgrlY SESAIONS. TtauustAY.—PefOtoMrl Rol.brook Gasket!, jun.,</p>
        <p>filackbtitn.</p>
        <p>DRUNICIINNIISS.—The followine rpreong were fin.(l for zhis offence :—John Cronan, Mary-street, and 'William Bushe,•ll, 8r,71.(1 Ile4V-1 Cottages.</p>
        <p>BREACH:7.S OF TEN PEACE.—For this offenco Frank Gold- ing and MicLa,i Winnion, of Elinbeth-street, and T;tuotlly Mitchel!, QI Walinsley-street, was bound over to keep the peace for three months. • UNLICHNSIZD DOG. --JallS Paireell, of Timperley-st., Nva.9. tined 7s. 63. and cts for keeping a dog Wit:llKit license.</p>
        <p>CHIMNEY FIRM.—For having allowed the chimney of his house to be on fire, William Doi)ly, of Wahnsley-st., WA, fined Is. and costs. Fentous 13:CYCLE RIDIVG.—Frank Barlow Derb -road, Farnworth, was summoned for haviw. at Farnworth,on the lct inst., ridden his bicycle on the highway at s furious rate. Defendant was fled 2. 63. and costs.</p>
        <p>TIMSPASSING ON THE RAILWAY.—Henry Cunliffe, Jame Unworn and John Bromley ,all of Bold, were summoned for having trespasPA on the railway at Bold. Mr. Fenaa prosecuted on beirilf of the L. and N.-W. Rsilway Co., and defendants were each fined ss. and -costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>THINGS HEAP.° AND sp:EN AT</head>
        <p>THINGS HEAP.° AND sp:EN AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Br AN OCCASIONAL CORIZESPONATCNT.</p>
        <p>Once again the Wesleyan body of Runcorn has been visited by one of their favourite pre.achers, the Rev. Peter Mack2nzie, and once again have they shown their appreciation of his powers by assembl- ing in large numbers at the St. Paul's Chapel, and the Publio Hull. At the former place he delivered an aokuirable discourse, full, as his sermons gener- ally are, of sound practical advice of the spiritual Welfare of his listeners; whilst at the latter he lec- tured on " Prince .Absalom." If the criticisms of the 4entlemen seated upon the platform are to be taken into account then the subject was brilliantly dealt with. It is to be hoped that are long we shall again have the pleAsuro of the Rev. Peter Mackenzie'presenze amongst us.</p>
        <p>The presentation recently made by the runcarn Football Club to one of their late members, Mr. AV. H. Broady, shows how highly that combination .estimated his services during his connection with them. Mr. Broady's football career in Runcorn is certainly an honourable one, and it must be ex- tremely gratifying to him to know that his endea- vours to place the club on a firm basis have been duly appreciated tiot only by his fellow club-mates, but by the townspeople generally.</p>
        <p>An object deserving of public sympathy and sap- pert is the present endeavour of Dr. Gast*!di to free his schools from debt. The managers of the St. Edward's schools have been put to considerable expense in having to carry out important alterations required by the Education Department. In order to assist in clearing off the debt thus incurred, the St. Edward's Amateur Dramatic Society gave two very creditable performances en Wednesday and Thurs- day evenings, of the Irish drama "Pike O'Callag- han," in the Public hail. There were fairly large audiences and it is expected that the venture will, to some extent, help in freeing the schools from the debt.</p>
        <p>The oft mooted question of the provision of public baths at Runcorn is assuming every day a more important attitude. The argument advanced against it are very weak when the advantages which such a provision would bring are taken into serious consideration. Everyone will readily admit how essential swimming is to the preservation of human life,and it is urged in favetur of the proposal that not only would baths afford facilities of the acquisition of this art, but would also materially decrease the disease rate which was 41. in the .E last year at Runcorn, A large number of local medical gentlemen have pronounced in favour of such a provision in view of ale benefits it would confer upon the town, and Mr. J. T. Brunner, ALP. for the divisinn,has likewise expressed his approval by letter to bl r. Collier of the scheme. A meeting of ratepayers is to be held in the Public Hall on Wed- nesday next for the purpose of taking the matter into consideration and should a resolution in favour of the proposal be passed it is expected to be dealt with by the Improvement Commissioners at their next meeting.</p>
        <p>The important ceremony of laving the founda- tion stone of the new Technical Institute at Run- corn will be performed to-day (Saturday) by Mr. J. T. Brunner, M.P. for the division. Mr. Brunner is expected to arrive at Runcorn by the 133 p.m. train from London. .He will be received at the rail- way station by varibus gentlemen forming a depu- tation from the Board of Commissioners' and will thence proceed to the Town Hall, where he will be met, by the remainder of the Commissioners, .them- hors of the School Board, and other public gentle- men, as well as representitives of the different trades in tl!e town. The time fixed for the cero- molly is 2.30 p.m., and the proceedings are antici- pated to be of a most enthusiaqic character. Should we be favoured with fine weather there is at pre- sent no reason to doubt that such will be the case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <p>THE NEW POOR RATE, CLIFTON.</p>
        <p>Messrs. C.-iteynolds, J.P., and G. L. Davies, LP.. have affixed their signatures to the new poor rate for the township a mmAnting to is. in the £ which is esti- mated to realiv&gt; £205 13,3. 31.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN'S NEW POOR RATE. The °veneers of Runcorn have levied a poor rate of 10d. in the £ returnable by the 29th Sept. next, which has been dui), sanctioned by Messrs. John Brundrit,J.P., and J. L. Lewis, J.P. The township's rateable value is £60.875 14s. 10d., and the rate is estimated to produce £2,536 94. 9:1-A. The rate is the same as that levied in the corresponding period of last year.</p>
        <p>If you want your WITCH SAPAIRED •by skilled workmen take it to PRITCHARD'S. • Advt.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BURIAL BOARD,</p>
        <p>Mr. W. F. Salkeld, secretary to the above Board, in his annual statement, submitted on Wednesday after- noon last, reports that the number of interments for the year ending March 25th,1894. was 503 against 467 in the corresponding period of last year, or an increase of 36. The fnps received during the past year amounted to £467 17s. 61. as against £456 19s. 10 last year, showing an incrrase of £lO 17s. 10d.—A cheque for interest amounting to £23 64. was drawn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>WARRINGTON MUNICIPAL FINANCE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUNICIPAL FINANCE.</p>
        <p>SOME CRITICISMS AND SUGUESTIONS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>INTERESTING INTERVIEW.</head>
        <p>INTERESTING INTERVIEW.</p>
        <p>The other day an " Examiner " reporter had a conversation upon the financial condition of the . Corporation, with a large ratepayer of the town, who, besides being a well-known financier, has had considerable experience in the municipal affairs of large towns. The subject of providing a comdrehen- sire scneme for the improvement of the town, cropped up for consideration. " What is your idea concerning this matter?" asked the reporter. " Well was ,the reply, " first. of 'all I consider that what Warrington wants is a cable road running from Sankey to tile Cemetery and from Longford to Latchford, and there should he introduced the sys- tem of correspondence tickets so that you can get from one quarter of the town to the other with speed and comfort." " What is the correspondence system." "Simply the issuing of a ticket,say at one penny, which will enable the passenger to travel along the wnole length, or change at the intercepting routes." "You would propose that th;s work should be done by the Corporation ?" "Yes, decidedly. It is a fact that the iron trade of Warrington is by no means in so flourishing a condition as it might be. There are to be seen iron- workers idling about in all directions, with their hands in their pockets, unable to find work. The construction of a cable road would .find immediate work for most of these men by creating a necessity for the large quantity of iron required in the streets for the lines: and girders, and other work. There would be required large quantities of vvire-rops cable as well as machinery at the central power , station."</p>
        <p>"But the streets have already been objected to by the 1,-)cal Government Board as too narrow for trams."</p>
        <p>"Just so. My contention is that the streets should be widened and the present buildings, where neces- sary, pulled down. and larger buildings erected,fOur or five stories high. Suoli buildings on account of their height and modern facilities in the way of elevator, ire proof filors, and better sanitary arrangemeirs would conimanu nigher rentals, and naturally increase the assessable value of the town."</p>
        <p>"The question asked will be where is the money for this to come from r'</p>
        <p>" I think no difficulty would arise in that direction which cannot be overcome. The , Government have quite recently urged upon local authorities the duty and obligation of establishing works of public utility awl public benefit for the purpose of dealing with the great problem of the unemployed. For this puroose there are being offered at the present momentextraordinary facili- ties to local auteorities for the purpose of borrow- ing money at a low rate of interest. Now it has been urged that the people of Warrington are already burdened in their rates, and that to launch out in what is regarded by some people as reckless expenditure will be nothing less than a suicidal policy." " But there are such people.' "To those who hold this opinion it would be pei haps well to point out one or two facts con- tained in the Borough Treasurer's report for tie year ending March, 1893. New, from that report it appears th.tt the Corporation of WarrLdgton are paying interest en loam at the rate tir 4 per cent., whilst at the same time they are inved ng money at 2/ per cent. Well nr3wl th public want to know is there tuly Pq!”9 in that. 1' But to proceed. The as- • semi&amp; VA:, of I,A.raringt,on is. £150,000, which re- pre9ent,l £3,000,000 of property. 1.,10w in the first place there can be no doubt that thie ile:elS,aFle value is dscidedly too small. It is a kli3wn fact that the per capita wealth of 60,000 inhabitanta should anemia to no lees than £6,009,000. Toe borrowing powers on the assessable v elite. of £150,000 at ss. in the X (we are paying 6. 41.!) is X 37.500. Now if the Borough debt was conso.li- dated, a million pounds could be borrowed at 2i per cent., and only entail en interest of £27,500. Tile Borough debt is £568,656. Of this amount, on the score of water alone, £277,713 has been incurred and for tills amount £9,176 has been paid in in- terest last year which is equz,l to 3 and per cent. The works are capitalised at about 30 years' purchase. They will last for generations and the repayment of the capital is arranged for 69 years. This period is too short." " Can it legally be extended ?" " Yes. In 1881 the Bradford Corporation under the Ipowere of their Water and Improvement Act borrow- ed for the purchase of their water-works £400,000 the term of repayment being 108 veare. In addition to this Bradford borrowed £172,.000 in 1882 at the same term of repayment for LlitOr gas uedertaking, whilst cur term for repaying the loan on a eimiltar undertaking is ab.mt 50 years. From that, one is unable to believe that our Corporation LI very wide awake. Tee Public Health A.ct, a copy of which I have at hand, provides in section 233 " that any urban or rural sanitary authority may, with the eanctioo of the Leal Government Board, for the purposes of defraying any costs, charges, and ex- penises incurred, or to be incurred, in the execution of the Sanitary Acts, or this Act, or for the purpose of discharging any loans contracted under the sani- tary Acts or this Act, borrow or re-borrow and take up at interest any 1811 Ms of money necessary fur defraying soy such costs, charges, and expenses or for defraying any such loans as aforesaid."</p>
        <p>"Yes "Having obtained the necessary money to defray the Parliamentary costs of obtaining the Local Act, you now then proceed to get the time which is desired and is deemed necessary, and in accordance with the best private Act Legislation, such as in the case of Bradford, (108 years.) At present it con hardly be argued that the terms fixed by the General Law are consistent in this respect, for while as many as GO years for the repayment of loans are allowed in 6011210 instances, only 30 or even less are allowed in others for precisely the same purpose." "But to revert to the subject of consolidation?" "I contend that if the Warrington debt was con- solidated, powers could be taken to borrow addi- tional money for the purpose of making the cable road and carrying out the other improvements mentioned at the, outset. This .would eventually mean an additional source of income on account of the revenue from. the Cable roads as well as the in- creased assessment from the 'modern property erected in place of the low storied old fashioned buildings that have been in existence in the centre of the town for the last 300 years. I contend also that, under proper management, the cost of these public works need not entail a single penny piece of increased taxation to the inhabitants of the borough. Indeed it would lead in the near future to a reduction of the rates, and would give an impetus to the trade and commerce, and the prosperity of the town to an extent almost beyond computation." ! " How cou:d this be done ?"</p>
        <p>"The -borough debt is ~C 568,656 the interest for which at 3:i per cent. involves the Corpora- tion in a liability of £19,000. Jr the debt were consolidated money could be borrowed at 21 per cent. and the Corporation, upon the same liability could borrow an additional £122,000. This would more than fitly for the establishment of the cable mid. But with the consoli laticn of the debt a *inking fund may be established whereby the money ,orrowed for permanent works could be repaid at 108 years instea4 of in the short periods at present in force. And thus on ne score of the repayment of capital the burden now borne by the rateayers of warrington would be farther relieved. p By ebtisolidating the Warrington debt money could be obtained at a lower rate of interest, saving the inhabitants or ratepayers over £3,000 annually in interest, equal to 51, in the pound on the rates, the repayment of the principal also being extended for twice the permd,equivalent to an additional 7d. in the pound on the riti es. Why should the people of Warrington pay for a sewer in 20 years that will last 100 years or more? Why should a principle be adopted in our Local Government that not found to worc. advantageously in our great commercial enterprises,—say our great railway or shipping comnimies ?</p>
        <p>"Have you any cases in point ?:' " Yes. Take the London and North Western Railway Company, or the Canard .Company. If they were to attempt. to repay their capital in 50 years they could pay nothing like the interest they do at the present time. Yet the Warrington people are being- made to repay in 50 or 60 years, both in- terest and capital on property absolutely freehold and that will benefit generations to come, a hundred years-hence?'</p>
        <p>"Would there be any difficulty in obtaining money at the love rate of interest named, for a term of 100 years?"</p>
        <p>"No. There w)uld be no difficulty whatever. The very people whs4 will have their money returned to them will re-invest in new stock,und the munici- pal stock will be quite as good as Government "Do you think the sp,tem of cable roads would pay _ _</p>
        <p>Decidedly. They psy in all citie, in America. Wheeling, in South Virginia is a town of only 30,000 intutbitants, and the cable roads pay there. rhe idea is that the lines should be laid ht.forti the town grow any larger, and have the net work ready for extending. One of the advantages which Would accrue would he the connecting of ths two railway station,,., and this is very desirable."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>THIN GS II EAR') AND SEEN</head>
        <p>THIN GS II EAR') AND SEEN</p>
        <p>AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The dispute at Puekiey-street Chapel between committee of the chap,l and the minister, the Rev. W. L. AlcNair has caused considerable, interest in the town. It is not a common occurrence for a minister to announce his resignation from the pas- torate, at a public service, and for a prominent member of the congregation to get up and say that the minister':; resignat:ou was accepted " without regret." These disputes in places of worship are not at all calculate 6 to raise the said places in the estimation of outsiders. It would be far better and far more seemly for the parties to settle their dis- putes between themselves, and not to make them the talk of the town.</p>
        <p>The Wesleyans of Latchford made a step forward on Thurday afternoon, when the foundation etones of a new chapel were laid. That such a place has been required for a considerable p,±riod is shown by the fact that though there has been a Methodist Society in Latchford for forty years, there never has been a Wesleyan Coupe!, the meetings having to be held in a Temperance Hall. Latchford for a long period was a kind of Church preserve, and the first persons to break away from the church were the Good Templars,and then Methodism was established in the village. Mr. L. Ellison has been identified with the cause for the greater portion of this period and be bolds the post of superintendent of the Sunday school at the present time. The site of the new building conic] not very well be improved because it stands at the meeting of four roads, and there is a growing population about that part of Latchford. The ceremony and evening service were very successful financially, considerably over £lOO being realized.</p>
        <p>Amateur photographers in Warrington increase in numbers each year, and the exhibition given under the auspices of the Warrington Amateur Photographic Society on Tuesday evening was a decided improvement on previous exhibitions. About 200 views were shown, all of them having been taken by the members. There was no lack of variety in the views, and it must he said that with the music which was interspersed, a vely pleasant evening's entertainment was provided. The holidays seem to offer the photographers the best oppor- tunity for bringing into use their cameras, and the views shown on Tuesday evening proved that this is done to advantage. • The photographer not only has the pleasure of viewing enchanting scenery,but is able to bring home with him he picturesque in.. Miniature to gaze upon at his own fireside.</p>
        <p>There seems to be some doubt as to whether the Queen will visit Warrington or not when she for- mally opens the Ship Canal next month. The account publiehed in the early part of the week stated very specifically that Her Majesty would break her journey North at Warrington, embark on the Canal at Latchford and return. vain to Latch- ford after sailing, along the Canal t 9. B.rtoll, Q€ mrr; iY Tratie this dietrict the pivot of t I)6Whole pr()SeA ing:, leaving 3.rauchester pretty much out in the cold. NaturrOly ell Manchester people who talk about the Canal .n.l manage it wt. if it were their "very own," wete g ievously appointed by the royal programme, and there were even some insinuations that lier Majesty has long cherished a dislike for Cottononolis. In a later ver- sion we are toll, however, that the Queen (I.,)es not intend to come to Warrington but will embark at Maui and continue journey to Balinoral from that point when the ceremony is over. There seems to be only one thing clear, and that is, that the final arrangements for the royal visit have not yet been definitely made.</p>
        <p>MERLIN.</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED BATEr., T-611. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>MR. J. T. BRUNNER, ',FSVOURIBLE TO</p>
        <p>THE SCHEME.</p>
        <p>Mr. Walter Collier is still working indefatigably for his scheme, namely, the provisien of public baths for Runcorn. Haring corresponded with most of the local medical practitioners upon the subject, and received extremely satisfactory re- plies, he wrote to our well-known Parliamentary representative for his support. In response to the appeal, Mr. Collier has received the following letter, which has been penned by Mr. J. T. Brunner him- self :</p>
        <p>"9. Eanismora Gardens. London, S.W., April 10th, 1894.—Dear Krpatly regret that my time will be too fully occupied on Saturday next ta permit of my attend- hg a meeting of raterayersto■upport tha proposal for public baths at Runcorn. Tha proposal, however, has my warmest approval, and I will do all that lies in my piwer to furthesr it. Yours flithfully, J. T. BRUNNER. —Mr. Waltr Collier."</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Taylor, IP., Chairman of the Commissioners, has eonysne;l nseetinz of ratepayers t 4 discuss the sub- ject for Wednesthy next, in the Public Hall, Runcorn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>lIALTON.</head>
        <p>lIALTON.</p>
        <p>FINANCIAL POSITION or THE FEMALE FRIENDLY SOCIETY —According to the statement of accounts of this Society just published for the year ending Dec. 31st,the amount of sick pay allowed to members was £9l 16.; funeral money £7O, and surgeon's are-qints £36. The amount of subscriptions was £l6l 3.6 d. The aggregate amount of the society's funds is £1,4-38 16s. 11. d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>WESTON.</head>
        <p>WESTON.</p>
        <p>TEE Tow:carries Ncw Poon 11.1TR,—A TIPW poor rate of 1.3. IA. in the £ lia9 been pined by rtieqrs. Jamei Handley J.P., and S. B. Chadwick, J.P. This rate is expected to realize £674 ss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>FRODSHAIL</head>
        <p>FRODSHAIL</p>
        <p>ENTERTAINMENT IN TEE NATIONAL SCE3OLS.—A most successful entertainment was on Monday evening given in the National Schouls by members connected with Mr. Teare's evening class. There was a large attendance, and the programme, which included songs, pianoforte solos, readings, and recitations, was heartily enjoyed. FUNERAL OF D. ItOEINON.—On Saturday afternoon amid many manifestations of grief and respect the re- mains of the late Dr. Robinson, of High Lea, Overton, Frodsham, were laid in their last restina place in the burial ground of Overton Church. Shertiv after two o'clock the mournful procession, including- representa- tives from most of the friendly societies in the district. as well as prominent inetnbers of the medical and cleri- cal prefessions, let the late residence of the deceased and jaurneyed towar...A the church at a slow pace. On arriving at the church gates the cortege was met by the Hew. H. B. Blogg, W. D. Dickenson, and S. C. Rarmhr, accompanied by a surpliced choir. The Service inside the sacred edifice was very affeqing, and was conducted by the above mentioned clergy in t'-a,preeence of a iaree congregation.. - The coffin was of-nack, surmounted by a brass shield, upon which was inscribed :—" John Robinson. born October 11th, 133; diial April 3rd, 1894." Numerous floral tribtites were laid upon it, each hearing expressions of sympathy and reeepeet. On Sun- day evening the Rev. H. 8. Blogg, vicar of the Parish ! Church, Frodsham, made a feeling reference, during his ' sermon, to the death of Dr. Robinson. whom he described as "an upright and honest man." AIICIIDEACONAL VISITATION. —The Venerable. Arch- deacon Barber made his annual visitation to the Parish Church, Frolaha tn. on Tuesday afternoon, for the pur- pose of admitting the churchwardens and sidosmen in the Rural Deanery. A moderate number Of clergy were present, in alclition to the officials elected at tbe last vestry meeting. A shot service havirr been reanduc.ttd by the Vicar, the Rev. H. B. Illogg, an address was de- livered by the Von. Arclideaeon. in the course of which he exhorted the wardens and sidemen tnmaintain intact VIP various institutions of whiea thy had the charge. He also appealed to managers ef voluntary scl,ools to do the same. especially in view of the increased require- ments of the Educational D.partment. The eereniony of admitting the wardens and sidemen was then per- formed t the conclusion of which tea was partaken of :a the seeonlroom. At a meeting snnsequently held, the Vs a. Archdeacon 11.erher presiding, a resolution w o od against the poliev of disestablishing and dis-a)- dowing the church in Wales.</p>
        <p>Mr. W R.3thbonft, member for the Arfon division Ot Carna.rvonshire, nas deoicted not co sees re-alection.</p>
      </div>
