      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>DOG FIGHTING.—EXEMPLART PITNISRMENT. Joseph Hadland, 25, Gas-street, was charged with ill- treating a dog, on March 24th, by inciting it to fight. P.C. Clarke said that he was in Mersey-street in the afternoon of March 24th, and saw the defendant in com- pany with another young man with dogs in their pos- session. He commenced to incite his dog to light the other,and at length threw it upon the other dog. A fight ensued between the two. dogs, and the two had to be almost choked to be separated. The de- fendant had been warned at Christmas for the same thing.—Mrs. Forster corroborated the officers' Statement. Two witnesses were called by the defendant to state that the dogs commenced fighting themselves, and that defendant separated them himself.—The maeistrates however considered the case proved, and tined defendant 40s. including costs. • A SMART WARRINGTON YOUTH: A MOTIGIR'S PAP:FUL SURPRISII. A youth named Stanley Findon was charged at the Warrington Borough Court, n Saturday. with stealing from the shop of Joseph Partington, 43, Bridge-street, on Friday morning, a cheque of the Nominal value of £7, the property of Joseph Partineton. The Chief Constable said that the boy lived in Gorsey-lane, and was the errand boy of Mr. Partington. Mr. Partington was a tailor and had done some business with Mr. Arthur B. Russell Davies, formerly of Grappenhall, ter which that gentleman sent a letter on Thursday with a cl-eque of £7. On Friday morning-, the prisoner went to Mr. Shaw's shop, opposite to Mr. Partington's, with the cheque and said. that he wanted to have it cashed for Mrs. Partington. Mr. Shaw had cashed several notes before, and althouv,h he saw that the cheque was not endorsed, he thought it would be all right, and handed the boy the £7. A few minutes later, the boy came in very quickly and said that he wanted to take the cheque across to ne endorsed by Mrs. Partingtan. Mr. Shaw, however, refused to give it hinnand later in the evening he sent the cheque across to Mrs. Partington to be endorsed. She, however, said that she knew nothing about it, and Mr. Partington on coming home also' said he knew nothing about it, although there was an account due to him from Mr. Russell Davies but he had never re- ceived the cheque from him. The boy came to his work that (Saturday) morning, and cn being questioned, at first denied knowing anything about it,but on Inspector Spinks coming into the shop, he then said that the £7 was in a room at the back of the premises, where it was found. The boy was then taken to the police-stat'on, and on being charged with the theft, he said that he had found the cheque in a letter which was on the coun- ter along with some more, and he opened it and tore up the letter.—Mr. Partington and Mr. Shaw having given evidence, the magistrates were shoat to give their decision, when the boy's mother came into the court apparently ignorant of the charge against her son. When informed a painful scene ensued. She becitne hysterical and at length fainted and had to be carried out of the room. Mr. Burgess (presiding magistrate) said that the magistrates had decided to order the boy to receive twelve strokes of the birch rod. They hoped that it would be a lessen to hiaa in future not to de such thing again.</p>
        <p>SUNDAY NIGHT PESTS. • George Bebbington, 8 Marson-street, Wm. Woodward , 14 Catherine-street, and John Wilkinson, West-street, ' three youths, were charged with disorderly conduct in ' Sankey-street, on Sunday night. P.C. Bell- in proving the case said that the thtee defendants were in S•-•.nkey- ' •, street on Sunday night, in company with other boys. g They were behaving very disorderly and causing reat annoyance to passengers in the street. Witness cau- e,e boned them. but soon afterwards he saw the three defendants going along the street again and knorking against almost. everybody they met. They knocked a • girl Gown, and a gentleman was almost knocked to the floor. He hroug,ht Bebbineton to the police station, and than got the names and addresses, and reported them. Mr. Monks in giving the decision of the magistrates said that they bad decided to hind the defendants over for six menths, and they would have to pay the costs 55. The magistrates also hoped that in future that should this conduct he repeated, the person subjected to this annoyance would take out a summons against the . youths who were tnisbellaying themselves, and an exam- ple would be made of thein. CAUGHT IN TILE ACT. John Smith, 20, Hill-street, was chareed at the War- ' j'rington Berough Court., on Menday. with felonieuslv breaking into the shop of Edward Green, 23, Butter- market-st., between 11.30 on Saturday night and 12.30 on Sunday morning, and stealing therefrom a fettle of pickles, a tin ef paint, a ham, and a shiding in cash, valued in all 9s.—Prisoier pleaded guilty—Mr. Hunt said that P.C. Cooper (36) had taken ill from pleuraw - during the night, and could not attend the court that morning,but as the prisoner had pleaded guilty he would simply state the facts of the case. The shop was shut up on Saturday night by dward Green at eleven o'clock and was bohed with a padlock outside. At about 12.30 a.m. on Sunday, P.C. Cooper was passing the shop and heard a match struck by someone inside. He also noticed that the door was even. He went inside and there he met the prisoner who, upon seeing the officer. exclaimed. " Oh! it's all right. I am the manager.' The officer, however, told him he would have to go to the police-station with him, and upon being searched. the bottle of pickles and tin of paint and the le. were found upon him. These were identified by Edward Green. The shop was then examitied and it was dis- covered that every drawer had been ransacked, and a ham was found in one corner ready to be taken away. The prisoner had came from Glasgow a short time age, and as he only lived a few doors away from the shop ,he had not far to take the things if he had not been caught. Be had got in by breaking the padlock.—The prisoner was sent to gaol for three months hard labour. A HEAVY SENTENCE UPON A WIPE BEATER. At the Warrington Borough Court on Monday, Mark O'Brien, 47 Victoria-St., was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Dallam-lane on Saturday night, also with assaulting Inspector Spinks and Detective Mona- ghan at the same time and place,and unlawfully cutting and wounding his wife Mary Ann O'Brien with intent to commit previous bcdily harm on 10th March. The charge was subs(quentiv reduced to cornmittine an ag- gravated assault upon his wife. Inseector Spinks said that he received a complaint on Saturday night of the prisoners behaviour in Dallam-lane, and he went there in company with Detective Monaghan. The prisoner was behaving in a most outrageous manner, and as soon as he saw Detective Monaghan he ran away from him, but he (Inspector Spieks) caught him. A struggle then ensued and the prisoner kicked and bit witnese several , times, and it to( k him and Detective Monaghan all their time to hold him until assistance arrived. He was the most violent character that had been in the court for sonte years. There had been a warrant out for the prisoner for assaulting his wife and that was the remelt they were so anxieus to capture him.—Detective Mona- ghan corroborated the inspector's statement, and said that he was kicked also, and the prisoner mede several attempts to bite him but could not get his teeth through his coat sleeves.—Mrs. O'Brien said her husha,nd came in drunk on March 10th, and commenced to beat her. He struck her on the forehead with something which caused blood to flow freely, and she had been attended by Dr. Pox ever since for the wound, and she had to keep her bed for a week in consequence. She had not received a sovereign from her husband for the last twelve months, and she applied fora separation. Annie Jordan also gave evidence of the assault. —The prisoner</p>
      </div>
