      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT. SATURDAT.—Before Mr. J. Burgess (chairman). and Mr. J. Geddes. A PRISONER DISM/SSED.—A BIGHT TO STAND AT ONE'S OWN DOOR.—Wilbatn Higham was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Knutsford-road that morning. P.C. Vish stated that he was on duty in Knutsford-road at about 1.20 that morning, and saw the prisoner near a door. He asked him his n,,me and address which he refused to give. He had had some drink, and witness therefore brought him to the police station. The prisoner said that he had been to Newton Bridge,and had walked back that night. He was at his own door when the officer came to him, and he was annoyed at him and that was the reason he refused to give his name and ad- dress—Mr. Burgess said that the case was not proved, and the magistrates would therefore dismissed it. The magistrates were further of opinion that a man had a perfect right to stand at his own door and refuse to give his name. A Roans AND VAGABOND.—George Baker was sent to gaol for seven days on the charge of being a rogue and vagabond found sleeping on the premises of Dallam forge early that morning, and having no visible means of sub- sistence. MONDAY. —Before Mr. F. Monks (chairman), Alderman Harrison, Mr. W. Pierpoint, Mr. S. Wright, Mr. J. W. Hayward, and Councillor Roberts. BEGGING.—Robert Thompson was dismissed on promis- ing to leave the town, for begging in Knutsford-road on Saturday night. P.S. Chantler proved the case. SUMMONED FOR DRUNICHNNEBB.—JO6II Laff, 7, Board- man's-yard tined 55.; Elizabeth Chatterly, no address, ordered to leave the town; Michael Dowd, no address, 55.; Thomas Pannell. 9, Hiil-stree:, fined ss. and costs; Jane Tickle, 8, Turner-street. sa. and costs. FURIOUS DRIVING. —Joseph Williams. cab driver, 3 Wharf"-street was fined 51. for furiously driving a horse and drag, to the danger of passengers in Wilderspomi Causeway on March 29th. P.C. Cooper (20) who proved the case said that the defendant was driving ten or</p>
        <p>twelve miles an hour. CRUELTY TO A rionsa.—John Johnson, Ship-yard,was charged with working a horse while in an unfit state on March 31, and Patrick Clarke, 12,Morris-st.,was charged with causing it to be worked. Inspector Luff said that the first"deferriant was driving the horse attached to a 'bus in Market Place. The horse was excessively lame, and upon examination, he found it suffering from a corn. The horse, after witness warned Johnson, was taken lelck to the stable, and witness there found that Clarke had sent it out.—The defendants were discharged with promising not to repeat the offence. CAUGHT AT LAST.—James Higham 9, Prince-street, was bend over for six months, and ordered to ply cests for fighting at Bridge Foot on August 2nd, 1893. P.C. Cor- coran proved the case. Tire prisoner had been working in St. Helens from the time the warrant was taken out, and said chat he did not know there was any summons against him. TUESDAY. e—Before Mr. E. J. Guest (chairman), and Dr. Starkey Smith. DRUNK.—Martin Duffy was fined 109. and costs, or in default 14 days' imprisonment, for being drunk in Regent-street, the previous afternoon. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—SamueI Bond, a stranger in the town, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in School Brow at midnight. He was fined ss. and costs. THEFT or Botrree—Francis Ellis, a married woman, was charged with stealing from the shop of Messrs. Baker and Sons, Sankey-street, a pair of boys' boots, value 23. 6d. Prisoner pleaded guilty. The Chief Con- stable stated the prisoner was seen by a person to take the beots. The manager of the shop was informed, and DS. Monaghan was made aware of the theft, and he apprehended the woman in Bewsey-street. In reply to the chargd of stealing the boots she replied, "Yes, I did take them." The prisoner who had been convicted previously for the same offence was fined 103. and costs, or one month's imprisonment. TEMPORARY AUTHORITY.—James Burrows, late of Hyde, applied for and was granted temporary authority to sell at the Queen's Hotel, Sankey-street, in lieu of Mr. Laurence Flood. Scatooe. CASES.—Mr. G. Riley,school attendance officer, prosecuted in the following cases of irregular atr.endance at school :—James Hennigan, labourer, 12, Buckley-st., boy birched; John Dickson, painter, Rodney-st., 55.; Thomas Ball, hawker, Silver-st., 55.; Henry Jewess, forgeman 21, Howard-St., ss. ; Mary Bennett, 120, Pier- point-8t.:23. 6d. ; Elizabeth Moore, William-st., ss. ; and John Green, labourer, 4, A.ntrobus-at., dismissed. WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. J. Fairclough (chairman) and Mr. J. Geddes. ARREARS OF PAYMENT.—James Ellison was sent to gaol for one month for being £2 Bs. 9d. in arrears of an order for the maintenance of his wife. FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Wright (chairman), and ouncillor Roberts. ALLEGED DESERTION TOOK THE ARMY.—William Rogers was charged with presenting himself fDr enlistment at the Warrington barracks, into F.M. Argyle Southern Highlanders, on the previous day. A sergeant from the barracks said that there were reasons to believe that the prisoner had deserted from the Argyle regiment. and the prisoner was remanded until Monday for further in- quiries to be made. A DOG AT LARGE 114 TIIH PARK.—James Bridge, 43 Winwick-road, was fined in the costs for allowing his dog to be at large in Bank Park on March 23. The park keeper (Wm. Ashton) said that the defendant refused to give his name and address when he was requested to do so, and he had to follow him to Tanners-lane where he ran away and be (Ashton) had to make inquiries to find his name.—The defendant explained his conduct by saying that Ashton came to him in a very blustering manner, but this, Ashton denied. ' BREACH OF CONTRAZT.—A claim against Elizabeth faylor, Buckley's-court, for 2s. for neglecting her work and breaking her contract with James Welsby, fustian cutter, was adjourned for one week to allow her time Ito pay.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY.SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY.SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Messrs. C. Reynolds and G. L. Davies. A NEGLIGENT FATllER.—Superintendent Atherton ap. plied for an order of maintenance against Daniel Gil- more, Penketh Lane, Runcorn, whose son is at present in an industrial schoolat Birkdale. For some consider- able time now, the rather had not contributed towards his son's maintenance whilst in the school.—An order was made for the payment of the costs and is. 6d. per week. 'OE SATURDAY NIGHT."—FOI.• being drunk and dis- orderly at Runcorn on the night of the 31st March, Patrick Corcorran and Thomas Lydiate were fined ss. A DANGEROUS DOG AT RUNCORN.—AIfred Jackson, 23, Bold-st., was summoned for keeping as dangerous dog, thc.. same not being under proper centrol, on the 28t13 ult.—Defendant pleaded not guilty.—lsaac Halewood, 21 years of age, stated that at the latter end" of Febru- ary he was standing against the gate of Mr. Jackson's house when his deg ran at him, knocked him down and then bit hirn on the leg.—James Donlon, 14, John-st., ' deposed that on the 18th March Mr. Jackson's dog threw him to the ground and bit him on the leg.—Two other children named Clara Rowles, 11, Canal-st., and Walter Sinnott, Stone-st., gave testimony supporting the con- tention that the dog was dangerous.—?.C. Gregory said that prior to the 29th March, the day on which the sum- mons bad been taken out against defendant, complaints had been received regarding the dog kept by the defend- ant. He called upon Mrs. Jackson and warned her the previous day but the following day the dog was loose and bit anotherchild.—The animal referred to was coloured black and white.—Defendant said his dog was a black One and had no white fur.—Defendant was ordered to pay the costs and to keep the dog under proper control; otherwise it would be destroyed as well as defendant being liable to a penalty of 203. for every day which the animal was not under proper control. THEFT OF BACON.—Sarah Withington, John-st., charged with stealing a quantity of bacon valued 4d. from Mr. Walter Collier's shop in Ckurch-st., Reincorn, on the evening of the 7th inst.—Evidence was tendered by Sarah Baynes and Annie Curzon proving the thaft, and the defendant was fined ss. or seven days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>----- THE FATAL AFFRAY T PRESCOT</head>
        <p>----- THE FATAL AFFRAY T PRESCOT</p>
        <p>INQUEST AND VERDICT. An inquest was held on Monday at Present relative to the death of William Travis, miner. Thomas Jones, a member of the l.cal football club, has been arrested charged with having caused his death. It is alleged that he struck and kicked the man so savagely that he died soon after the attack.—The first witness was the widow, who was very much agitated. She said her hus- band was a collier,agcd 52 years. Be left home at half- past five o'clock on Friday night in good health. He came back sober. He went out again, but did not say where he was going. Afterwards she went up the lane and saw him lying en the ground. The prisoner, Thomas Jones; was close to, but witness did not see him do any- thing to her husband. The latter was carried into the house, where Dr. Barlow attended him until his death, which teal place at half-past four on Saturday after- noon.—Thomas Fildes, a painter, said he was in the Horseshoe Inn on Friday afternoon. Jones came in about five o'clock. Witncs:s was pulling the blind down when Jones struck him in the face. Being asked what that was for, he said he could do it aoain. There had been no quarrel up to the time. The prisoner pushed, and witness and Travis fell. The landlady told them to go out, but witness stayed, and half-an-hour afterwards Mrs. Travis said her husband was hurt.—llinson Fildes said Jones came to his house and asked for Thomas Fildes. 'the latter was not in, hut seeing Travis, Jones said " You will do." Travis went out, and when in the road Jones took his aAct cif and threw it in the road. He then ran at Travis iiiith h;s fist, and struck him a violent blow on the chin, knocked him against the wall, and kicked him threo timvs in the lower part of the,body with his deg. Travis lry there until Constable Ormemd came in five minutes. Travis gave Jones no provocation whatever.—The juty retail-tied a verdict of " Wilful murder" against Thomas Jones.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 14, 1894,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 14, 1894,</p>
        <p>cident deprived the Heathens of the services of Broady, whose shoulder was hurt. For some time the warfare was rather tame, the only items of note tieing smart kicking by C,liapinan and Bose )w on the one hand, and Smith and Baldwin on the other. A free kick enabled the visitors to invade their opponents' quarters, until Elampson and Bailey headed a fine dribble to the other side. Gregcry and Barrow retaliated with a dribble, but the advantage was only momentary, as ForJer came away in the loose in fine style to the visi- tors' 25. Some good forward play by Glover, Gregory, Harnpson, Freeth,, and Knight, for their respective sides was witnessed, and Fairhurst, giving, a smart pass to Gibson, this player ran in splendidly, but the easy place was a failure. Thrs.visitors' halt backs were weak, and entirely failed to feed their three-quarters, preferring to kick down the ground,the leather always being returned asith interest. Appleton foiled several opponents, but a clever bit of passing between Smith, Baldwin, Barber, Cross, and Foy found the latter running strongly to the line. At half-time Warrington led by one try to nothing, and upon the resumption Freeth ran to his opponents' quarter. A flying kick relieved, hut Fearnley passing to Cunningham. this player made tracks for the goal line. Good passing by Warringtem was ruled forward, but the attack ultimately enchd in a tangible result. as Forster dodged round the pack and scored. Then the Heathens had a turn at attarking,. but Houghton made a fine dribble, Longmore saved smartly. Barber, who had been comparatively idle,then took the leather to the Warrington 25, and the home forwards relieved, Cross saving the onward rush. A long kick by Boscow was • mulled, and Flampson rushing up secured the leather and romped in, but the try was not allowed. Directly I afterwards, however, Canningham took adva.ntage of a mistake, and picking up smartly scored a try far out, '; place failing, and Warrington won by 0-3-2 to 0-0-0.-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>.4* Secretaries of FoAb?dl Clubs are requested to note that reports of matches played on Saturday must he sent to th,-. head office, Iletyser-street. Warrington, not later than tc,, o'clock on Wednesday rnornis2.</p>
        <p>--- GFOP.GI', G. BUSSFY and Co. are leading makers of Footballs, Hockey. Coif, mud requisites for Sports, Games. and GymnsOL. Catalogue may be had on application to the sports Manufactory. Peckham, London, S.B. BEST •E-CED.(..,. BRAND.</p>
        <p>RUG BY. WARRINGTON v. ST. HELENS. There was a great amount of interest centred in this match at Helens, for Warrington had been one of the few clubs who had beaten the Second League champions this season. The teams had ,not met since 1890 previ-</p>
        <p>ous to the pres,ent season, and at the last encounter at St. Helens, the Warrington team completely routed the</p>
        <p>St. Helens men. The Warrington team had also been victorious m every preceding match between the two teams, but as St. Helens have been going so strongly of</p>
        <p>late, it waa felt that they had nnw a splendid oppor-</p>
        <p>Wray of at length lowering the "wirepuller's" colours. That the greatest interest was centrel in the engagement</p>
        <p>VMS notified by the record-. attendance which numbered close on 10,000 of whom a great number journeyed from Warrington. The teams had met previously this season on Good Friday, when Warrington somewhat fortun- ately won. The 'Saints . had not had their colours lowered at the Knowsley-road enclosure this season, and the locals were confident of the home team maintaining this consistency-. St. Helens were fully represented, but whilst Warrington had their three-quarter line upset by the absence of Broady, the combination included the veterans Turner and Ashton; both of who'll had retired from active service. The game was commenced by Turner. who not only started up the slight slope but also had a stiffish breeze to contend against. Foulkes , was tested at the home 25, but the locals taking advan- tage of a false sere* of the " scrum " by the visitors at once transferred play to Heesom's charge. War- rington were first -penalized, but Heesom got the Sphere -safely away, and then came a bout of effec- tive work started by Turner, .Jolley, and Dakin, followed by passing en the part of the Bates, Evans and Nevins, the latter kicking judiciously. Appleton gave tbe • Saints a footing in the Warrington quarters once, and Heesom was compelled to save. Carey ran smartly following the drop-out. and when 'tfa,rrinaton looked certain scorer, Doherty effected a clinking save. Again•did the visitors' front rank display , fine form, and Foulkes was anything hut easy in staving off the repeated onslaughts. The visitors were next penalized, and Cross took the ball to haltsway,And tak- ing a huge drop he succeeded in lowering the Warrington goal, giving his side a lead of three points. Exchanges followed, and clever play by James Bate and Evans realized half way, but St. Helens persisted in the attack, Cross again reaching Heesom with a huge punt. The Warrington custodian effected a speedy clearance, and: then a- sueeession of free kicks against the locals tv as followed by Turner, Ashton and Taylor rushing to the Fairclough accomplishing a smart save. The visi- tors did not relax their efforts in the least, and although Appleton took a pass from Cross, Potts did n ot permit him to travel far. Nevins kicked well under difficulties whilst from a penalty, he made a brilliant shot at goal. Scrimmages ensued etnder the home pots, and James Bate made a wide pass across to Carey, but the transfer was lacking in judgment,and the three-quarter was soon held. A moment later Cross got clear with a grand dribble, and was only stopped five yards from the line by the united action of Evans and Heesorr. . Free kicks again to Warrington gave slight relief, but misfortune now befel the visitors, for Jolley in spoiling Fairclough had his shoulder damaged, and though he returned after a lengthened. absence he was practically of little help to his comrades. Despite this disadvantage the Warringtonians played up pluckily, and St. Helens were hard preseed until a couple of kicks by Doherty sent another minor Up. John Bate was prominent soon after the drop-out, and clever passing along the line of three quarters gained slight relief. Another free kick against Warrington was taken by Fairclough, but this time failure attended the effort, and no sooner had the oval been dropped out than Evans ran brilliantly down the open, and got to within a few yards from the line, and the oval being telsen over in a general rush by the Warrington 'forwards, Doherty raced up and kicked dead as the visitors were in the act of grounding. Fol- lowing this hard luck came a fine specimen of combina- tion by the Warrington front division. and Nevins and Evans were conspicuous with useful kicks, whilst Carey was to the fore in spoiling Doherty from getting clear. Dakin and Ashton dribbled well, and then the Bates led up to ii severe pressure on the home goal, and Foulkes for the second time adopted the saving clause. The visitors still pressed, and half-time arrived with the locals leading by a penalty goal. With the advantage of the wind it was thought that the visitors would soon rub off the deficiency. and at the onset a vigorous at- seek was ect up. Cross restarted and from a long dribble along the popular touch, James Bate almost effected a score. Wild passing by the Warrington backs lost them a distinct advantage. and Sudlow kicking well followed up quickly and Heesom was floored before he could rid himself of the leather. White- ley ant away from a line out only to be pulled up by Potts, but - the home combination -would not be 'denied the attack, and fierce scrimmages ensued under the Warrington posts. Cross tried repeatedly to -force his way over,but John Bate tackled resolutely,and relief was eventually forthcoming when Appleton failed to take a pass, and Evans and Carey rushed the leaaer to half way. A free kick to ,Warrington improved their position, whilst Dakin twice in quick succession came away from the ruck and was only checked by Foulkes kicking into tench. Grand play by Heesom stayed off an attempt to clear, whilst a beautiful round of passing, taken part in by the Bates, Evans, Potts, and Nevins, saw play a yard from the corner flag. Cross again proved the saviour of his side, and though Ashton and , -Berry made one determined effort there was no finish to the essay and only a minor accrued. A few minutes after James Bate should have scored from a pass by Thorniley, but the same hard luck was noticed when a pretty exhibition of cross-dribbling saw Carey held under the posts. The play now became exceedingly vigorous and stoppages were frequent, whilst the clamax was reached when Whiteley was ordered off the field fr,r striking an opponent. Minors were conceded the visi- tors with amazing rapidity, and though Turner made a -brilliant effort near the close, nothing tangible resulted, and the outcome of a hard and vigorous game was a victory-for the home team by a penalty goal to nil. Teams :—F. Heesom, back; W. Nevins, J. W. Evans, C. Potts and Carey. three quarters ; James Bate and John Bate (captain).- half hacks; Fair Turner, J. Jolley, J, T. Thorniley, H. Ashton, J. Berry. Buckley, G. Dakin, and J. Taylor. forwards. St. Helens : !Foulkes, back; Sufi- low. Appleton, Fairclough, and Doherty, three-quarters; Cross and Ashcroft, half hacks ; Gill, Dale, Winstanley, Lloyd Davies, Rimmer, • Wilson, Whiteley, and Graham, forwards. '</p>
        <p>WARRIN'OTON " A" V. STOCKTON FlRATlf..".Barber, Broady, and Cross, assisted their former colleagues in this match, in which there aris great interest evidenced. There was a :rood number of Spectators present. The home team commenced against a stiff breeze, and before the game was many minutes old, an unfortunate ac-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>PEN KETII.</head>
        <p>PEN KETII.</p>
        <p>CONCERT.—Last night week a concert wag given in connection with the Wesleyan Band of Hope, in the Wes- leyan schoolroom. The Rev. John Gunnell occupied the chair. The entertainment was given in aid of the funds of the society,by the "Widnes Victoria Entertain- ment Society " and consisted of songs, recitations, choruses &amp;c. The company consists of young men who devote choruses, spare time to the promotion of the temper- ance cause. Songs were given by Messrs. H. Barber, W. Hague, and A. Shingler, :the latter receiving a well deserved encore fcr his rendering of" Anchored." A violin solo was given by A. Hague, and recitations by Messrs. Jenkins, Wright, and Cros-ley. Two dialogues were given by the members, and some very well ren- dered choruses at intervals. The entertainmeut was most enjoyable, and the vote of thanks passed to the performers was heartily endorsed by all. AWARD or PRIZES.—On Sunday afterncon, at St. Mary's mission room the distribution of prizes won at the annual scripture examination took place. Mrs. Jeans presented the prizes to the successful schnlars,a.nd the Rev. E. S. Jackson delivered an address. The prizes consisted of very tasteful prayer and story books, which gave great satisfaction to the recipients. FUNERAL.—On Saturday afternoon the remains of Mr. William Ashburner were interred at St. Mary's Church, Great Sankey. The deceased it will be remembered was drowned at Stockton Heath on the 3rd inst. He has been in the employ of a Penketh tradeunan for the last four years, and was well liked in the village, and his untimely end has caused much regret. His fellow work- men attended the funeral, and also several of his com- panions. The boys had subscribed together and bought a very. pretty wreath as a token of respect to their deceased companion. The Rev. J. R. Jones performed the ceremony. • A SURPRISE PARTY.—On Monday, on the occasion of the wedding of Mr. R. Higham, the bridal party were treated to a slight surprise. Mr. Higham is an old and respected member of the St. Mary's brass band, and his fellow members decided to honour their friend's wedding with a parade. The band marched through the village past the bridegroom's home and then proceeded to the house of the bride where the wedding was being " kept up." It is needless to say the honour was much apnreeiated. CONSERTATIVB MIETING.—A meeting of the Sankey Polling District of Newton Division Conservative Associ- ation was held at the Conservative Club, Penketh, on Tuesday night. Mr. W. P. Skelton presided, in the ab- sence of Major Ridgway, and was supported by Mr. J. W. D. Barron, of Hyde, and Mr. W. Holland Owen, hon. solicitor to the Newton Division of the Conservative As- sociation, and Mr. W. A. Maudsley, hon. secretary to the division. There was only a small attendance. Major Ridgway was re-elected Chairman of the District, Mr. Davies (Bold); Mr. R. Brimelow (Peaketli), Mr. W. D. Houghton, and Mr. W. P. Skelton were re-elected vice- chairmen, Mr. Thomas Baker being added to the num- ber ; Mr. Henry Foster was re-elected hon. secretary, and Mr. James Davies (Bold) was elected hon. treasurer. in the place of Mr. Humphries (deceased). The four delegates to the Council were re-elected, as was also the Committee. OPENING OF • GOLF CLUE. —OR Saturday, the MerSey Golf Club opened their links in Cuerdley Marsh, near Penkethiin the presence el a large number of spectators. Mrs. D. M. Meßechnie performed the informal ceremony of driving the first ball. Afterwards a very pleasant game was indulged in by a large number of members present. The officers for the ensuing season are :—The Hon. T. W. Legh, M.P., honorary president; Mr. D. M. Mcliechnie, captain ; Mr. F. W. Gilbert, hon. treasurer; honorary secretary, Mr. T. Crosby, Ash Villa, Penketh.</p>
        <p>DRINKING AMONG THE ANCIENTS.</p>
        <p>It is stated that four great Asiatic monarchies of an- tiquity, Assyria, Babylonia, Media, and Persia, were not conquered and destroyed by the sword, until their earlier characteristics of manliness, patriotism, and morality had been sapped by drunkenness and de- bauchery. Familiar, but always impressive, is the ac- count history gives us of the visit of the young twelve- year-old Cyrus to his grandfather, Bing Astyages, of Media. The little fellow, destined later to overthrew Media and Babylonia, and to found the great Persian monarchy, was so astonished and disgusted at the riot- ous drunkenness of the Median court, that he refused to touch the wine, a custom expected of him as cupbearer to his grandfather. He "could not understand how the people were willing to drink till they had fallen into such a bestial state. And time brought the days when this Cyrus subjugated Media, and deposed his grandfather 8.C., 559. A few years after, when combined against Babylonia and Lydia, Cyrus was defeated just out- side the walls of Babelon. But Nabunallid (Belshazzar) the victor, instead of following up his success, arranged in its celebration that infamous feast in the midst of 'which the ominous writing was flashed along the wall by the unknown hand. During this fatuous debauch, Cyrus re-gathering his remaining forces, stormed the unprepared city, and slew Belshazzar in his cups. Per- sia, in its turn becoming weakened and emasculated by wine and the habits it generates, passed under the con- quering hand of Alexander the Great, the same who for a time withstood the corrupting influences of Persian svbaritism, and the intoxications of his own triumphs, but of whose death by intemperance Seneca writes "Here is this hero, invincible by all the toils of prodigi- ous marches, by all the dangers of sieges and combats, by the most violent extremes of heat and cold, here he lies, cenquered by his intemperance, and struck ta earth by the fatal cup of Hercules." It is difficult to imagine more horrible deeds than were done by some of the Persian rulers when under the influence of drink. On the plea of giving his people proof that Wine had no effect on his nerves, Cambvses ordered his cupbearen—the son of his chief officer Prexaspes—to go to the opposite side of the room, and there to stand quietly with his left arm over his head: Prexaspes was present, but before he could even imagine what was to happen, Cainhyses had taken aim with a bow and arrow and shot the boy through the heart. He then had the heart cut out from the youth's trembling body, and held it triumphantly before the wretched. father's eyes, exclaiming that he desired that this proof that wine did not harm him should be made known to his subjects. According to Fleredotus, Cambyses con- fined drinking to himself, his army being allowed only water. What views were held in ancient Pereia, con- cerning drinking is apparent from such facts as, for ! example, that preferment in office largely depended on how much a man could drink without losing his reason. Indeed, Cyrus, who fell in a duel with his brother Artaxerxes, had urged, among other reasons why he should be chosen before his brother, that he could drink a greater quantity than Artaxerxes" without being in- ebriated, or his passions disagreeably excited." As the great Asiatic monarchies fell first by wine and then by the sword, so Egypt, the history of whose vast and highly civilised power reaches back over three thousand years before Christ fell likewise into the slough of drink and licentiousness, and was conquered by the Persian province, B.C. -332. "The Classical Journal" for April, 1813, gives this specimen cf old Per- sian poetry :--`• With your liver intoxicated with blood it is delightful to reel like a flame ; intoxicate.d with blood, it is delightful to wallow on the ground ; whilst jovial to plunder the bower like the breeze ; to cull the rose on which the gardener has bestowed his willing care, is delightful But ins drunken fit,never bethought so weak as to rise up the first to make peace, because to be angry afresh is delightful." The Egyptians, whose country was famous for its corn are regarded as the earliest brewers, and it is claimed that they knew how to extract the juice of barley nearly two thousand years before Christ ; but wherethey learn- ed to ferment it does not appear. They very early used what they called grain wine at their libations. flerodo- tus tells us that beer or nine drawn from barley was the liquor principally used, and he describes the clergy as feasting upon the sacrifices and quaffing the sacred wine. From about four to three hundred years before Christ. the Egyptians had a number of grain wine manufac- tories at Pelusium, on the Nile. But the ancient Eeyp- tians knew also how to make intoxicating drinks from • fermented juices, such as those of the palm, figs and pomegranate. The condition of Egypt, before its in- vasion and desolation by the Persians as regards tem- perance and morality was, as we know, moss lament- able. Men and women gloried in drunkenness and shame. The few remnants of sculpture and painting that remain from the art of those days gave ample proof of the state of the people at that time. Masters are represented as carried home from their banquets in sottish unconsciousness. The dames are represented struggling with nausea from their too copious bibbinga and hurrying the maids with the necessary bossl. Joseph- us speaks of them as the mast debauched people. Great efforts had been made from time to time to save Egpyt from this evil. Several of the Pharaohs issued stringent, mandates against drunkenness, and the ominous care- mony—apparently not commanded--of placing in the, centre of the banquet tables, when the wine was "be- ginning to tell," a skeleton crowned with a funeral wreath, dates from those days. The custom of greeting women by kissing on the mouth is said to date from this time, and to have been adopted in order to discover if they had tasted wine. Among the many devices to check intemperance, was a law that the friends and re- latives of the dead should abstain from all wine and luxuries for a certain time, according to the rank and station of the departed. The higher the rank or import- ance, the longer was the abstention to he observed, ' which is significant of the great respect really felt.for temperance. " If," as Morewood so eloquently says of ancient Egypt. "a secret glow of veneration arises for ' a nation so long distinguished in the annals of antiquity for all that was majestic and mighty, whether we con- sider its almost superlmman structures its profound education, its wonderful inventions, or the splendour, pomp, and glory, which surrounded its early inhabit- ants, how different the feeling which presses on the ' heart of him who, standing to-clay in the shadow of the ' Sphinix, sees only the lonely Nile and the far-stretehine torrid sands, both alike as dumb and vestigeless to him of those nobler realities as are the strong lips and fixed unsleeping eyes."</p>
