      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>JUNIOR CUP COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>JUNIOR CUP COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Sr. PETERS V. LATCHFORD RANGERS.—This match was played on the Warrington ground on Monday evening. The Rangers started the oval, and the Saints at once began to press, and after twenty minutes play Moores scored after a grind run, Rutter failing at goal. Nothing more V 4 as scored before the interval. In the second half Letwless'and Roberts scored, Rutter converting the lat- tpr try. The final scores were—St. Peters, 1 goal, 2 tries; Rangers. nil. WAR RINGTON RANGKRS Y. STOCKTON HEATH.—Thesp junior Rugby clubs met,in the second round of the Junior Rugby Competition, on the Warrington ground on Thursday night, before a good number of spectators. The teams were very evenly matched, and the fir 4 half ended with a clean sheet. At the close of the second half, however, tr,vans dropped a goal for the Rangers and as this was the only score, the Rangers won by four points to nil. During the game Barber, who played for Stockton Beath, made a splendid run almost the length of the field, hnd was most unfortunate in Rot scoring.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN V. BIRKENHEAD WANDERERS.—A contest in which unmistakable interest was centred, was decided on the St. Anne's ground, Birkenhead, on Saturday afternoon, between teams iepresenting the above clubs. The visitors were without six good men, namely, St übbs, W. Faulkner, Abrams, Mcores, Farmer and Lightfoot. whilst the home team were only minus the services of Herbert. The Runcornians, immediately the game began, pressed heavily, the first goal being notched by Butter- worth. Just upon the call of half time the same player secured a second goal through the instrumentality of Taylor. In the second half, play was all in favour of the visitors who were certainly by far the better men,being 1 successful in scoring three adclitiontl goals and a try, the game resulting in a win by five goals, one try, to nothing. RUNCORN RECREATION V. WIDNES RANGERS.—The " Recs." journeyed on Saturday afternoon over the water to play their return match with the Rangers which at the finish ended in their defeat. The visitors commenced operations and appeared to have matters pretty well all their own way, Hughes being successful in securing the first " try " which was the only point scored in the first half. on the resumption of play the " Recs." were bound wanting in their usual dash, and allowed the homesters to possess the " helm "-for some time. Towards the close of the game they tried their utmost to regain their lost ground, but their efforts were 1 fruitless and the result was a victory for Widnes by one goal, two tries, and two minors, to two tries and five minors. -.N.-</p>
        <p>RUNCORN VICTORIA Ir. LOSTOCK GRALAM.—On Satur- day afternoon at Lostock these teams tried conclusions, the former being one man short. This, however, did not exercise a fatal effect inasmuch as after a most in- teresting came they found themselves the victors by two goals (one dropped), two tries, four minors ; to their opponents (Lostock) three tries and one minor.</p>
        <p>- - WIDNES v. CROMPTOIC—PIayed at Widnes, on Saturday before 2,000 spectators. Barber kie,ked off, and Widnes at once attacked. The Crompton lines had several nar- row escapes, and then some fine kicking transferred the play to the home 25, where Barber saved cleverly. The home three-quarters then shmwed some brilliant combin- ation, but the Crormaton defence was very safes and play settled in midfield. The game deteriorated into a series of scrimmages, neither team being able to claim much advantage. Some fine passing by the home team enabled Clarke to score from a pass by Rispan. Barber failed at goal. The remainder of the play was principally con- fined to ' the centre. Half-time scoie :—Vs idnes 1 try, Crompton nil, After the restart goals were dropped by Carr and Respan, while Clarks (2), Alford, Dunhavand, and Gracat,vained tries. Final result :—Widnes 4 goals, 4 tries, (30 points), Crompton nil. RUNCORN v. RIPST o CIEUISIIIRB.—This match, which was fixed for the benefit of various medical charities already reported, created considerable interest and would csrtainly have been 'witnessed by more people at Goodison Park, on Wednesday afternoon had it not been for the wet weather. As it was, however, there were only about 1,500 spectators. Runcorn opened play and in the first half secured a try to their opponents nil. In the second portion of the game they continued to press heavily, not affording their enimies a single chance,and having all the best of the play. The final result was : Runcorn one goal, three tries; Rest of Cheshire, nothing.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS RECREATION v. OLDIIAM. —Played at Old- ham on Saturday. The home side kicked off against a strong wind. The Rees. were short of Fildes and Potts, Pennington being abFent from Oldham.. From a line out, Seddon got the ball near the half flag, and dropped a splendid goal. From the kick off, Oldham rushed well down the field • Wilson made his mark, and kicked in touch. From this M'Cutchenn kicked, hut Trow charged down, and just missed scoring. Later Oldham got a free kick in front of the post, and forced the Recs. on their line ; but at this point Trow took the hall well into the Oldham quarters, where the Recs. severely pressed, hut could not score. Varley at this point got hurt, and left. the field. Frun a scrimmage on the line Fillingham receiced, and ran over near the corner. Hetherington failed at goal. Rain at this time was coming down ' freely. Half-time score :—Recreation 4 points, Oldham 3 points. Yates scored for the Recs. after the re- start. Final result :—Recreation 7. pGints, Oldham, 3 points.</p>
        <p>Rt. PTURE CURED WITHOUT OPERATION.—S. J. Sherman, Hernia Specialiit, 64. Chancery-Lane, London; 26, King- stree', Manchester; and 4, Burlington-chambers. sew-street, Bfrm'ngli im. Book on rupture and evil of trusses, post free, 7a.—Advt.</p>
