      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO MU. G. WEEKS AT</p>
        <p>AFFAIRS AT ST. Joas's</p>
        <p>There was a very pleasant gathering in the dis- trict Infant schoolroom, Earleetown, on Tuesday evening for the purpose of presenting to Mr. G. W. Weeks, late organist at St. John's Church, a token of esteem from the members of the choir, on his retirement from the position of organist. Mr. J. Lawless presided, and he was supported by Mr. J. Wad- dington and Mr It. Jones, and a good number of the choir and friends. The Chairman said they all knew why they had met that night, it was to make a presenta- tion to Mr. Weeks. He was sorry there were not more: members of the choir present, but it seemed to him there were some guilty consciences about. All the members of the choir had subscribed to the present, and if they had subscribed with a good heart and free will, they should have been there that night. Mr. Weeks had had a most responsible post, and he had spoken harsh at times, and there had been grievances. If they had thought that they were all working in God's work, they would have put their grievances aside, and come for- ward and made the meeting a happy one. He did not see why grievances Should exist. Mr. Week' had done his duty while he had been amongst them, although there were times when it was necessary to speak harshly. Again, the Vicar who was not amongst them, ought to have had a voice in the presentation ; but perhaps it wouli not have been consistent for the Vicar or church- wardens to have had a voice in it—(iiear, hear)—seeing that they had treated Mr. Weeks so badly. (Applause.) Mr. J. Waddington said it pleased him very much in- I deed, when he was asked to come and make the pre- sentation to Mr. Weeks. He had the greatest respect for Mr. Weeks. He had not had very much to do with him, but whenever he came into contact with him, he found him all that could be desired. (Hear, hear.) He bad the greatest honour in presenting that timepiece to Mr. Weeks, and he asked him net to look upon the clock for its intrinsic value, but in remembrance of the choir. He then handed him the clock, w Well is a pretty lever travelling clock and case, striking the hours and half hours on a gong. It has been made by Meesrs. Nlorath Bros. of Liverpool, and bears the following inscription : *• Presented to Mr. G. W. Weeks, organist and choir- master, by the choir and friends on his leaving St. John Church, Earlestown, Easter 1894." Mr. Weeks, ins acknowledging the gift beg,ged to convey his warmest thanks to the members of the choir. During the seven , years he had been amongst them, he had endeavoured to do his duties satisfactorily to himself and the choir, as far as circumstances would permit, and as far ads lay in his power. Whatever difference might have arisen between himself and the (Moir was a thing of the past. Mr. Southern in proposing a vote of thatice to the chair- man said he had given expression to many things he would have liked to allude to himself. He had seen this coining for more than two years, and it had cowrie at last, and when he asked one of the wardens what Mr. Weeks had been turned away for, he was told that he did not get up concerts &amp;c., in order to defray the expenses of the church, as he promised to do. He (the speaker) said that was not true, It was simply to make room for another' and why had they, advertised in the papers, when they knew someone had already been appointed. He was pleased there were rase or two of the choir not present, because if they had been present they would have been out of place. -Meer, hear.) The Chairman briefly acknowledged the vote, and moved a vote of thanks to the collectors. He said they had no people's warden in St. John's Church ai he did reot attend to the money. The Vicar's warden did that,and he (Mr. Lawless) had made up his mind that not a penny should, after Easter, go on the plate from him, but be would give it to some other object. Mr. H. Jones replied on behalf of the collectors, and the remainder of the evening was spent in a social manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>EARLESTOWN BAPTIST CHURCH.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN BAPTIST CHURCH.</p>
        <p>SALE OF WORK.</p>
        <p>In connection with the above place of worship, and towards raising the sum of £l5O to clear off the debt still remaining on the chapel, a two days sale of work was held in the Town Hall, Karlestown, on Wednesday and Thuralay. The seven stall.; were heavily laden with all kinds of articles, peculiar to such affairs, clothing, needlework, arid fancy work of various kinds predominating. There was also a variety : of artistic items, and a large assortment of toys to please the youngsters. No. 1 t:tall was presided ever by Mrs. Cottam No. 2, Mrs. Phillips, ; N.). 3, Mrs. Harvey ; No: 4,Mrs. Ilulme ; No. 5, Mrs. Grissold and Mrs. E. Eckers- , ley ; refreshment stall. Mrs. Clare ; flower stall, Misses Partington and Miss Hulms, and the bran pie was in charge of Miss Limond. Judging by the condition of the hall, and the stalls., it is very evident that all connected with the Baptist persuasion in Earl-stown have worked energetically to make the affair a success, the Rev. J. Cottom, Mr. G. Clarke and Mr. S. Harvey bein the moving spirts, their afforts being well backed up by an -active committee composed chiefly of ladies. In addition to the stalls, there was a" fine art " gallery, which was under the supervision of MessraG.S. Clarke, W.H.Kenn- ing, and L. Harvey. A number of exhibits of a curious character bearing satirical titles were laughingly inspcc- ted.—There was also a museum of foreign curiosities,very nicely arranged in charge of Mr. K. U. Halme. At in- tervals during the afternoon and evening of each of the days, some very pleasing instrumental and vocal enter- tainments were given. Mr. J. H. Peplow and Mr. E.C. Pryor beguiling some portion of the time on Wednes- day, with violin and piano, their selections being very well submitted and much appreeiate(l.—Children's con- certs, under the direction of Miss J. Parting-ton, and , choir concerts under the direction of Mr. E. Eckerslev were also given. Concerts were also given by ladies and gemtlemen connected with other places of worship, who gave their services in support of the cause, amongst. others there were Miss Wood, Miss May Clare, Miss Urquhart. Mrs. Latimer, Messrs. F. Clare, E. C. Pryor, and Mr. J. Nichols. Mr. H. Jones of the Congregational Churah also brought a party and gave a m usical drill. The sale should have been opened on Wednesday, by Dr. R. Gordon Smith of Liverpool, but he was unavoidably prevented from being present, and the opening ceremony was performed by Col. • Whitney, of Liver- pool. who was accompanied on the platform by the Revs T. R. Matthews, T. W,ynnelones,Messrs. Aveline,Drirlg, and Harvey, and there were about 150 persons present. Col. Whitney was very brief in declaring the sale open, remarking that the cause was a good one, and deserved their earnest supp)rt. He said he did not believe in bazaars, if any other way of raising money . coull be devised. On Thursday another disappointment took place, owing to Mr. J. Scholefield. of Bootle, who should have opened, the sale, not being present, and the opening ceremony= had to be performed by the Rev. J. Cottam. The sale was carried on very briskly on Wednesday,and about £BO represented the result of the first day's ta- ints, -which was considered very satisfactory.</p>
        <p>WORTH KNOWING.—WOODEND'S FAMOUS NINE OILS. is a bon to sufferers and acts like magic in all eaAeR of RHEUMATISM, SPRAINS, BRUISES. and STIFF JOINTS. Price Is. 4d. per bottle, carriage paid.—J.Woonma chemist, 22, Pitt-street. Liverpool.—Alvt.</p>
        <p>The dispute between the Nelson weavers and the masters with regard te the adoption by the latt-r of the Choi.leF list was accentuated on Wednesda7, when the operatives came nut on strike. Nearly 2,000 hands are idle.</p>
