        <p>THE NEW ST. HELENS RAILWAY.</p>
        <p>An official inspection of the works on the Liverpool, St. Helens. and South 145,ncashire Fana .way took place on Tuesday. The party cons 10; of Mr. H. Seton-Karr, M.P. chairman of direct% Mr. William Pollitt, general manager of the Mt' chester, Sheffield and Lincolnshire Railway pany ; Sir .Douglas leox, engineer; Mr. Wilooeil: engineer, Colonel Hutton Mr. Holt,secretary to the and L ; Mr. Chapman, Hutton, Joseph Beer:hal:o&lt;o. FL S. Oppenheirn, solicitor ; and Mr. S. W. Pilling, C`',..„.6 trsctor. A. start was made in M.S. and L. coaches fr,:t Lowton Junction at half past ten, but on arriviag Po. Lodge-lane, Haydock, the party were transferred °the truck, and in this they were conveyed partly oeei rlrmanent lines and partly over. the contractor's 1,",,0 to Merton Bank-lane St. Helens. A double line lia3bB'o, laid from Lowton to Golborne, two miles, The line l'eria I crosses the main road and the main line of the 1,i3,g N.-W. by a line viaduct. About two miles further a deep cutting is in progress, and, vnerdly ePeal‘dirTyit between Lodge-lane. where the Ashton Station o" .„ill placed, and Holly ',link, where the Haydock Station be situated, a larre amount of work is necesslry to ci`',,*ct plete the undertaking, The party traversed the viary-, from Blackbrook to Sr.. Helens, and found it in a . Able state. The directors expressed themselves saik with the work f the contractor, and, with the 1731500, of additional capital, the St. Helens. Haydock, and Golborne Stations should soon as,ume shape. The party lunched at the Fleece Hotel, St. and returned at half pait two o'clai!lt in the tNt, or Haydock, and were again transferred to the ;pore °- lions carriages en, route for home. at. On Tuesday afternoon the ground for the tlecLe'et, He!ens Station was laid out by Sir Douglas Fog, of the line, and Mr. William Pollitt, general man* the Manchester. Sheffield, and Lincolrsiibile llailwaY•</p>
