      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <p>EIiRLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>UNTIIID SUNDAY SCHOOL DEMONSTRATION. A meeting of the Committees of various Sunday schools was held on Tuesday evening, when it was decided to hold the annual demonstration of Sunday schools on Whit Monday. The rou, e was decided upon, and the ballot tor places was made. NEWTON NURSERY BOWLING CLUB. The bowling season in connection with this club was inaugurated on Saturday, under favourable conditions. A handicap had been arranged, for which there were 40 entties,and some well contested games were played. At the close the first prize. consisting of a set of bowls was secured by Mr. G. McCormick, Mr. J. McDonald being second, Mr. W. McCormick jun., third,and Mr. J. Randall, fourth.</p>
        <p>CHARITABLE CONCERT AT EARLBSTOWN. On Saturday evening a concert was given in the Co-operative Hall, by the Earlestoscn Wesleyan Band of Hope, in aid of a blind man. The Rev. T. Wynne Jones presided, and there was a very fair audience. The concert was much enjoyed, the programme being nicely selected, and sustained by Misses May Clare, Davies and Jones, Messrs. F. Clare, Pryor, Warburton, Whatmore, with Mr. A, Limond as humorist. CHILDREN'S CONCERT AT NEWTON. On Wednesday evening a children's concert was held in the Congregational t'clioolroom, Newton, in aid of the Band of Hope, and towards renovating the class rooms of the school. The Rev. James Allatt presided, and there was a very fair audience. An excellent program•ne of songs, recitations, musi- cal drills, action songs. &amp;c., were given by children connected with the Sunday School and Band of Hope, in a very efficient and pleasing manner. Mr. Isherwood also gave a selection on the Fairy Bells, and musical glasses.</p>
        <p>SONS OF TEMPERANCE. The Semite! Morley section of cadets of temper- ance met on Wednesday in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown. Bro. J. Charnley V.A. preiided. Several samples of work was brought in by the members for the National Cadet competition at Newcastle- upon-Tyne to be held in Whit-week. The work has to be forwarded not later than the 14th. At eight o'clock the adult division met. Bro. S. Foden pre- sided. The financial scribe and auditors presented the quarterly reports which were considered very satisfactory and adopted. Afterwards,arrangements were made for the Grand Division annual meeting on Saturday afternoon next in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown. GOOD TEMPLAR&amp; The weekly session of the Hope' of Eariestown Lodge of Good Templars, was held on Monday even- ing in the Co-operative Hall. Bro. G. E. Warburton presided and there was a very fair attesdance. One candidate was initiated. The digest, of business for the meeting of No. 1 Convention to be held on , Saturday at Widnes, was read by Bro. E. Newman. , Q. M. Sergeant Roberts of Warrington was present as a visitor and gave an interesting address. An open meeting was subsequently held, when the atten- dance was augmented. Songs, recitations readings 1 So., were given by various members,and the session proved most enjoyable. , NEWTON CONSERVATIVE ASSOCIATION. The annual meeting of the Newton-in-Makerfleld polling district of the Newton Conservative Asso- ciation, was held is the Town Hall, Newton, on Wednesday evening. Mr. T. G. Gillespie presided, anti there were present Messrs. R. Stone, G. Clarke, W. Mawdsley, S. Leather and others. After the election of officers, the following resolution was unanimously passed, on the motion of the Chair- man, seconded by Mr. R. Stone: "That this meet- ing desires to express its unabated confidence in the Unionist party in the House of Commons, and to thank them, along with the Unionist members in the House of Lords, for their determined opposition to the Home Rule Bill which led to the defeat of this mischievous measure by so large a majority.' VIADUCT INSTITUTE BOWLING CLUB. The opening handicap in connection with the above club, took place on Saturday aftsrnoon on the Institute Green in fine but cold weather. The green was in excellent condition censidering the early date. The handicap was well patronised, no fewer than 40 members taking part, and some goon play was seen. The first prize was a handsome set of bowls given by the club, and was taken by John Green, the second prize a set of carvers,wss secured by Jas. Gees, and J. Butler took, as the t'ital prize, an inkstand. The prizes were distributed by Mr. James Mitchell, vice-president. The bowling woe exceptionally good throughout the day, and if some of the members maintain their present form, the club will not be far of the top in the Newton Divi- sion Challenge Competition.</p>
        <p>WARGRA.VE .CHURCH DIY PCHOOLS. The report of the Inspector on the examination in religious instrultion recently held iti the above schools has been received, and is presented in three groups. In it the Inspector states: The (order and tone of the school are excellent, and the examina- tion shows that • the religious teaching has been thorough, sound, and such as to call fo:•th the in- telligent interest ni the children. Of this intellig- ence the plainest indications are coupled in the highest group, Where work, well above the average has been done. The second group also shows os much keenness as for the age of the pupils, one could desire. The youngest children, group 3, are very well taught, and answer correctly and well. The written work in all the groups is remarkfibly accurate, and as neat as it is accurate. Hymns are very nicely sung, and the large selections of collects are well known and very reverently repeated. Present 249, average 220.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>HOUSE BREAKING AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>HOUSE BREAKING AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>At the Town Hall, Newton, on Monday morning, before uolonel Wood, a man named Joseph Booth, alias Bowers, 60 years of age, living at Porshaw's common lodging-house, was charged with breaking and entering the dwelling-house, 430, Wargrave- road, some time between 3 p.m. and 11.30 p.m. on Saturday last, and stealing therefrom a gentleman's watch, lady's silver watch and gold chain, value £1.4, the property of Richard Coppul. The prosecu- tor and his wife left home at 3 p.m. on Saturday, leaving everything secure. On returning home at 11.30 p.m., they found the house had been entered by the breaking of the back scullery Window catch and on going upstairs they found that the dressing r table drawers were pulling out and put on the floor, and the articles named were missing. Information was giving to the police, and P.C. Schofield appre- hended the prisoner an hour afterwards. On being charged prisoner said he did not do it. On search- ing him the police found the lady's watch and gold chain in his trousers pocket. Ile was remanded until Saturday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>HEALTH OF ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>THE HEALTH OF ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the St. Helens Health Committee, on Wednesday. Cou ncillor J. Forster presiding, Dr. M'Nicoll medical officer. submitted his report for the year end- ing December 31 last, which ,stated that the past year was characterised by two prominent features from a public health point of view. The first and most impor- tant was the amount of real sanitary work that had been accomplished in the direction of sewage disposal, provi- sion of proper hospitals for isolation, of good slaughter house accommodation; the extended borough and powers given by the Act of 1893; the passing of new byelaws for streets and buildings, and the extension of the water supply. The second important characteristic they all deplored. and that was the high mortality tram measles and diarrlicea. The epidemic of measles during the first"two quarters caused 135 deaths, and during the exceptionally hot summer, 198 deaths were caused by diarrhcea,.ls6 of which were those of children under three years of age. The births during the year num- bered 3,029, and the deaths 1,769, including 962 males and 807 females. The death rate for the year was 23•7 per 1,000, and the birth rate was again one of the high- est in all England-40.1 per 1,030. Sinallpox,wliich was .so prevalent in other neighbouring towns, was success- fully kept in check. The most favourable circumstances happenedlor the development of typhoid fever, and, as a result, we had a larger number of cases (315 cases and 52 deaths) than usual. The medical officer also sug- ' gested the great danger of an epidemic. The report was adopted, and ordered to be printed and circulated.—ln their ordinary fortnightly reports, the medical officers reported that during the past fortnight the births .had been 118 and the deaths 67, the latter being at the rate of 22.8 per 1,000, against 15.6 in the previous fortnight, and 22.9 in the corresponding period last year. The deaths from zymotic diseases numbered 11, and 40 cases of sickness from infectious diseases bad be reported.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday the Tay Jute Works, Dundee, were closed owing to a dispute with the workneople with re- gard to the hours of labour. Nearly 3,000 hands are idle.</p>
        <p>Edison has been saluted by the G.O.M. through the phonograph, the ex-rremier having spoken a compli- mentary message to the great inventor into the machine. Edison has not been outdone, but has returned the com- pliment handsomely. Here is his opinion of Mr. Glad- stone: "He is the most marvellous organism that has been produced for nineteen centuries. The evolutions of another five hundred years, however, may make such men common."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>ST HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST HELENS.</p>
        <p>• Comucir Gums' Smoot,. The annual" children's evening "in connection with Cowley Girls ichOol, S. Hens, was held in the Town HAI, St. Helens, on Wednesday night, under the pre- sidency of the Mayor (Mr. A. Sinclair). The Mayor's prizes were distributed by. the Mayoress (Miss Sinclair), and the subsequent programme included charades,glees, and musical drill by the gITiS. NA.REIOW ESCAPE OF A ST. HELENS GIRL. On Tuesday afternoon Agnes Brunskill, aged eleven, daughter of Mr. Brunskill, butcher, Higher Parr-st., St. Helens, was walking along the canal side when she stumbled over a stone and fell into the water. Another girl, who witnessed the accident, raised an alarm, and nu engine driver at M-Bride's Alkali Works promptly seized a rope and ran to her assistance. The girl was got out and taken home in a very exhausted condition, when the usual remedies were succe.,sfully applied. THE PROPOSED NEIN; BATHS. The Corporation of St., Helens having applied to the Local Government Board for sanction to borrow £3,000 for the erection of new baths in the neighbourh.od of Sutton, near St. Helens Junction, the Local Government Board has instructed Colonel John Ord [lasted. R.E., to hold an inquiry into the scheme Qn Friday, the 20th inst. Ratepayers may be present. No opposition is expected, as the hats will be a boon to the working people of that district.</p>
        <p>BUTCHERS AND THE MEAT INSPECTOR. A momorial from the St. Helens Butz Association urging that the time had now arrived for the appoint- ment of a meat inspector, who should devote the whole of his time to the duties of the office, and that he shou7l wear uniform distinct from that of members of the pollee force, was read at a meeting of the St. Helens Corporation Health Cominitte on Wednesday afternoon: The Chairman •(Councilior Forstn) suggested that ths committee could best consider the matter when the new cm-Duration abattlir had been erected,and it was decided to leave it over until that time. UNIONIST DEINNSTRATION.</p>
        <p>The Unionists of 'St.- Helens held a demonstration in the Volunteer Hall - St. Helensson Wednesday night. The chair was occupied by C.).lonel R. Pilkington (Lib- eral Unionist), who was supported by Mr. Dunbar P. Barton. QC., m.r„ for Mid-Armagh, Mr. H. Seton-Karr M.P., and others.—The Chairman moved—" That this meeting desires to acknowledge the splendid services of the Unionist leaders in their grand struggle against the unconstitutional and disastrous policy of Home Rule, cud approving of their political conduct gen erally,lire- by pledges ilFelf to continue its support to them lathe future." Mr. H. Soton-Karr, M.P., beconded, and Mr. . Dunbar P. Barton supported the resolution, which was. unanimously carried. THE RETIRING WATER ENGINEER. On Saturday, in the Mayor's parlour of the St. Helens Town Hall, Mr. D. M. F. Gaskin was presented with a ' silver tea and 'coffe service; value £l5. The coffee pot bears the following inscription :—" Presented to Drmald, M. F. Gaskin, Eq., M.lnst.• C.E., for seventeen and a half years, water engineer to the Corporation of St. - Helens, by members of his staff as a mark of their high- est respect, on his appointment as water eagineer to tie Corporation of Nottingham, 9th April, 1894." The monogram " D.M.F.G." is eneraved on each piece of plate. The service has been supplied by Mr. F. Hilton, of Church-street. This week Mr. Gla,skin will be the re- cipient of a handsome present from the heads of the de- partments at the St. Helens Town Hall. FIRST AID AT ST. HELENS. Thursday, at St. Helens Town Hall, a distribution of certificat!,s lo members of the borough police force, who hare•passed the examination in the first aid under the St. John.Ainbulance Association took place. There were sixteen recipients. The class has been instructed by Dr. Robertson, assistant medical 'officer of health. The full strength of the St. Helens police force isseventy nine. Of this number sixty-eight have passed the third examination, three have passed the second, seven have passed the first, and only one man, who has recently joined the force, rernains unqualified. The Mayor (Alderman A. Sinclair) presided. Alter Mrs. R. Pilking ton, of Rainford Hall, had distributed the certificates, Chief Constable Wood, on behalf of the police force, tl,anked Dr. Robertson for instructing the class,and Mr. ' Thomas, the secretary, for the services he had rendered in a mbulanee•Work. and Mrs. Pilltington for presenting the crtificates.</p>
        <p>QUARTERLY MEETING OF RECHABITES The quarterly meeting of the District Council of the Independent Order of Rechabites was held in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown, on Tuesday even- ing. Bro. Clarke, D.C.R., of Ashton presided, assisted by all the officers and the trustees of the district. The annual report and balance sheet was read by Bro. W. Hi.'ll, D.S., of Warrington, in which he said he had great pleasure in stating that the district had an actual increase in members of 39, although the total number initiated during the year was 85. The number of members at the end of 1892 was 427, arid at the end of 1893, 466. There had been a loss through lapses of 40, and deaths 6. The Council was called upon to look into the question of the leakage, • and endeavour to stay, or find out its cause.' The sum of £75 had been paid in death claims, which was the heaviest they had had. The amount of the district funds at December 31st, 1892, was £570 , 80., and at the end of December 1893, £595 ' " I 5s 5d an increase of nearly £2O on the year, which s was satisfactory considering they had paid such a 1, large amount in death claims. The scheme of in- fantile insurance was brought up for discussion and tables, with rules in relatim to same were presented, I but they were allowed to lie over until the next meeting. The, next meeting will be held in Penketh n July.</p>
      </div>
