      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>tetters to the iebitou.</head>
        <p>tetters to the iebitou.</p>
        <p>MR. HARROP AND TIPI WARRINGTON GAS COMMITTER.</p>
        <p>Mr. John. Harrop. of 122, Bridge-street Warrington, Furni-</p>
        <p>ture s.nd Bassinetb, Manufacturer BEGS to explain to the HATEP.A.YERS and In- HABITANTS of Warrington and Neighbourhood, that the reason of the Gas Cunruitteii cutting off the Gas from his premises on Thursday- last, was that he refused to comply with the unnwessary demand of the Gas Committee to give security for G4s to be suirplied to him, although his whole sale customers in Warrington are large consumers of gas, and no deposit has been demanded from any of them.</p>
        <p>Mr. HARROP begs further to say that all his Gas Accounts for the prvt two years during which he has been in Warring- )n have been p.nictually paid when they have become due. He will therefore be compelled to light, up his shop for the preseet with lamps, until he can make other arrangemc nts for future lighting. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. lIARROP prormses to give to the putdlc In a few days a full explanation of the pr ,ceecnngs between the Gas Com- mittee and himself, to allow the public to judge for them- selves whether he has been 'airy treated or not. Nexr. week the wht.le of tit:, Correspondencn between Mr. Harrop and the Gas Committee will be published in these Columns.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>ST. JOHN AIIBULkNOK AsSOCIATION.—A</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN AIIBULkNOK AsSOCIATION.—A</p>
        <p>SUGG ES,TION.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln your issue of Saturday last a correspondent signing himself 6' Progression" Wrote on behalf of the St. John Ambiilance Association and urged upon some influential g,entleinan to give a cup for competition to the Warrington ce.ntre. Personally. I cannot see what good this would do, and what " krogrcssion " really is wishful for, judging from the tone of 'his letter, is to arouse the interest in so noble a cause. Is is seven years since I obtained my medallic-in. and from what I have been told there are upwards of 300 members in Warring- ton who like myself have no means of keepin!..., them- selves in touch with the movement. If anything ia done at all, I should suggest that a Society be formed. officers elected to secure a place for holding practices, tc!%, in. To this end I shah be only too glad to give what help I can, and wouli meet your correspondent " Progression " any time during next vrea either at my private address, or at the Bookstall, Central Station. Thanking you in. anticipation l'or inserting this in your valuable paper.— I remain, yours truly, H. PRITCHARD. 162 Orl3rd-lane, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>THP: BOARD OF GUARDIANS AND THE PARISH COUNCILS ACT.</head>
        <p>THP: BOARD OF GUARDIANS AND THE PARISH COUNCILS ACT.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Referring to the report in your issue of the 7th , inst. of the reconnneudations adopted at the meeting of the Beard af Guardians, I think it only fair that the rate- payers of Warrington should have an epportunity of knowing that an additional injustice has been inflicted i upon them, and that of the six Warriugton Guardians , who were present, not one supported me in resisting this ; injustice. I may be told that the injuetice is of a trifling charac- ter, but .as it materially affects the constitution at an elective body I cannot- share that opinion ; nor, as is clear from their votes, was it shared by the representa- tives of the out parishes of the Union, whose relative position will be improved at the expense of Warrington. Hitherto representation has been based partly on per- sonal, partly on property qualificationa, but even on that system Warrington was at a disadvantage compared with the other townships el the Union. Though pos- sessing nearly half the rateable value and more than half of tne population of the (Ilion, Warrington only returns one third of the Guardians, and as compared with some of the rural parishes the contrast is of the most extraordinary character. In the new Act—commonly spoken of as the Parish Councils Act—the democratic principle of one man one vote is frankly accepted, and careful provision is made in various sections and clauses for the grouping of small parishes, the beeaking up of large ones, the alteratieu of parish borough and county boundaries and the vigorous application of dernociatic principles in local government generally. lam quite aware that it is impossible to get absolute uniformity of representation—the complexity of our local arrangements forbids that—and I said so as plainly as possible at the Board meeting ; but the Act makes ample provision for equitable readjustments of boundaries and of representation, and I think that the Guardians had no moral right to accept a scheme which aggravates instead of diminishes existing in equalities. Under the scheme submitted by the .Clerk (I take the figures of the 1891 census for the areas affected, in ac- cordance Fah sec. 75 clause 2 of the Act) 43,958 people in Warrington will be represented by 8 Guardians ; 19,396 people in Newton and Haydock will have the same number; in other words a Newton ratepayer will bave 4 times the voting power of one in Warrington ; under the existing system he has 4 times the power. Under the existing system a Cuerdley ratepayer has 23 titilfe the voting power of one in Warrington ; under the new serjeme e' e wig have 28 times that power. It malt,. be that a Tlay.i2f,lf. c9l.!?r is 4 times as intel- ligent, ifidd that a Cneraley Algricultaat W?`:".;.1.10 28 times as'intelligent as a Warrington workingman, but 1' appreh: 'd that when next November comes round, some of the,: SS ela will resent the injustice, which in *de- fiance ' , the spirit of the now Act, and of all modern legislatiOn, is inflicted upon them by the scheme drawn up by tft, elerk, submitted to the board and rushed throu ' About adequate consideration or discussion : I am alf opinion that the Warrington ratepayers' just resen ..'," t will not be diminished when they realise that m eselitary protest against this unfair scheme was deride '4.od voted down by the other Warrington Guar- dians . rant. —Yours truly, ALFRED J. JOLLEY. S4X;IALLSNI. Sir,--I-notice in your columns of March 17th, a long letter bea3ed "Does Socialism mean selfishness l'" and signed E.'Whitharn. New I have carefully perused that letter and I do not find any argument dealing with the heading.- The questions I asked concerning Socialism of Mr. Whitham, remain unanswered, and if be ever answers them I am prepared with a fresh batch equally as pertiaeut, showing that, so far as Socialism goes, his cry is that of one who has eaten his cake and is desirous of a slice of the cake of those who have saved theirs. Apart from the question,he talks of much that is beyond my knowledge. If I felt interested I might inquire further, but lam not interested. Some allusion is made to my remarks concerning his running down the cause of temperance in the columns of the "Examiner" In those letters of which I spoke, he claimed to be a friend of temperance and was engaged in pulling teetotalers to pieces. To msetempetance means teetotalism,and every moderate drinker (if he be only a moderate drinker and always moderate) is a will-o'-the-wisp, temeting his fellow man to destruction; yea, every moderate drinker is the betrayer of his fellow man and when he sees a drunkard in the street, he can say to himself : "My influence has been in favour of drunkenness." There would be no drunkards if there were no moderators. Every man who pulls or attempts to pull teetotalism to pieces, is attacking the only influence in this land which has done anything to item the tide of drunkenness, and I always doubt the desire of, any man,to conscientiously attempt to sober his fellow maim, who would destroy or weaken his only safe guard. I well remember the sub- stance of H. W's words, "I sin a pot-house demonstra- tor of the value of temperance." It. as he points out, Sir Gilbert GreenalPs trade-is an unholy one and has the . tendency to deprave his fellow man, he can take the , unction to his soul that he was a patron of Sir Gilbert's institutions and aided the increase of his wealth and li is power for evil. klr. Whithaca says, in other words, he is in ware that Socialism proposes to give everyone the same wage. If that be not part of the programme of Socialism, then the line taken by "Common Justice" is evidently at fault, fer he claims the ability of the individual as the property of the nation. Perhaps the two will fight this point out and let us know their socialistic decision. With reference to this point, I consulted my friend Mr. Maliony, the leader of the labour party in this labour represented constitu- ency. " Well," said be," that is part of our programme. It is pretty evident you could riot decrease the profits of capitalists in most industries in Middlesbro'. The shareholders would be very glad to get the principal back. What profits there are, are swallowed up by the officials." If there were no levelling up of salaries, said one, how much better should we be. Is one Town Clerk worth 10 policemen? or could he do 10 policemen's work? Evidently, here is a Sack list who would deny that the various institutions named by your correspond- ents are carried on, on Socialistic lines. Now to both Mr. Whitham and Mr. " Common Jus- tice "I will observe that I am not arguing land nation- alization ;I am arguing state manufacture, in the same way that I should argue if we had land natinealisation. That the profits of tilling the soil should below, to the tiller, and the man who was rewarded by good crops for extra skill and labour should net be compelled to share them with the man who laboured indifferently.</p>
        <p>The profits of both arm and brain belong to the indivia- ual. Because selfishness can bo pointed to in individual- i ism, that does not prove its absence in Soeialism,or that selfishness i not the motive of Socialism. I am as desirous as anyone to see the abuses of individualism removed, and have advocated in a Newcastle paper, methods for effecting this end, which metliQds I nodc. have since heon adopted by the hatters' ttaries union (I tient claim ills as a result ol such letters.but it may be).</p>
        <p>There are two firms in eliddlesbro' which are in the same i business ; one pays, the other does net. It is said that tee one is too big to control, hence its non success. Any woaks in open competition which gets beyond the con- trol of the inspiring brain of its dictator is bound to be unsuccesefel. What would be our position under national control ? The road to the greatest benefit of ths greatest number does not lie through Socialism. I think my two opponents not only get across with each other but get across with themselves. In one place H. W. tells us a man with ability can alwa.es make away, and, again, that the capitalist gets the profit of ability,and ability starves. He takes us back to Milton, James Watts, and Goldsmith. I say the position is better to-day ; that is part of my argument, and when we run through the names of Holden, Sir G. Elliott., Edison, Bell, Bessemer, Siemens, Gilchrist, and Thomas, Sir Titus Salt, and a host of others in manufacture, art, and literature, we are compelled to admit that these men in benefitting the nation have benefited thems:eves alai that is how it should be. Of course under a Social- istic state they would not have been able to do this. INay, your Socialistic post office tried to rob Bell of the profits of his genius. It is really ludicrous to observe the positions my Socialistic debaters pot themselves in. Mr. "Common Justice " tells us the railways are prac- tically socialistic, and in a subsequent letter points out Jay Gould as a sample of the evil of individualism, because,l suppose because he left upwards 0f£20.000,000 worth of socielistic stock. Perhaps it would be as well if I turned my attention exclusively to' Common Jus- tice" (conspicuous by invisibility). He does not see nay argument about the potato. I know that he who sows hopes to reap more than he sows or he would not . sow. The manufacturer who builds his works sows,and in the employment of labour, engages labour to gather the crop. " Common Justice" is one of those who screed simply give him his potato back. I have referred, his story of a farm of 100 acres paving £2OO a year reit £lBO profit to the farmer and 153. a week each to two labourers, to my farming friends and they say if he would publish a book as to how it could be done,it would meet with ready sale as they were sure there were plenty of farmers like themselves desirous of being up to date. These friends of mine had the im- pudence Mr. "Common Justice" to question the veracity of the story. The man of the middle ages again Mr. "Common Justice" claims he showed was better off. He showed nothing about hint only that his beef was cheaper. Certainly he said so-and-so said so-aneeso. I reply that the apse dixit of a man conversant with the condition of the people and who4e writing is probably read by friends and who has his credit at stake, should be of more value than the declaration of an unknown writer that so-and-co said it. "Common Justice" says the people were better housed, and I showed from what is not denied that their sanitary condition was worse aril it so how could they be better housed? There can he no greater proof of the improved condition of the people than the birth and death rate providing the conditions of life with regard to marriage, &amp;e., are practically the same. A perusal of the increase of the population gives the palm to the present time. I have only to consult those who can remember the early years of the present centery to know something of the past condition of the people ef this country. The dearness and scarcity of clothes, the insanitary condition of houses and drinking water and the prevalence of permanent skin diseases such as itch, &amp;c., and the periodic visitations of epidemics. which with our present intercommunication would have decimated the land. We are told a man of ability exercises his ability be- cause he likes to exercise it, and Edison is cited as an inventor who invented because he liked it. Edison in- vented to profit himself. If that were not so, he would not have patented. or reserved the profits of his inven- tions to himself. In benefitting; himself he has benefit- ted the world. "Common Justice" says genius can in- vent a machine without money but money cannot invent a machine without genius. The majority of inventors would have been lost without money and intich has been lost to the world because of the want of it. There would have been no Darwin as we know him if there had been no wealth in the family. Neither would there have been a Ruskin. These men like 99 per cent, of their kind are geniuses by reason of opportunity and that is why the inventions in machinery come from the working classes. According to" Common Justice" genius will come out irrespective of surroundings. Si Handel would still have been a great musician bad he been reared in the South Sea islands. Take this to heart "Common Jus- tice," "Man is the creature of circurastancee."—Yours, &amp;c.. T. Y. HOWCROFT. Middlesborough, April 9,1894.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>ENIMA.NITiL CHURCH CRIUSTIIN ENDEA.VOUR, SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>ENIMA.NITiL CHURCH CRIUSTIIN ENDEA.VOUR, SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>SECOND ANNIVERS.kRY.</p>
        <p>To celebrate the second anniversary of the Young People's Society of Christian Endeavour in connec- tion with the Einmanuel Reformed Church, Bold- street, a tea and consecration meeting was held in the schoolroom over Arpley Station on Thursday evening. The room had been prettily decorated for the occasion with banners, flowers and plants. A good number eat down to tea,and the meeting which followed was largely attended. The president of • the Society (Rev. H. 'l'. Reed) occupied the chair,and • there were also present the Rev. J. Kirk Macona- chic, congregationalist minister, (Manchester) lt-•v. G. J. Lacell (Rector of Christ Church, Hemel Hemp- stead), and Rev. D. W. Roberts (Warrington). After devotional exercises the</p>
        <p>11-17. E. T. RZED said he thought a great many of those present would recollect the bseinning of their Christian Endeavour Society. A little over two years ago they met together—chiefly Sunday school teachers and elder scholars—and decided to form a branch of the Young People's Society of Christian EndeaYour. They were aware how their meeting had been kept up with the ut- most regularity, and how many of their members hen heen blessed of (led and how their numbers had in- - t • e,r6A1.4, hda ram china v-f 2: K't 40M•le! were formed in consequenee of their stear::iug one there, but he did not wish them to think that that was alto- gether so. The news of the rmytalent wa? Tread far air: wide, and the other societieslii LEW te'ere =get have heard of it before,bUt they at Emmenuel Church had thehonour of beginning the first Curistian Endeavour Society in Warrington. (Applause.) Some tims ago they had a convention which was a (Treat suoce-ie and since that time representative.; of the societies had met and LiPt:i had drawn up the constitution for the formation (e` a union of the Warrington and District Young People's Societies of Christian Endeavour. That union wotald unite them all into one Christian Eadoevour baud, and from time to time they hoped to have concoctions simi- lar to the one in February, and which they trusted would stir up a great deal of interest in the neiehbeur- hood. (Applause.) This movement was growing by leaps and beundo ; societies were being started on every hand, and from all qnarters they head of the great blessing that had come to the young people. So great had the movement become that the leaders felt ja,tifiel in having the British Convention in London, and were holding it at the well-known Metropolitan Ta.bernactn which would hold five or six theusana peop:e. This great convention of all the societies would be heel at , Whitsuntide, and it was hoped that it would bring the movement more to tke front and create new interest. Ihe speaker then referred to the work the society was doing in connection with their Church, Sunday Schooi, and Band of Hope Society, -which he (tee speaker) thought was the best way of making their lauenes telt in the community. Miss LEES, secretary of the Emmanuel Endeavour Society, then read the annual report iron; which it , appeared that the members on the books were 42 active members, 11 associate members, and 3 honorary niem- bers. The lowest attendance at any meeting that had been held during the year was 36. A junior branch had also been formed which comprised 6 active members and 32 associate members. As the outcome of their society six other societies had been commenced in and about Warrington. The united convention on the 1111 Febru- ary exceeeed their most sanguine expectation. In conclusion she said it was most encouraging to note the marked progress of the society.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. Kum MACONACIIII3 said he was a warm friend of the Christian End..avour inow.ment. He con- sidered the society was doing a great deal more t draw the different denominations together than a great many other methods. The speaker then 'puke on the subject of individualty in the service of Lord .U'Sll3 Christ. The Rev.GEOROB J. LACELL also addressed the meeting. It was not, he said, his first appearance in Warrington. It was many years since he last preached here, and he missed many old faces. He was very pleased to be there, especially on an occasion like that, and he considered that their Cluistian Endeavour Society was a very pro- mising child for two years old. The rev. gentleman delivered a most impressive address, speaking more par- ticularly on the motto of the society " For Christ and the Church."</p>
        <p>During the evening several new Christian lindeaweir hymns were sung by the active members. A feature in the evening's proceedings was the reading of the roll call when about 20 of the active members gave their testi- monies on the following subject " What has the Lord Jesus Christ done for me, and what am I doing for Him? " Mr. Gandy presided at the harmonium. The proceedings closed with prayer by the Rev. D. W. Roberts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>HOME SECRETARY'S MARRIAGE.</head>
        <p>THE HOME SECRETARY'S MARRIAGE.</p>
        <p>The marriage of Mr. Asquith, M.P., the Home Se,cre- tary, and Miss Margaret Tennant, daughter of Sir Charles Tennant, Bart., Glen, Peeblesshire, is maw (IC- finitely arranged to take place at Sr. George's Church, Hanover Square, on Thursday, the 10th Mar, at half. past two o'clock. The Bishop of Rochester (Dr. Randal Davidsov) will officiate, assisted by the Rev. Canon Scott Holland. of St. Paul's Cathedral, and the Rev. Charles Anderson, Rector of St. George-'3. Vanity Fair" says Miss Tennant will have a consi- derable dowry. Sir Charlas 'Tennant is giving her a house and settling upon her an income which has been broadly put at 1;3,000 a year. She has already received a number of presents.</p>
      </div>
