      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDA'Y, APRIL 14, 1894.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDA'Y, APRIL 14, 1894.</p>
        <p>dowlim</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES A::;1)</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES A::;1)</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Warrington "came down a peg." on Saturday, which all admirers of their recent good form canno?.; help hut regret. The defeat which St. Helens inflicted upon them was nit a very great one and was brought about more by good luck than anything else. One of the isioA strik- ing features of the mat:ll—and it was striking in More ways than one—was the vigorous methods which were adopted towards the close. Casualities were far more , numerous than pleasant and it was a regrettaUe fact ' that the referee had to reward the extraordinary vigour of one of the St. Helens men, by ordering him off the field. Until recently, Warrington appeared to be under the ban of being a 'trough team," but I think I am not far wrong in assertine that, though they are men quite capable of looking after themselves, they have seldom revealed any lack of good temper except in eases like Saturday, where they have been driven ta it. Ia all protahility, St. Helens will hear again of the taisports- man-like conduct -indulged in. Justice has generally been slightly blinlfolded when Warrington has been in- volved in these matters in the way of a defendant ; let us hope she will take away the bandage now Warrington has become plaintiff.</p>
        <p>St. 11,1 ens were lifted into the happy position of vic- tors by a penalty kick from Cross, their captain, and the success of this kick was not the outcome of so much skill as chance. Apart from this, the NVarrin g,on team showed themselves to be in good play. vastly superior to their opponents. Heesom was in faultless form at hack, and showed up in good contrast with Foulkes. The two Bates, however, showed a slight falling ofT in their combination, and were fairly well watched Ws' Cross and Ashcroft. These tyre opposing forces, in fact seemed to have made quite a half-back fight of tue mateh,and were almost always at close quarters. Among the forwards, Dakin displayed more dash and energy than perhaps anyone els in the forward ranks.</p>
        <p>The "A "team Match was accompanied by a very unpleasant accident. The unfortunate person waa Broady, the now popular three-quarter back, who was engaged in assisting his old comrades in tho Stockton Heath team. The accident resulted in his sustaininr, a hr-ken shoulder. A great deal of sympathy was felt for this promising player, and I reiterate the hope that hti will soon recover.</p>
        <p>The junior cup competition is proceeding apace, and the thronged " gates " chich have assembled to witness the matches that have been played, prove how inaccu- rate are the suggestions made in certain quarters that to junior football all the interest lies with the association code. Whilst there can be no doubt that association is more popular than it was years as, anyone cogninnt of the true state of public sentiment on the matter, can- not but see that Rugby has, so to speak, the " ayes " in an overwhelming majority. The silver cu nis now on view in the shop window of Messrs. Dolan Bros.. florse- market-st., and it is surrounded with samples of the medals which are to be presented to the players taking Dart in the final.</p>
        <p>The draw for the semi,final will take place on Monday evening next at the Norton Arms. The final matehei will take place on the Warrington Football club's ground. Authorities on the game are unanimous in the opinion that better football has so far been shown in tht com- petition, than has been seen previously in the play of local junior clubs. The postponed Lancashire Cup Championship match, between Wigan and Swinton was played on Wednesday evening at Swinton, and the result was that Swinton have been relegated to the second place.</p>
        <p>The tabulated results of the competition up to date 2re as follows : _</p>
        <p>For. Ag3t.</p>
        <p>'PI/. Won. Last. Drn. Et T. (LT. Pta. 01.dham Swinton lB .11 1 6 13 22 .. 2 5...2 Warrington Wizan lB 9 4 5 12 11. 2 5.23 TyMesley 18 ... 7 ... 8 ... 3 ... 7 19...11 14 ...17 Broughton Hangers_ 18 ... 5 ... 8 ... ... 11 8 ... 815 -.15 1t,411,14.1e Hornets nrr E 13 ... 0 ...17 ... 1 ... 4 7 ...34 40 ... 1 iouThton</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>CYCLING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Cycling Club deserve a figurative slap of congratulation, on their hAcks. Tney intend to undertake the organization of a cycling club sports. which is to be held on June 16th. It ha alrfiviy been decided to spend £45 in prizNi, which will be dividA among three cycle races, and three flat race. I hope they will receive all the support they require, for the Warrington C.-cling Club is growing to a very strong condition, and promises to lxicomo a clvib of consider- alile importance among other similar institutions.</p>
        <p>In connection with the club, there' were two " runs " on Saturday. "Pa " took about 1?. members under his charge, and mad* a visit to St. Helens, where thry in- dulged in the luxury of seeing the Warrington Football Club beaten by the locals. Some sixteen other mem- bers of the club placed thmmwelves under the control or their sub-captain the" Genial EXclard," who took them to Bndworth through Arley. After riding romad Marbury Mere they rode into Budworth, and made slet- ful inroads into a feast of ham and eggs. The country was in a tvautif tit condition, and the trees were yordant with the blossoms of spring. Bud worth, which always looks enticing at this time of the year, gave one of the party quite a shock ; he had a severe attack of " sprisg poetics'," and before he could be brought round had babbled endless lengths of consternating rhythm. S,anf! " harm pop," and a turnip, df)Waef, gtiffii his equilibrium safi until 11 cho th scenary of Wlrrington, when the attack of rhapsody disap- pelted,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>" VILLAGE WEDDING."</head>
        <p>" VILLAGE WEDDING."</p>
        <p>isTomnrisu CE.IREMONY AT PADGiTI3</p>
        <p>it is a long time since the village se Padgate wore an air of such festivity and brighteesa as that which characterised it on Wednesday. On that day there occurred the wedding of Miss Ellen Bennett, I eldest daughter of Mr. William Bennett,Lyndburst, Bruche Avenue, to Mr. C. Dennison Stovin, of Bindbrook, Lincolnshire. Though the sky had an Unpropitious aspect in the early part of the morn- ing, te elements were bright and cheerful at'the hour of the ceremony,and if it be true" that happy is the bride that the sun shines on," then indeed Mies Bennett should have a pleasant prospect be- fore her. The whole village participated in the happy event, and from the windows of most of the houses in the village, there were unfurled to the breeze flags of many colours with bright hued bunt- ing in profusion. With the hour of the ceremony, there came throngs of expectant onlookers who waited about Brache Avenue and the wesieyan einepol; and long before the time annonneed for the ceanmencement of the service, the chapel was thronged to the utmost of its holding ca- pacity 'with eager spectators. The building had been appropriately decorated. At any time it is a place pretty to look upon, but on this occasion the tinted rays of sunlight streamed through the stained windows and shone upon a wealth of floral adornments, and upon the bright dresses of the ladiee, making the chapel seem more prepossessing than ever. As the wedding party approached the church they were greeted with lively selections of music played by the I ndia,trial Schools band,and for some distance from the chapel they had to proceed hetweea t 00 deep lines of spectators. Upon the party entering the church, appropriate selections of organ music were played by Al r.W.S.Woode. The bride,who was led down the aisle by her father, presented a charming picture in her white silk dress of moir antique,and tulle veil,surmounted with a coronet of orange bloeoms. Her bridesmaide were Miss Edith Beneett, Miss Iletty Bannettand Miss Lily Richman. e,on,who wire attired in blue and fawn dressee,with trimmings of pink, blue, and gold and undone silk and they each carried a bouquet of beautiful flowers. The service, which was of a very impres- sive order, was opened with the singing of a well- known nuptial hymn" The voice that breathed e'er E'en." The officiating ministers were the Rev. J. Kirtlan, Superintendent of the Warrington Wee- leyan Circuit, and the Rev. J. Gunnell, of Penketh. The bride was given away by her father, and the best man was the bridegroom's brother, Mr. At the conclusion of the service, as the party com- menced to leave the church, the building was tilled with the music of Mendelesolas " Wedding March," played on the organ in excellent style by Mr. Woods, On emerging from the vestibule of the church, the. happy pair were greeted with showers of congratu- lations—and rice. And then ono of the prettiest in- cidents of the ceremony took place ; the members of the bride's Sunday Sehool class preceded her and strewed the path with white flowers. A short time after the ceremony, the wedding breakfast was helloind was attended by more than 50 guests. Groups of the wedding party were taken at Lynd- hurst by Mr. Birtles, photographer, Sankey-street. During the day, the newly united couple left for Cheltenham, en route for Devonshire and Cornwall, where they will send the honeymoon. Oa the followiog evening Mr. arid Mrs. Bennett enter- tained a party of the teachers, choir, and Miss Ben- nett'.' Sunday School ease, as well as a large, num- ber of other friend. The party was held in the schoolroom, and lacked nothing to make it a ; pleasant event. A large number of appropriate and valuable presents were received by the bride and bridegroom, and among them were a beautiful bible and album from the Wesleyan Churca Con- wegation, and a handsome pair of nut crackers irom the members of the bride's class.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>MUST.' FOR TIIE PEOPLE.</head>
        <p>MUST.' FOR TIIE PEOPLE.</p>
        <p>THE FIRST WARRINGTON VOLUNTEER BAND.</p>
        <p>BY I..UkE BLUNT.</p>
        <p>It. was about the 2f_Yli time we had described a ' circle r0,...nd the band stand. All hop, of obtaining a seat had long since ranked among the vain thim,v which are not to be enjoyed. One's lungs were full of dust ; and this, together with the broiling heat, made one literally drip with perspiratioa— and ebullient profanity oE a mild character. With the aid of persistent endeavour—and alarums-1 had succe;.-tded in fulfilling a vow, vowed in a rash moment, to rise in time to see the first band per- formance of the season in the rark. And now I found that even the subiimest passages in the music failed to stir any slumbering ambition in me,higher than a deep and sincere yearning for a seat ! But this was ao attribute I did not know was concealed about me—this yearning—lor I never yearned I before ; not even my bread. At length, however, ingenuity came to my aid. I went and stood be- hind a young man who sat at, the end of a seat. And presently a dainty little maiden sauntered to the front ; she had tobacco coloured tresses of hair —and a solicitude for the company of a swain. 1 knew this, because ever and anon she turned her head and rmle,d sweetly and winningly. The young man stopped chewing shavings off the knob of his stick, and looked interested. 1. threw some sheeps-eyes over his head at the maiden and she giggled. This young man's vanity prompted him to believe he had made " an impression.' and when the siren nodded amiably in response to a salute from the Subscribt:r, Mr. Adonis blushed, rose, and with a show of the ,:xquisite blandishments, which are the stock-in-trade of the modern masher,ambled forth to pay his court. And so I sat down and ceased to yearn.</p>
        <p>I took a turn at thinking, for a change. The band playing was that attached to the local corps of Volunteers, and the fact reminded me of the con- versation I had, a few weeks before, with one of the organizers of the first Volunteer band. SOrae 30 years woo it number of local gentlemen formed the first Volunteer company, and finding it ,some- what irksome to be marching around without mar- tial music, Lit,upon the idea of having a band. It was liggested to a number of young working- men of Latchford, who, after holding a meeting, sel out and collected enough money to purchase 14 instruments. The idea of wearing gorgeous- uni- forms and playing spirited music entered into the souls of the young men and raised them into a whirl of enthusiasm—for a week or two. Then the at- tendance at the practices began to fall off, and the reason of this diminished gladness became appar- ent, when one of the band—then the landlord of the Dog and Dart—wanted to know, in an injured tone : " When are us gooing to have the cloothcs." But the answer, while intended to be redolent of encouragement, NVloil VagUe and had a far away sort of ambiguity about it. Some of the members hat encouraged blissful dreams. of being able to delight their households by the strains of " Tommy make room for your uncle,' Sze., after a lesson or two; and row that they never seemed to get beyond the notes—and had not even the uniforms--they felt hurt, and spoke to the. bandmaster in tones that implied a suspicion of seeming to feel that they had been defrauded. And at length, like the in- vited kfuests to a certain feast in a parable, they could not attend—some because of one thing, some because of another; and only two members put in an appearance. These two—Mr. Ellison and Mr. J. Norbury—carried on the practice, and were alone in thcir glory anti uniforms—amid the dumb trom- bones and siftqlt cornets. But as one of them con- acientiously boasted afterwards : Nothing was lost, because tnev played the whole 14 instruments between them. Toey gave each a turn.</p>
        <p>At the end of 12 months however, the band could scrape through a few tunes tolerably well The Volunteers took them into their service, and they attended the first review at Chester. The then Colonel of the corps granted the band, at the out- set, the privilege of attending private engagements in the uniform. Now about five years afterwards, the band was augmented to 36 and the extra in- struments were purchased by the officers. With this augmentation came a revocation of the privi- lege granted by the Colonel. The Colonel, said the band had broken his contract; and with all tho untamed daring of ;7uitic recruits, defied his mar- dal authority. That very week they attended an lengagement with their uniforms on; the. Calonel vowel to unbend their defiant obstinacy, and in- structed Band sergeant Ellison to collect a tine of 2s. 61. from each defaulter. The musicians .met this with majestic disdain, and with a jaunty levity answered the explanation of the collector with a " Go lengi---schich signified that argument wasn't necessary. The next step 17f9.5 t') break the hearts of the efficer3, and this was to be done with a, humility of manner duly conforming with their ,rnt as 6.0 to the next parade they all caul?, armed with 3(1 . written resignations, the drawing up of which had been to many the great- est and most memorable literary endeavour of their life. At length the time for dismissal came, and then, at a given signal, the band marched in single file towards the Colonel, and as each passed he saluted his commander and handed to him his resignation. The Colonel did not give any outward sign of grief: the memories of the surviving mem- bers only retain the recollection of him using a eolitary but emphatic D, of significant force. The band became the Latchford Brass Band and for some time the Volunteers were divorced from that sweetest of military joys, the accompaniment of music. However unkind this peremptory move of the first Volanteer band might have been, it seems to have had a salutary effect upon those officers who would withdraw a given privilege. It can be said with coulience that no privilege has since been revoked—but, maybe, this is because none have since been granted.</p>
        <p>Daring the fire years that elapsed before the divorce, there had occurred many incidents and occasions, which in after years stood forth in the memories of the members, and were shaped into delightful reminiscences by the chastening hand of Time. ()ace they nearly won the first prize at a band contest at linuteford—and on another occa,siee they almost won the gratitude of the undertakers' , by getting nearly poisoned with veal pies. The first, incident was in connection with a contest at a Manchester and Liverpeol Agricultural Show, and there was a large number of contestants in the com- petition. The Warrington band were under the leadership of Mr.Oakden.who was also well-known as the leader of the regular band at the barracks. The end of the contest was a " tie " between War- rington and Snlybridge, and Warrington made a vigorous claim for the first, prize on the grounds that. StaiN bridge bandincluded professionals. But the claim did not hold g-od, and eventually the judges —arneere whom was. Dam Godfrey—gave over the first prize to Stalybridge. The incident of the pie-. though in every detail a success, was no regarded with favour by these dissatisfied beadsmen. They wanted taeir friends to see, when they were Out at a sham fight at Newton, that although they had no actual enemies to face, the inexorable privations of war were suff n.ed by them. And yet most of them used words that ought only to be whispered in the ear, when they were rendered able to carry out this wish, by being reduced to a stai.e of dilapi- daticm gloriously martyr-like in effect. It occurreJ this way. They were returning home from the fray and being tired and hungry. called for pies and ; beer at a certain inn. They did not call in vain. Every man had his veal pie—and every man's veal pie had hire ; for during the vigil watches of- that night there was not a member who was not calling ' lustily for a doctor—and a; the same time being - • ; reduced to the spare condition of an old cam- ' paigner, by the shrewd pangs of acute diarrhoea. And yet they grumbled! On the same day, too, it is said that the band added to the lustre of its name. The story goes that while the fight was going on the greater part of the band, seeking rest in eecluaed spot, came across two single combatants. The two had met on the toed, and. had pranced up to each other ;seising to itnnginery loginnds in the space behind, and shouting "On lads, on." After a few convulsive clutches they rolled into a ditch. The band ant excited in the fray, and played frantic music. But neither of the combatants would call " 'nough,"and claimed each other as prisoners. How long they would have continued the struggle it is hard to say. But the Colonel and some of his men attracted by the erratic music swooped down upon the group. I arrested both combatants—and made the band feel like to many hens on hot girdles. The most, not- able record, perhaps, in the band's five years of ex- istence was that of being present at the first camp- ing out of the Volunteers. The campin a out ground was a large field off Lovely-lane. The corps went to camp on the Saturday, remained there un- til Monday morning. At four o'clock they had to turn out for drill at half past five they went to</p>
        <p>town and worked until six in the evening, and then they returned to camp and underwent, drill until late at night. This had to be kept up all week,and the hard work thus necessarily entailed should be a sufficient te.;;timony of the enthusiasm which the volunteers of that day took in their institu- tion.</p>
        <p>When I recalled these little facts, and remem- bered that the band was really the only one in town at the time, it occurred to me that during 36 years the spread of musical accomplishments in Warring- ton was almost beyond computation. Here we had the Bands Associatiou,with which the performance then being given was ccannected. In that associa- tion there are alone 209 able instrumentalists, dis- tributed in the following manner : Volunteer Band, 20; League of the Cross, 30; Borough Band, 27 ; St. Pads, 21; Fletehers, 21; Cockhedge Miission, 24; Brick-..4reet, 24 ; Brick-street Mission, 24 ; Latch- ford. 24; Buckley-street. 24; 31i. Down's Military Band, 50. In addition t..) tee members of thes.3 band,,, it is estimated that there are also 1,200 other instrumentalists in. Warrington. Soon, there is no . doubt, the Bands .A.soeiation will bring this scattered m ultitude of musicians into closer sympathy with each other. They have already, . during the pist twelve months, done much in this direction. They are striving, with commendable zeal, to orgaaizt for the coming season, a pro- . gramme of unprecedentei excellence, of perform- aces in the park. But I should not like to spoil the dignified solidity of the present hi.stoncal article, with light, airy facts of this character. So I prepose to devote • an interview—with Mr. .J. Taylor. the secretary—to the matter, next week. Historical facts, and the other sort, don't blend very well ; besides, the Editor, unlike the twe cons- batants above mentionod, has called '"nough."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>QUEEN AND THE SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>THE QUEEN AND THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>THE VISIT TO WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>It is now announced that the Queen will formally open the Ship Canal on the 21st—it may possibly be th 2nd—of May. In the course of the last few days, we understand, the London and North-Wes- tern Railway Company have been informed that tier Majesty will break her journey to Balmoral at Warrington. instructions have been given for the , conveyance of the Royal train to Latchford station, and thence by a line to be specially made, to the , Latchford lacks. Here the Qaeen is to board the Royal S,acht Aiberta (which will be accompanied by its tender the Enchantress) and sail towards Manchester. The infirmities of age might prevent her Majsety from making the full journey to the Salford docks. It is the desire of her advisers that F &amp;he shornld avoid the fatigue of a progress through the streets of the city and of Salford. According to the present arrangements the Alberta will stay her course at .Barton. Returning to Latchford, the Queen will resume her journey to the North.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER VERSION.</p>
        <p>Our London correspondent says the " Manchester Guardian," in the course of an interview with the Lord Mayor of Manchester (mho is at present iu London) on Thursday aftetnoon, learnt that, according to the last arrangements of the Ship Canal officials, Her Majesty would on the opening day embark at Irlam lock and proceed up the canal to Msnchester, returning thence to Irlam to continue her train journey to BalmAtal from that point. The Corporation have as yet been able to make no settled plans. Nothing can be done until Sir Henry Ponson by's return to London, which will not take place till Monday next. The Lord Mayor hopes that the Queen may proceed to the Town Hall and depart for the North from the Exchange Station. This, at all events wili be suggested.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>THE CANAL BILL.</p>
        <p>Petitions praying to be heard against the Manchester Ship Canal Bill in the House of Lords have heßo. deposited by the Mereey Docks and Harbour Board, the Manches- ter. Sheffield and Lincolnshire Railway, the Hull an. Liverpool Red Oxide Compasiy, the North Staffordshire Railway Company, the Corporation of Manchester, the Sling)sliire Union Railways and Canal Corulany, the Duke of Bridgewater's Trustees, Brunn,t, Mond aol Company, Limited, Executors of Um N.iii of T. A. Walker, deceased, North Staffordsliira .Chamber of C&amp;lki- mercy, Corporation of Warrington, South Yorkshire Coal Owners' Association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>RUMOURED FINANCIAL DIFFICULTIES.</head>
        <p>RUMOURED FINANCIAL DIFFICULTIES.</p>
        <p>,1A Manchester oorrespondent says :—" The air is full uf exciting and dkagreeable rumours about the Ship Canal, its management, and its financial prospects. Though the officials decline to give information on the subject, it is confidently stated that serious differances ef opinien have sprung up among the directors. Though a story of the resignation of Sir John Harwood lately published vi se contradicted, it is stated that for several , weeks that the reiignatin ha! actually been in the hands of the Lord Mayor of Manchester, and rumenr is busy with a story of the res4nation of a high officia!,partially- convevent on a stormy directors' meeting last week. The disagreements which have arisen,and which threaten serious consequencls, have reference te the future financial policy .of the !management, which now, seems certain to involve demands for more money." ruE QUE3TION OF WHARFAGE ACCOMMODA-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>TION AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>TION AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>We understand that a deputation from the Warring- ton Chamber of Commerce had an interview, in com- pany with a deputation from the Town Council, with the directors of the Ship Canal, on Thursday. The depu- tation drew the attention of the directors to the import- ance of some accommodation for the loading and unload- ing of vessels, being provided at Warrington. The great inconvenience caused by the existing arrangemsnto was also pointed out and Ow Direet9ri finally agreed to give the matter their careful attention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>GHASTLY DISCOVERY AT SALTPORr.</head>
        <p>GHASTLY DISCOVERY AT SALTPORr.</p>
        <p>Our Saltport correspondent gives particulars of a sen- sational discovery made at Saltport on Wednesday. About six o'clock the body of a boy, unknown, was found floating in the ship Canal at Saltport by Mr. Wm Hough, the harbour master. The body now lies at tie Weaver Hotel, Weston Point. P.C. Harrimersley exam- ined the body, which is a mere skeleton, and must have been in the water throe months at least. Nothing is left of the head except the skull, and the hands and legs are off, but have been recovered. It is supposed that the boy was between eight and twelve years of age. The following is a description, as near as can be given : —Jacket, vest, and knickerbocker trousers of light brown tweed, knitted grey stockings, lace-up boots never mended, merino jersey fastened at the neck with a brown horns stud, no shirt. In the button-hole of the jacket just below the chin was a piece of twisted tar- rope wrapped round with flue copper wire. This is twenty-two inches long, and the police believe it was used as a whip for horses. The body was found close to the sluices, and may have been washed into the canal from the Mersey. The boy was, to julge from his dress the son of respectable parents.</p>
        <p>Yesterday (Friday) morning an inquiry was con- ducted by Mr. J. E. Worsley, coroner, at the Weaver . Hotel. Weston Point, relative to the above ease.—P.C. Flammeriley and Mr. William Hough, harbour master, ! ,move evidence similar to the information already given. The first named witness added that he could form no idea as to whether the boy hall been drowned or thrown ! into the water when dead. There was nothing to guide him iu forming such an idea. None of the hones were ! broken about the body, so that the probibility was that he had been drowned.—The Coroner, in sum. mine, up tke evidence said from all appearances the body had been held together by the clothing. It was impossible to say whether there were any wounds on the body but the constable reported that no bones were broken. There were no signs upon the skull which would lead to any suspicion that injury had been caused to the head. With regard to the cord found tied near the throat of the body it was impossible to account for. It might probably have been used to tie the boy to some timber to help him to float supposing that he had been on a vessel that had been wrecked. On the other hand it might have been used to ensure his sinking with something else on the end of it. The Constable how- ever lad pointed out that no such weight would have , been required to make so small a boy sink. The body ! had e7idently been in the water two or three months. ! Every effort had been made to identify the body but , without suecess. There were two verdicts which the jury could find and they were "Found Drowned "and 1" Found Dead." ! The latter perhaps was the safest.— The jury chose the verdict of' Found Drowned" with which the Coroner expressed his concurrence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>CONSERVATIVE MEETING AT PADGATE.</head>
        <p>CONSERVATIVE MEETING AT PADGATE.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting for the Warrington Polling District of the Newton Division was held on Mon- day evening in the Padgate Church schoolroom.— Alderman Francomb presided, amongst those pre- sent were—Alderman Sutton, Councillor Bennett, Messrs. W. Houghton, James Houghton, Joseph Houghton, G. Mather, London, T. Smith, Jos.Potter, John Fairclough. A. Carter, Gorge Hatton, 11. Antrobus,T. Houghton, James Daintith, James Ratcliffe, John Timis, W. Holland Owen (hon solici- tor to the Association). W. A. Mawdsley (general •icretary). On the motion of Alderman Sutton, 13tonded by Mr. W. Houghton, ,Mr. Joseph Davies was re-elected chairman of the district. Alderman Francomb was re-elected vice chairman, and Alder- man Sutton bon. .secretary and treasurer. Messrs. W. Houghton, A. Bennett. V. Royston, and F. Appleton wereelected delegates to the Council for the ensuing year. The old committee were again elected</p>
        <p>NEW WESLEYAN CHAPEL AT LATCH-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>FORD. LAYING OF FOUNDATION STONES.</head>
        <p>FORD. LAYING OF FOUNDATION STONES.</p>
        <p>The Wesleyans of Latchford have for a number of years, been desirous of having a more commo- dious place of worship in their district, and for a considerable time past have been working to attain this object. Oti Monday afternoon, the foundation stones of the new chapel were laid.which testified to the progress made with the project. The cause of Wesleyan Methodism was established at Latchford about forty years ago, hut the congregation has scarcely ever had a place to call their own to worship in. The !hist place they met was at the end of Wash-lanesand subsequently they took an o!d barn from which they removed to the Temperance Ha!! where they at present remain. They, however, cannot call their present abode their own, for they only rent the place. When the new chapel is built, they will, however, meet in a place which is thairs ex- elusively, and with the erection of this handsome struc- ture, there will no doubt be a large increase in their membership. The site is an excellent one,heing CCHWerli- ently situated at the junction of the old The! wall-lane and Knutsford-road. It was formerly occupied by the old barn which did service for many years as the old Temperance Hall.</p>
        <p>There was a large number present at the ceremeny of laying the foundation stories, despite the threatening aspect of the sky at the tine. Among these present were Revs. C. Garrett, J. Kirtlan, G. D. Mason. J. S. Hughes, and G. E. Cutting, Messrs. P. Silcock (archi- tect), L. Ellison, G. Weaver, W. trough, E. J. Guest, J P., R. G. Henshall, W. Winstanley, E. Brown, S. B. Stringer, S. Moss, J. W. Hayward, J.P., F. Wright, W. Bennett. Lawson, J. Hartley, Harrison, J. Horton, H. E. Roscoller, W. Glea.ve, Miss Bessie Dutton, Mrs. Cooke Mrs. Bennett, Miss Tat, Mrs, and Miss Hartley.—The proceedings opened by devotional exercise, after which The Bev. J. KIRTLAN in making the statement et the eoet of the structure, said the present site was secured some two or three years ago in the time of hie predecessor, the Rey. John Gunnell, who wss happily present that day. The cost of the site was £512 which was a good sum, but not more than it was worth, be- cause it was in such a splendid position. The total cost of the scheme was £1,609. They would receive hack £212 for the rental of two cottages which had been bought along with the land, so that they had to dcel really with aleaut £1,400. According to the regulations of the Wesleyan body, they had to promise that thte-e quarters of the outlay at least shonld be raised within twelve months from the opening of the chapel. He was glad to say that the trustees had signed the schedule agreeing to raise that sum within that time. They had not yet le-tabled that sum by a long way. They had re- ceived £5O from the Methodist Extension fund, and £5O from the general chapel fund. They had also received anther £5O free of interest to be paid back in ten years. Including all s,muces of incotne, they had at the peesent time £3455. Si. As they weuld see, they would require £l,lOO to meet the requirements of the chapel committee, sa that they had edit to raise £5OO. They hoped that dav to gct £2OO, and if they could obtain £2OO by that day's services, £lOO at the opening services, arid should get. an additional £l5O in the subscription list,they would just about carry their scheme without breaking anybody's heart or any- body's head. (Laughter.) He hoped that there would bea very liberal response on that day's services, and he also lisped that. the subscription list would he largely increased. Mr. Kirtlau then read the subscription list up to date which made a total of £598 ss. SJ. He said that he had received a letter from Dr. Gornall who wrote that he would be unable to be present at Latch- ford at the foundation stone laying, as he had to he at Panketh in order to attend professional duties. He was very sorry it had happened so. He would like to have end Mr. Garrett once mere, whose fervour as a friend of the people was as valuable as it was 30 years ago when he (Dr. Gornall) first heard him preach in Preston. The first stone was laid by Mrs. John Hartley, wife of the treasurer of the buildinr fund. The Rev. J. Gunnell in prcentine the trowel said that the inscription on the trowel was " Presented to Airs. John Hartley, Latchford, April 12, 1894." It was with great pleasure that he presented it to Mrs. Hartley, and he hoped that she , would long be spared to look back to this day, and he hived that when the building was erected, site would he aide to co-operate in the work at the chapel.—aira. Hartley then performed the ceremony of laying the stone.—Mrs. R. G. Henshall laid the next stone, the trowel being presented by Mr. E. J. Guest, J.P.—The third sterie was laid by Miss Hartley, Mr. George Weaver presenting the trowel.—Miss Toft, daughter of Mr. John Toft, Warrington, laid the fourth stone, Mr. Luke Ellison presenting the trowel.—Mr. Edwin Brown, of Lynam, laid a fifth stone, and Mr. S. Moss, in present- ing the rrovael expressed his pleasure at presenting it to a brother local preacher.—The sixth stone was in the name of the scholars frenn all parts of the Warrington circuit. The stone was laid by Miss Bessie Dutton, and Mr. R. G. Henshall presented her a bible as a remetn- brance of the occasion.—Miss Dutton suitably replied, after which the scholars from the various chapels af the circuit filed past and laid purses containing the money they had coliected for the.occasion upon the stone. This was a new departure, end the result has justified the veeture, for no lees then 43 purses were brought and laid upon the stone. The trowels which aie-re made of •,,iiver were ;:upplied by Mr. G. Blaekhurat, jeweller, SI/Ail-2; net. The Rev. CA.RL2S GARUIVt r, chairman of the district afterwards addressed the spectators. fie said that he congratalated the Latchford Methodists heartily upon that day's event. He was glad that they had had such a beautiful afternoon for the cer.mony, and that they could meet together in comfert. He also congratulated them upon their Aclection of a site. Nothing cculd have been more suitable for the purpose. The place would be an ornament to Latchford. He was not in any way bigotted, mod he always rejoiced in the success of other denominations, but he had a peculiar joy in the progress of the Wesleyan Methodists. The doctrine' preached in the Wesleyan places of worship had no uncertain sound, and he hoped that they in Latchford would go on and prosper. He also hoped that they would be workers. They heard a great deal about un- employed, but he thought there wore far more unemployed in the church than elsewhere. He de- plored this, but he hoped that they in Latchford would have plenty of work to do, and that they would he a power in the village. The afternoon'.; proceedings were brought to a close, by the singing of the Doxology, and the pronouncing of the Benediction.</p>
        <p>A good number sat down • to tea at Bold-street Wes., leyau schoolroom, after which a public meeting was held. The 'chair was occupied by Mr. Harrison (late -stationmaster at Latchford Station), and he was sup- ported upon the platform by Revs. J. Kirtlau, G. D. Mason, G. E. Cutting, and E. Smith, and Messrs. J. Hartley L. Ellison, and P. Harrison (organist). _ .</p>
        <p>'Mk" Rev. J. KIRTLAN said that there was now £77 3s. 7il. taken in aid of the scheme so. that they still re- quired £4OO. %The CRAIRMAN said that he well remembered coming to Latchford sixteen years ago. He looked for a Wes- leyan chapel and to his surprise, he found an old barn used as a Wesleyan mission. He at once threw in his lot with the little society, and he had watched its growth for these hi x tee n years. He was delighted that day at toeing, the memorial stones laid of a real Wesleyan chapel. It was his heart's desire that that should have come (luring the time when he resided in Latchfcrd,Out better late than never, lie only wished that he coold have been with them to help te raise some of the money and do some of the work for it. He trusted that the place would be the bird' place of many a soul, and ha hoped that Latchford which had been neglected in the past would now be able to do great work for God and Metho- dism.</p>
        <p>Mr. BAiITLEY then gave financial result for the after- noon. He saiit that the amount laid upon the memorial tone by ttechldren was £l5 15s. (Applause.) They had collected £lO 18s. 3,1. during the afternoon, and £6O had tir•en given at the other foundation eton. He wishel to express his appreciation of the deep symp,kthy tiiey h.kd received from members:of other denominations</p>
        <p>The G. D. MASON then read the named of the various chitdren who had brought purses during the llerno,7.n. He hoped that when the chapel was opened it would be a source of great blessing to the village, and fa is only fear was that it would very soon prove too small for the purpose. The Rev. G. M. CUTTING as an old minister of the cir- cuit [-aid that ho waa happy to look upon old faces, as there was something inspiring when one saw old friends. He looked back upon his work in the Warrington circuit with pleasant memories, and ho wished t.) congratulate hts Latchford friends upon the step they had taken that day. They ought to have taken it years ago. De hoped t hat they would have faith to overcome all obstacles, and that the church would be powerful against all forms of</p>
        <p>The Rev. Edward Smith said that he was pleased to see such aggressive work going on in Warrington. Because the Latchford chapel was a small chapel he felt deeply in sympathy with it, He always liked small chapels as a preacher could have more of his own way in them than in larger chapels. Ile was indebted to a small chapel for his commencing the life of a minister. He would not have had the courage to have preached in a larger chapel at first. (Laughter.) Ma rev, gentleman who is a native of Widnes, went on to give interesting reminiscences of Warriegton, and his eloquent aAlress was frequently applauded. the Rev. J. GutiNELL sulssequently addressed the meeting, after which the proceedings terminated with the pronouncing of the benediction.</p>
        <p>BANE QUAY NINSION P.S.A. A P.S.A. in connection with the above school was held on tsunday, presided over by Mr. flunfer, of Leigh, who gave a very instructive addr?se. Solos were rendered by sir. leieldhouse and Miss gent, and recitations by Mr. F. Norman, These trieetius,;A which are more especially intended to bring the parents of the scholars in toucfi with the work of the teachers, have so far been very successful, and enc-ucage the superintendents in their new yen- ture.rds was the third meeting of the P.S.A., and through the k:ne'ness of the singers and reciter, a most enjoyable hour was spent.</p>
